<article class="jr-j" id="alexander" data-kids="0">
<h2>Alexander the Great's Campaigns</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">336–323 BCE &#183; <a href="/life/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> &#183; 21 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Alexander of Macedon became king after the assassination of his father, Philip II, in 336 BCE. He first secured Greece and the north, then led his army east against the Achaemenid Empire of Darius III. The campaigns took him through Anatolia, Syria, Mesopotamia and Persia to Bactria and the Indus Valley.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Greece and the Balkans</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="39.800,22.700" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Mount Ossa</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 336 BCE</span><p>Facing a revolt after Philip's death, Alexander rode south with his Macedonian cavalry. Finding the Thessalians blocking the pass, he had his men ride over Mount Ossa and came out behind them, and they surrendered.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.906,22.879" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Corinth</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 336 BCE</span><p>After stopping at Thermopylae, Alexander went on to Corinth. There he was given the title Hegemon of the Greek forces against the Persians, and there he heard of a Thracian uprising in the north.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.784,24.976" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Mount Haemus</span> <span class="jr-date">spring 335 BCE</span><p>To secure his northern borders before crossing to Asia, Alexander advanced into Thrace. At Mount Haemus his army defeated a Thracian garrison holding the heights, then crushed the Triballi who attacked it from behind.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.321,23.318" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a></span> <span class="jr-date">335 BCE</span><p>While Alexander was campaigning in the north, the Thebans and Athenians rebelled again. Thebes chose to resist, and the city was razed to the ground with great bloodshed. Its fall brought Athens to submit.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Asia Minor, Syria and Palestine</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="40.200,26.400" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Hellespont</span> <span class="jr-date">334 BCE</span><p>Alexander crossed the Hellespont into Asia. It took over one hundred triremes to carry the whole Macedonian army, and the Persians decided to ignore the movement.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.228,27.242" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Granicus River</span> <span class="jr-date">May 334 BCE</span><p>The Persian satraps of Anatolia drew up on the east bank of the Granicus River. Alexander attacked at once, broke the Persian line and won the battle, fought in north-western Asia Minor near the site of Troy.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.038,27.424" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Halicarnassus</span> <span class="jr-date">334 BCE</span><p>Orontobates and Memnon of Rhodes entrenched themselves in Halicarnassus. Alexander's army broke through the walls, Memnon set fire to the city and withdrew, and Alexander handed the government of Caria to Ada.</p></li>
<li data-ll="36.838,36.164" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Issus</span> <span class="jr-date">November 333 BCE</span><p>Darius took personal command of his army and cut Alexander's line of supply, so that Alexander had to turn back. The battle was fought near the mouth of the Pinarus River, south of Issus, and Darius fled, leaving his wife and much treasure.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.267,35.200" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tyre</span> <span class="jr-date">332 BCE</span><p>The Tyrians refused Alexander and killed his envoys, and he besieged the island city with a causeway and, in the end, a fleet. Those who sheltered in the temple of Heracles were pardoned. About 30,000 people were sold into slavery.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.508,34.460" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Gaza</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 332 BCE</span><p>Batis, commander of the fortress of Gaza, refused to surrender. The stronghold fell after three failed assaults, and Alexander was seriously wounded in the shoulder. The men were put to the sword and the women and children sold into slavery.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Mesopotamia and Persia</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="11">
<li data-ll="36.360,43.250" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Gaugamela</span> <span class="jr-date">331 BCE</span><p>Alexander crossed the Euphrates and the Tigris without opposition and met Darius's much larger army at Gaugamela. The result was a decisive Macedonian victory. Darius fled with a small part of his force.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.523,44.424" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 331 BCE</span><p>After Gaugamela, Alexander advanced to Babylon, one of the Persian capitals, and from there went on to Susa.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.189,48.256" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 331 BCE</span><p>At Susa Alexander found 50,000 talents. He sacrificed at the altars and held a torch race, and sent 3,000 silver talents to Antipater to fund the fight against the Spartans.</p></li>
<li data-ll="30.708,51.599" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Persian Gates</span> <span class="jr-date">winter 330 BCE</span><p>Alexander took the route towards Persis and walked into an ambush in the narrow Persian Gates, where the satrap Ariobarzanes made a last stand. The Persians held the pass for a month before Alexander encircled them and broke through.</p></li>
<li data-ll="29.935,52.890" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 330 BCE</span><p>Four months after reaching Persepolis, Alexander allowed his troops to loot the city. A fire broke out and spread through it. It is not clear whether this was a drunken accident or a deliberate act of revenge.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Central Asia and India</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="16">
<li data-ll="40.400,69.400" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Sogdian Rock</span> <span class="jr-date">327 BCE</span><p>Oxyartes of Bactria had sent his wife and daughters, one of them Roxana, to the fortress for safety. Some 300 men climbed the cliff at night and the defenders surrendered. Alexander later married Roxana.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.746,72.787" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 326 BCE</span><p>Alexander gained control of the former Achaemenid satrapy of Gandhara, including the city of Taxila, whose ruler Omphis had complied with his summons. He then advanced into the Punjab.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.828,73.639" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Hydaspes River</span> <span class="jr-date">326 BCE</span><p>In the Punjab Alexander fought the king Porus and defeated him. It was the most costly battle the Macedonians fought. Impressed by Porus, Alexander let him keep governing his kingdom as a satrap.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.678,74.912" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Hyphasis River</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 326 BCE</span><p>Exhausted by years of campaigning and afraid of the Nanda army, the Macedonians mutinied at the Hyphasis River and refused to march further east. This river marked the easternmost extent of Alexander's conquests.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The return from India</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="20">
<li data-ll="30.198,71.471" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a></span> <span class="jr-date">after 326 BCE</span><p>On the way back, Alexander's army conquered the Malli clans, in the area of present-day Multan.</p></li>
<li data-ll="25.509,64.144" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Gedrosian Desert</span> <span class="jr-date">after 326 BCE</span><p>Alexander sent much of his army to Carmania with Craterus and a fleet along the Persian Gulf shore under Nearchus. He led the rest back through the Gedrosian Desert. According to Plutarch, he lost three-quarters of his army on the 60-day march.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">His army mutinied at the Hyphasis River and refused to go further east, so Alexander turned back, sending part of his forces by land and a fleet along the Persian Gulf shore, and leading the rest home through the Gedrosian Desert. After his death his generals, the Diadochi, fought each other for about 40 years over the territories he had conquered.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Wars of Alexander the Great&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="hannibal-alps" data-kids="0">
<h2>Hannibal's March over the Alps</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">218 BCE &#183; <a href="/life/hannibal">Hannibal</a> &#183; 9 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Hannibal led a Carthaginian army from Iberia over the Pyrenees, across the Rhône and over the Alps into Italy, to take the war directly to the Roman Republic. The two main sources, Polybius and Livy, were born about 20 and about 160 years after the event.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Saguntum to the Rhône</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="39.680,-0.278" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a></span> <span class="jr-date">218 BCE</span><p>Hannibal besieged Saguntum, a city under Roman protection, for eight months. Afterwards he sold all its inhabitants as slaves and shared the proceeds among his soldiers; the booty went to Carthage.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.602,-0.984" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Cartagena</span> <span class="jr-date">Winter before the march</span><p>Hannibal spent the winter after the siege here and sent his troops home to their own districts. He left his brother Hasdrubal in charge of Carthaginian Iberia.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.116,1.255" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a></span> <span class="jr-date">218 BCE</span><p>After crossing the Ebro at Edeba, the main column under Hannibal went along the coast. Each town on the way, Tarraco among them, was taken and garrisoned in turn.</p></li>
<li data-ll="44.256,4.648" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Pont-Saint-Esprit</span> <span class="jr-date">218 BCE</span><p>About 40 km upriver from Hannibal's camp on the Rhône, his lieutenant Hanno, son of Bomilcar, crossed on rafts. He then marched south to attack the Cavares in the rear while Hannibal crossed with the main army and the elephants.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Into the Alps and down into Italy</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="45.663,5.822" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Mont du Chat</span> <span class="jr-date">Autumn 218 BCE</span><p>In Dodge's reconstruction, the Allobroges held the pass here. Hannibal lit many camp-fires, seized the pass when they left it at night, and fought off an attack in which many baggage animals fell from the precipices.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.566,5.921" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a></span> <span class="jr-date">Autumn 218 BCE</span><p>According to Dodge, Hannibal took the town easily, stripped it of horses, captives, pack animals and grain, and ordered it destroyed to show what opposition would bring.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.676,6.393" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Albertville</span> <span class="jr-date">Autumn 218 BCE</span><p>In Dodge's account the Centrones met the army here with gifts, cattle and hostages. Hannibal distrusted them but hid it, and they guided the army for two days.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.680,6.884" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/little-st-bernard-pass">Little St Bernard Pass</a></span> <span class="jr-date">End of October 218 BCE</span><p>According to Dodge, the Centrones attacked in the narrowing pass by rolling boulders and killing many pack animals. The army rested two days, then three more. Historians dispute the pass: the Col de la Traversette, Col de Clapier, Col de Montgenèvre and a pass near Mont Cenis are also proposed.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.467,7.875" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivrea">Ivrea</a></span> <span class="jr-date">218 BCE</span><p>The descent was steep, a landslide blocked the path and many men and animals died. Hannibal encamped near modern Ivrea to restore the troops, with fewer than 35,000 of the 70,000 who had set out.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Hannibal encamped near Ivrea to restore his troops. The army had started with over 70,000 men and arrived in Italy with fewer than 35,000. Which pass it crossed is still disputed.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Hannibal's crossing of the Alps&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="paul" data-kids="1">
<h2>The Journeys of Paul the Apostle</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">c. 46–c. 60 CE &#183; <a href="/life/paul-the-apostle">Paul the Apostle</a> &#183; 21 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">According to the Acts of the Apostles, Paul made three missionary journeys, which Acts arranges as three separate accounts, and then travelled to Rome for his appeal to Caesar. It has been estimated that the journeys recorded in Acts covered over 12,000 kilometres by land and over 8,000 kilometres by sea.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">First missionary journey</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="36.200,36.150" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 46–49 CE</span><p>According to Acts, the Antioch community commissioned Paul and Barnabas, and Barnabas led at first. The traditional and majority dating of the first journey is 46–49 CE, against a minority dating of after 37 CE.</p></li>
<li data-ll="35.000,33.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 46–49 CE</span><p>In Cyprus, according to Acts, Paul rebuked and blinded Elymas the magician, who had been criticising their teachings.</p></li>
<li data-ll="36.960,30.854" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Perga</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 46–49 CE</span><p>Paul and Barnabas sailed to Perga in Pamphylia. There John Mark left them and returned to Jerusalem.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.306,31.189" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Pisidian Antioch</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 46–49 CE</span><p>On the Sabbath Paul and Barnabas went to the synagogue, where the leaders invited them to speak.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Second missionary journey</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="36.200,36.150" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a></span> <span class="jr-date">late autumn 49 CE</span><p>The second journey began in late autumn 49 CE, after the Council of Jerusalem. At Antioch Paul and Barnabas argued over taking John Mark, and parted: Barnabas took John Mark, and Silas joined Paul.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.664,32.211" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Lystra</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 49–50 CE</span><p>Paul and Silas visited Tarsus, Derbe and Lystra. At Lystra they met Timothy, a disciple who was spoken well of, and took him with them.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.012,24.285" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Philippi</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 49–50 CE</span><p>According to Acts, Paul cast a spirit of divination out of a servant girl. Her masters, who lost income from her soothsaying, seized Paul and Silas, and the two were put in jail.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.984,23.728" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 50 CE</span><p>Passing by Berea, Paul reached Athens, where he preached daily to the Jews and God-fearing Greeks in the synagogue and in the Agora. He was taken to the court of the Areopagus and preached to the elite there.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.906,22.879" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Corinth</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 50–51 CE</span><p>Paul arrived at Corinth and founded the church there, staying 18 months. The stay is dated from the reference in Acts to the proconsul Gallio, and Priscilla and Aquila became his helpers there.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Third missionary journey</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="10">
<li data-ll="37.941,27.339" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a></span> <span class="jr-date">before 56 CE</span><p>According to Acts, Paul travelled through Galatia and Phrygia and then to Ephesus, staying almost three years. He left after a silversmith incited a crowd against his preaching and pro-Artemis riots broke out.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.906,22.879" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Corinth</span> <span class="jr-date">56–57 CE</span><p>Paul went through Macedonia into Achaea and stayed in Greece, probably at Corinth, for three months. Commentators generally agree he dictated the Epistle to the Romans in this period.</p></li>
<li data-ll="39.752,26.159" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Troas</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 57 CE</span><p>Paul and his companions called at Troas on the way back to Jerusalem.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.531,27.276" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Miletus</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 57 CE</span><p>Paul and his companions called at Miletus on the way back to Jerusalem.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.267,35.200" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tyre</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 57 CE</span><p>Paul and his companions visited Tyre on the way back to Jerusalem, before a stop at Caesarea.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.777,35.234" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Jerusalem</span> <span class="jr-date">57 CE</span><p>Paul arrived with a collection of money for the local community, on what was his fifth and final visit. Acts says he was seized after Jews from Asia accused him of defiling the temple.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The journey to Rome</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="16">
<li data-ll="32.501,34.892" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Caesarea</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 57 CE</span><p>After a plot against him, Paul was taken to Caesarea and held under guard in Herod's headquarters. He was held there for two years by Felix, and when Festus proposed a trial in Jerusalem he appealed to Caesar.</p></li>
<li data-ll="35.900,14.400" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 60 CE</span><p>According to Acts, the ship was wrecked on Melita, identified with present-day Malta. The islanders showed Paul unusual kindness, and he was met there by Publius.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.069,15.287" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Syracuse</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 60 CE</span><p>After leaving Malta, Paul travelled on towards Rome by way of Syracuse.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.114,15.650" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/reggio-calabria">Rhegium</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 60 CE</span><p>Paul passed through Rhegium on his way from Malta to Rome, after Syracuse and before Puteoli.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.823,14.122" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/pozzuoli">Puteoli</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 60 CE</span><p>Paul's route from Malta to Rome ended with a stop at Puteoli, after Syracuse and Rhegium.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.893,12.483" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 60 CE</span><p>Paul arrived in Rome and, according to the traditional account, spent two years under house arrest. Acts ends with him preaching there from his rented home while awaiting trial.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">According to the traditional account, Paul reached Rome c. 60 CE and spent two years under house arrest. Acts ends with him preaching there while awaiting trial and does not mention his death, which is believed to have come after the Great Fire of Rome in July 64 and before 68.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Paul the Apostle&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="faxian" data-kids="1">
<h2>Faxian's Pilgrimage to India</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">399–412 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> &#183; 7 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Faxian, a Chinese Buddhist monk, set out from Chang'an in 399 at about the age of 60 with four companions, to find sacred Buddhist texts in India. He travelled west along the overland Silk Road, spent 10 years in India and came home by sea.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From China to India</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="34.308,108.858" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a></span> <span class="jr-date">399</span><p>Faxian set out from Chang'an, the capital of the Buddhist Later Qin dynasty, with four others, to look for sacred Buddhist texts.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.935,100.455" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhangye">Zhangye</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 399</span><p>At Zhangye five more pilgrims joined the party, which then travelled on across the desert and mountain passes towards India.</p></li>
<li data-ll="25.611,85.144" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a></span><p>Faxian entered India from the northwest and reached Pataliputra, which he declared a prosperous city. He collected Indian literature here and learned Sanskrit.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The return by sea</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="22.300,87.917" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tamralipti</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 409</span><p>Faxian left India from Tamralipti, a port he states to be on the eastern coast. Some of his Chinese companions chose to stay in India.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-7.312,109.418" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a></span><p>After two years in Sri Lanka, a violent storm drove his ship onto an island, probably Java. Faxian stayed there five months before taking another ship for southern China.</p></li>
<li data-ll="36.192,120.592" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Mount Lao</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 412</span><p>The second ship was also blown off course. It landed at Mount Lao in what is now Shandong, 30 kilometres east of Qingdao, after seventy days of dark skies and dwindling water.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.061,118.779" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a></span> <span class="jr-date">412</span><p>Faxian settled in what is now Nanjing. He wrote his book on the journey there around 414, then spent the following decade translating the sutras he had brought from India.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Faxian settled in Nanjing in 412 and wrote a book on his travels around 414. He spent the rest of his life translating the Buddhist texts he had brought back.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Faxian&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="hijra" data-kids="1">
<h2>The Hijrah: From Mecca to Medina</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">622 &#183; <a href="/life/muhammad">Muhammad and his followers</a> &#183; 4 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The Hijrah was the journey that the Islamic prophet Muhammad and his followers made from Mecca to Medina. Its date equates to 16 July 622 in the Julian calendar, and the year became the epoch of the Hijri calendars.</p>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="21.413,39.893" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">al-'Aqabah</span> <span class="jr-date">Pilgrimage season of 622</span><p>Muhammad met 75 Medinan converts, including two women, at al-Aqabah, and they took the second pledge there, also known as the pledge of war. Twelve delegates were chosen to oversee the pact.</p></li>
<li data-ll="21.422,39.826" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a></span> <span class="jr-date">May 622</span><p>After instructing his Meccan followers to relocate to Medina, Muhammad stayed behind to watch over them. In May 622 he left his home secretly with Abu Bakr.</p></li>
<li data-ll="21.383,39.850" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Mount Thawr</span> <span class="jr-date">622</span><p>Islamic tradition relates that Muhammad and Abu Bakr hid in a cave on Mount Thawr, about an hour's walk south of Mecca. After three days they set out for Medina on camels, with a guide, Abdallah ibn Arqat.</p></li>
<li data-ll="24.470,39.610" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a></span> <span class="jr-date">622</span><p>Medina, an oasis more than 260 miles (420 km) north of Mecca, was the destination. Its Arab tribes, the Aws and Khazraj, had fought each other for nearly a hundred years before 620.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The Meccans seized the wealth and belongings the migrants left behind, and Muhammad's followers suffered from poverty in Medina. In 638 the caliph Umar designated the year of the Hijrah as the first year of the Islamic calendar.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Hijrah&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="xuanzang" data-kids="1">
<h2>Xuanzang's Journey to India and Back</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">629–645 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> &#183; 23 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Xuanzang was a 7th-century Chinese Buddhist monk who sought original Sanskrit texts from India. Starting from Chang'an, he began a seventeen-year overland journey to India, defying his nation's ban on travel abroad.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Across Central Asia</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="34.308,108.858" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a></span> <span class="jr-date">627 or 629</span><p>At Chang'an, then under the rule of Emperor Taizong of Tang, Xuanzang developed the desire to visit India. Sources differ on whether he set out in 627 or 629, and most state 629.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.950,89.182" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Agni (Turfan)</a></span> <span class="jr-date">630</span><p>He arrived in the kingdom of Agni, where the Buddhist king and others tried to persuade him to give up his journey and teach them. He declined, and they equipped him with letters of introduction and funds.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.650,82.900" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Kucha</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 630</span><p>After a meeting with Turkic robbers who fought over their loot and dispersed, he reached Kuchi, which he reported had over one hundred monasteries and five thousand monks.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.839,75.291" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tokmak</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 630</span><p>He passed Aksu, crossed the Tian Shan and went on to Tokmak, where he met the great Khagan of the Göktürks and attended a feast.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.311,69.280" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 630</span><p>He continued west and then southwest to Tashkent, where he described monasteries with Buddha's footprints and statues.</p></li>
<li data-ll="36.758,66.899" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Bactra</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 630</span><p>He crossed a desert, icy valleys and the Pamir range, and came to Bactra, modern Balkh. He described a monastery there with a jewelled Buddha statue and a tooth relic of the Buddha.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.817,67.817" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Bamiyana</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 630</span><p>South of Bactra he crossed the Great Snow Mountains to Bamiyana, where his travelogue describes a colossal standing Buddha carved from rock, some one hundred and forty feet tall.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Through India</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="8">
<li data-ll="33.746,72.787" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a></span><p>He arrived in Taxila after crossing a river said to hold poisonous dragons and evil animals, and visited a major monastery of the Sautrantika school.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.500,76.000" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmira</a></span><p>In Kashmira he was received by the king and by monks of the Jayendra monastery. He recorded over 100 monasteries and more than 5000 monks.</p></li>
<li data-ll="26.740,83.889" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kushinagara</a></span><p>He came to Kushinagara, where he found the towns and villages deserted and in a dilapidated condition, and described a large brick temple with a reclining Buddha.</p></li>
<li data-ll="25.319,83.013" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Baranasi</a></span><p>At Baranasi he described a densely populated city whose people were enormously wealthy. He found few Buddhists there and over one hundred deva temples, most dedicated to Shiva.</p></li>
<li data-ll="24.695,84.993" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a></span><p>He visited Gaya and the Bodhi tree, and described the Mahabodhi monastery nearby with its many buildings and its decorative paintings.</p></li>
<li data-ll="25.137,85.444" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a></span><p>At Nalanda he lived among several thousand monks and studied logic, grammar, Sanskrit and the Yogacara school of Buddhism with his teacher Silabhadra.</p></li>
<li data-ll="16.576,80.357" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Amaravati</span><p>Turning south, he stayed at Amaravati and studied the Abhidhammapitakam texts. He observed many viharas there, some of them deserted.</p></li>
<li data-ll="12.831,79.708" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a></span><p>He went on to Kanchi, the capital of the Pallavas, and said that it was a strong centre of Buddhism.</p></li>
<li data-ll="25.137,85.444" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a></span><p>After travelling on through Nasik, Ajanta and Malwa, he returned to Nalanda.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Home to Chang'an</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="17">
<li data-ll="27.031,79.922" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kanyakubja</a></span><p>At the request of Emperor Harshavardhana he attended a great Buddhist assembly at Kanyakubja, which monarchs, monks, Hindus and Jains also attended.</p></li>
<li data-ll="25.450,81.850" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Prayag</a></span><p>Emperor Harsha invited him to the Kumbha Mela at Prayag, where he saw the Emperor's distribution of gifts to the poor.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.031,79.922" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kanyakubja</a></span><p>He returned to Kanyakubja, where Emperor Harsha gave him a grand farewell, and set out for China through the Khyber Pass.</p></li>
<li data-ll="39.450,75.983" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a></span><p>On the way back to China Xuanzang travelled through the Khyber Pass and passed through Kashgar.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.101,79.933" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Khotan</span><p>Xuanzang passed through Khotan on the same homeward route to China, after Kashgar.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.141,94.664" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a></span><p>Xuanzang passed through Dunhuang on his way back to China, before he arrived at the capital, Chang'an.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.308,108.858" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a></span> <span class="jr-date">7th day of the first month of 645</span><p>He arrived in the capital, Chang'an, 16 years after he left Chinese territory, and a great procession celebrated his return.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">He arrived back in Chang'an in 645, 16 years after he left Chinese territory, and a great procession celebrated his return. His biography says he brought over six hundred Mahayana and Hinayana texts, seven statues of the Buddha and more than a hundred sarira relics.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Xuanzang&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="jianzhen" data-kids="1">
<h2>Jianzhen's Voyages to Japan</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">748–754 &#183; Jianzhen &#183; 7 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Jianzhen, a Tang Chinese monk also known as Ganjin, was invited by an emissary from Japan to lecture there. Between 743 and 754 he attempted the crossing some six times, and arrived on the sixth attempt.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The fifth attempt and the road back</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="32.391,119.436" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a></span> <span class="jr-date">summer 748</span><p>Jianzhen left Yangzhou by ship on his fifth attempt to reach Japan. He had lived in the area since birth and had become a monk there at fourteen.</p></li>
<li data-ll="29.989,122.205" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Zhoushan Archipelago</span> <span class="jr-date">748</span><p>The ship made it to the Zhoushan Archipelago, off the coast of modern Zhejiang, before it was blown off course.</p></li>
<li data-ll="20.033,110.324" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan Island</a></span> <span class="jr-date">748</span><p>The ship ended up in the Yande commandery on Hainan Island. Jianzhen was forced to make his way back to Yangzhou by land.</p></li>
<li data-ll="29.705,116.002" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Jiujiang</span> <span class="jr-date">after 748</span><p>On the way back he lectured at a number of monasteries. He went along the Gan River to Jiujiang, and from there down the Yangtze River.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.391,119.436" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a></span> <span class="jr-date">close to three years after 748</span><p>The failed attempt took him close to three years. By the time he came back to Yangzhou he was blind from an infection.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The successful crossing</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="31.597,130.557" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a></span> <span class="jr-date">20 December 753</span><p>In autumn 753 the blind Jianzhen joined a Japanese emissary ship returning home. After a sea journey of several months the group landed at Kagoshima, on Kyūshū.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.685,135.805" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Nara</span><p>The group reached Nara in the spring of the next year and was welcomed by the Emperor. Jianzhen presided over Tōdai-ji and later founded Tōshōdai-ji in the western part of the city.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Jianzhen reached Nara in the spring of 754, blind, and was welcomed by the Emperor. He founded Tōshōdai-ji and died in Japan in 763.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Jianzhen&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="first-crusade" data-kids="0">
<h2>The First Crusade: From Clermont to Jerusalem</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1095–1099 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">The crusader armies</a> &#183; 18 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Pope Urban II called for an armed pilgrimage to Jerusalem at the Council of Clermont in 1095, in answer to a request from the Byzantine emperor Alexios I Komnenos for help against the Seljuk-led Turks. Bands of poor Christians set out first, followed in August 1096 by armies led by princes.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The call, and the Rhineland massacres</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="45.780,3.087" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/clermont-ferrand">Clermont</a></span> <span class="jr-date">27 November 1095</span><p>Urban II toured France from July 1095. At the ten-day Council of Clermont he preached to a large audience of French nobles and clergy, and the crowd answered, according to one version of the speech, with cries of Deus lo volt.</p></li>
<li data-ll="49.317,8.434" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Speyer</span> <span class="jr-date">May 1096</span><p>Months before the official departure, Emicho of Flonheim and his followers attacked the Jews at Speyer and Worms. The Bishop of Speyer had given the city's Jews a walled quarter a decade earlier to protect them from Christian violence.</p></li>
<li data-ll="49.999,8.274" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a></span> <span class="jr-date">End of May 1096</span><p>Emicho was joined by other crusaders and many locals in destroying the Jewish community of Mainz. One Jewish woman killed her children rather than let the crusaders do it, and the chief rabbi Kalonymus Ben Meshullam killed himself in anticipation of being killed.</p></li>
<li data-ll="50.942,6.958" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1096</span><p>Emicho's company went on to Cologne, and others continued to Trier, Metz and other cities. Church leaders did not sanction the violence, and the Archbishop of Cologne did his best to protect the Jews, though some took money in return.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">To Constantinople and across Anatolia</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="41.012,28.976" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></span> <span class="jr-date">August 1096</span><p>Peter the Hermit's army reached Constantinople in August and joined the force of Walter Sans Avoir. Their pillaging outside the city led Alexios to ferry them across the Bosporus within a week.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.720,29.500" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Civetot</span> <span class="jr-date">October 1096</span><p>Across the Bosporus the crusaders split up and plundered the countryside near Nicaea. Walter's and Peter's followers, led by about 50 knights, met the Turks at Civetot, where Turkish archers destroyed the army and killed Walter.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.012,28.976" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></span> <span class="jr-date">November 1096 to April 1097</span><p>The four main armies took different routes, some through the Balkans and some across the Adriatic, and gathered outside the walls. Alexios asked their leaders to swear fealty to him; Godfrey took the oath first, and Raymond alone only promised to do no harm.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.429,29.720" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a></span> <span class="jr-date">14 May 1097</span><p>The crusaders besieged the Seljuk capital while Kilij Arslan was away, and drove him back when he returned. After Alexios had ships rolled overland to the lake, the Turkish garrison surrendered on 18 June, and the crusaders were forbidden to loot.</p></li>
<li data-ll="39.775,30.517" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1 July 1097</span><p>The army marched in two groups. Kilij Arslan's mounted archers surrounded the Normans, who formed a tight defensive ring; the French came up, Godfrey broke the Turkish line, and the Turks fled.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Antioch and the road to Jerusalem</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="10">
<li data-ll="36.200,36.150" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a></span> <span class="jr-date">2 June 1098</span><p>The siege began on 20 October 1097 and lasted eight months, with many crusaders dying of starvation. An Armenian, Firouz, helped Bohemond enter the city, and in the sack the crusaders killed most of the Muslim inhabitants and many Christians.</p></li>
<li data-ll="35.643,36.668" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Ma'arrat al-Numan</span> <span class="jr-date">January 1099</span><p>In December, following the siege of the town, some historians describe cannibalism among the crusaders, although no contemporary Muslim chronicle mentions it. In January Raymond dismantled its walls and began the march south, barefoot and dressed as a pilgrim.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.530,36.040" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Arqa</span> <span class="jr-date">14 February 1099</span><p>Raymond planned to take Tripoli but first besieged Arqa. The siege lasted until 13 May, and the crusaders left having captured nothing. The ordeal by fire of Peter Bartholomew, who died of his burns, discredited the Holy Lance.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.437,35.834" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tripoli</span> <span class="jr-date">13 May 1099</span><p>The emir Jalal al-Mulk Abu'l Hasan supplied the crusader army with horses and vowed to convert to Christianity if the crusaders defeated the Fatimids. The army then passed Beirut on 19 May and Tyre on 23 May.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.927,34.862" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a></span> <span class="jr-date">3 June 1099</span><p>Turning inland at Jaffa, the crusaders reached Ramla, which its inhabitants had abandoned. They set up a bishopric at the Church of St George before continuing to Jerusalem.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.704,35.206" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a></span> <span class="jr-date">6 June 1099</span><p>Godfrey sent Tancred and Gaston of Béarn to capture Bethlehem, where Tancred flew his banner over the Church of the Nativity.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.777,35.234" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Jerusalem</span> <span class="jr-date">7 June 1099</span><p>Many crusaders wept at the sight of the city. The Fatimid governor had expelled its Christian inhabitants and poisoned wells nearby. An estimated 12,000 men remained, and a first assault on 13 June was repulsed at the inner wall.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.777,35.234" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Jerusalem</span> <span class="jr-date">15 July 1099</span><p>The crusaders stormed the city. Muslims were indiscriminately killed, and Jews who had taken refuge in their synagogue died when it was burnt down. Tancred's prisoners in the mosque were killed the next day. Some historians think later sources exaggerate the scale.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.662,34.546" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Ascalon</span> <span class="jr-date">9 August 1099</span><p>A Fatimid force of 20,000 landed at Ascalon. A crusader force of 1,200 knights and 9,000 foot soldiers attacked at dawn and routed it, but the city stayed in Muslim hands. Most crusaders then returned home.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The crusaders stormed Jerusalem on 15 July 1099, and a large fraction of its residents were massacred. A Fatimid counterattack was repulsed at Ascalon, and most of the crusaders then went home.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;First Crusade&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="bar-sauma" data-kids="1">
<h2>Rabban Bar Sauma's Journeys from Khanbaliq to Persia and Europe</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">c. 1220–1294 &#183; Rabban Bar Sauma &#183; 17 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Rabban Bar Sauma, a Church of the East monk born in or near present-day Beijing, set out with his student Markos on a pilgrimage to Jerusalem. Danger on the routes turned them back to the Ilkhanate, and in 1287 he went to Europe as ambassador of Arghun, who wanted an alliance against the Mamluk Sultanate.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The pilgrimage from China to Persia</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="39.904,116.408" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Khanbaliq</a></span><p>Bar Sauma was born in or near the site of present-day Beijing, then called Zhongdu and later Khanbaliq. In middle age he and his student Markos set out from Yuan China for Jerusalem.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.101,79.933" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Khotan</span><p>The two monks passed through the former Tangut country and then Khotan on the road west.</p></li>
<li data-ll="39.450,75.983" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a></span><p>Kashgar came next on the monks' route west, after the former Tangut country and Khotan.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.883,71.367" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/taraz">Taraz</a></span><p>They crossed into the Syr Darya valley at Taraz, on the way towards Khorasan.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.392,46.239" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/maragha">Maragha</a></span><p>The monks went on by way of Maragha, now in Azerbaijan, before turning to Mosul.</p></li>
<li data-ll="36.342,43.129" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a></span><p>Mosul was the last place named on the monks' route before they arrived at Ani in the Kingdom of Georgia.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.508,43.573" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/ani">Ani</a></span> <span class="jr-date">before 1281</span><p>The monks arrived at Ani in the Kingdom of Georgia. Warnings of danger on the routes to southern Syria turned them from their purpose, and they went to the Ilkhanate in Persia instead.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.315,44.366" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1281</span><p>Military conflict delayed the monks' return to China, and they stayed in Baghdad. When the Patriarch died, Markos was elected in his place as Yahballaha III in 1281.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The embassy to Europe</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="9">
<li data-ll="41.012,28.976" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1287</span><p>Bar Sauma crossed the Black Sea by boat to Constantinople, where he had an audience with Andronicus II Palaeologus. His writings describe the Hagia Sophia with enthusiasm.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.751,14.993" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mount-etna">Mount Etna</a></span> <span class="jr-date">18 June 1287</span><p>Sailing to Italy, the ship passed Sicily, and Bar Sauma saw and recorded a great eruption of Mount Etna.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.628,14.374" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Bay of Sorrento</a></span> <span class="jr-date">24 June 1287</span><p>A few days after arriving, he watched a naval battle in the Bay of Sorrento between the fleets of Charles II and James II of Aragon. According to Bar Sauma, James II won and his forces killed 12,000 men.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.893,12.483" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1287</span><p>Bar Sauma reached Rome too late to meet Pope Honorius IV, who had recently died. He negotiated with the cardinals instead and visited St. Peter's Basilica.</p></li>
<li data-ll="44.407,8.934" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Genoa</span> <span class="jr-date">winter 1287–1288</span><p>On the way to Paris he stopped in Tuscany and the Republic of Genoa, and spent the winter in Genoa, a banking capital.</p></li>
<li data-ll="48.857,2.352" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1288</span><p>In France Bar Sauma spent a month with King Philip the Fair, who received the embassy favourably, gave him many presents and sent the nobleman Gobert de Helleville to accompany him back.</p></li>
<li data-ll="44.838,-0.579" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Bordeaux</span> <span class="jr-date">1288</span><p>In Gascony, then in English hands, Bar Sauma met King Edward I of England, probably at Bordeaux. Edward was enthusiastic but could not join an alliance because of conflict at home with the Welsh and the Scots.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.893,12.483" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a></span> <span class="jr-date">Palm Sunday 1288</span><p>Back in Rome, the newly elected Pope Nicholas IV received him cordially and gave him communion on Palm Sunday. The Pope also entrusted him with a tiara for Mar Yahballaha.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.315,44.366" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1288</span><p>Bar Sauma returned to Baghdad in 1288 with messages and gifts from the European leaders. He lived there for the rest of his life and died in 1294.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Bar Sauma returned to Baghdad in 1288 with messages and gifts from the European leaders. The exchanges towards an alliance proved fruitless, and he died in Baghdad in 1294.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Rabban Bar Sauma&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="carpini" data-kids="1">
<h2>Giovanni da Pian del Carpine's Mission to the Great Khan</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1245–1247 &#183; Giovanni da Pian del Carpine &#183; 7 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Pope Innocent IV sent the Franciscan Giovanni da Pian del Carpine as papal legate to the Great Khan of the Mongol Empire, partly in protest at the Mongol invasion of Christendom and partly to learn the Khan's intentions and military strength. He carried a letter from the Pope.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Lyon to the Volga</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="45.767,4.835" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a></span> <span class="jr-date">16 April 1245</span><p>Giovanni set out from Lyon, where the Pope then resided, on Easter day. He was sixty-three and bore the Pope's letter to the Great Khan. Stephen of Bohemia travelled with him.</p></li>
<li data-ll="49.745,31.456" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Kaniv</span> <span class="jr-date">1245</span><p>Stephen of Bohemia broke down at Kaniv, near Kiev, and was left behind. Giovanni and his interpreter, the Franciscan Benedict of Poland, entered the Tatar posts here and went on towards the Don and Volga.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">To the Mongol court</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="3">
<li data-ll="47.198,102.824" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Sira Orda</a></span> <span class="jr-date">22 July 1246</span><p>After leaving Batu's camp on the Volga on Easter day, the envoys rode across the steppe and reached the imperial camp called Sira Orda, near Karakorum. They had ridden an estimated 3,000 miles in 106 days.</p></li>
<li data-ll="47.198,102.824" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Golden Ordu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">24 August 1246</span><p>The friars witnessed the formal enthronement of Güyük at another camp nearby, the Golden Ordu, and were then presented to the new emperor. Güyük refused to become Christian.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The way home</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="50.450,30.524" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a></span> <span class="jr-date">10 June 1247</span><p>The Khan dismissed the expedition in November, and the friars made a long winter journey home, often lying on the bare snow. They reached Kiev, where Slavonic Christians welcomed them with festive hospitality.</p></li>
<li data-ll="50.942,6.958" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1247</span><p>The travellers crossed the Rhine at Cologne on their way to the Pope.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.767,4.835" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1247</span><p>They found the Pope still at Lyon and delivered their report and the Khan's letter.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Güyük Khan refused to become Christian and demanded that the Pope and the rulers of Europe come and swear allegiance to him. Giovanni brought the Khan's letter back to the Pope at Lyon and later wrote the Ystoria Mongalorum, the oldest European account of the Mongols.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Giovanni da Pian del Carpine&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="rubruck" data-kids="1">
<h2>William of Rubruck's Mission to the Mongols</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1253–1255 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/william-of-rubruck">William of Rubruck</a> &#183; 6 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">William of Rubruck was a Franciscan friar who, with the support of Louis IX of France, undertook a mission to the Great Khan Möngke of the Mongol Empire. He set out on 7 May 1253 to convert the Tatars to Christianity, and on his return wrote an account addressed to the king.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">To the court of the Great Khan</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="41.012,28.976" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></span> <span class="jr-date">7 May 1253</span><p>William set out on Louis IX's orders on 7 May 1253. He stopped in Constantinople to confer with Baldwin of Hainaut, who had recently returned from Karakorum, and received letters from the emperor to some of the Tatar chiefs.</p></li>
<li data-ll="44.851,34.972" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/sudak">Sudak</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1253</span><p>After reaching the Crimean town of Sudak, William continued with oxen and carts. According to William, merchants from Turkey and from Russia met at Sudak, carrying cloth and spices one way and furs the other.</p></li>
<li data-ll="47.169,47.452" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Sarai</span> <span class="jr-date">1253</span><p>Nine days after crossing the Don he met Sartaq, who sent him on to Sartaq's father Batu Khan at Sarai near the Volga. William is said to have threatened Batu with eternal damnation if he did not convert. Batu refused, and sent the party on to Möngke.</p></li>
<li data-ll="47.198,102.824" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a></span> <span class="jr-date">4 January 1254</span><p>The party arrived in late December, a journey of 9,000 kilometres, and were received courteously. William had an audience with the Great Khan on 4 January 1254 and described the city's walls, markets and temples, and its quarters for Muslim and Chinese craftsmen.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The return</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="39.209,45.412" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Nakhchivan</span> <span class="jr-date">December 1254</span><p>William and his companions spent Christmas of 1254 at Nakhchivan, in present-day Azerbaijan, on the way back towards the Mediterranean coast.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.437,35.834" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">County of Tripoli</span> <span class="jr-date">15 August 1255</span><p>William and his companions reached the County of Tripoli on 15 August 1255, ending the journey.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">William reached the County of Tripoli on 15 August 1255. Unable to meet the king in person, he wrote him a long letter in Latin, the Itinerarium, describing his journey.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;William of Rubruck&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="marco-polo" data-kids="1">
<h2>Marco Polo's Journey to China and Back</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1271–1295 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> &#183; 11 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Marco Polo, a Venetian merchant, set off in 1271 at the age of seventeen with his father Niccolò and his uncle Maffeo to fulfil a request from Kublai Khan. He lived in the emperor's lands for 17 years before the family returned to Venice.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Venice to Kublai Khan's court</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="45.440,12.332" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1271</span><p>Marco, aged seventeen, set off with his father and uncle for Asia. His father and uncle had already visited Kublai Khan and returned to Venice in 1269, meeting Marco for the first time.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.926,35.084" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Acre</span> <span class="jr-date">1271</span><p>They stayed for a few months and spoke with Archdeacon Tedaldo Visconti. When he was elected Pope, he entrusted them with letters for the Great Khan and sent two Dominican fathers with them as legates.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.067,56.460" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Hormuz</span> <span class="jr-date">1271</span><p>They rode camels to the Persian port, wanting to sail into China. The ships there were not seaworthy, so they continued overland along the Silk Road. A caravan they joined was attacked by bandits.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.358,116.185" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Shangdu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">between 1271 and 1275</span><p>The Polos were welcomed by Kublai into his palace, and Marco, about 21, was appointed an emissary. Some scholars have doubted that he reached China at all, but most now accept that he did.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.391,119.436" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a></span> <span class="jr-date">before 1284</span><p>The book says Marco ruled the city for three years, but David Morgan notes that no Chinese source mentions him. The earliest manuscripts differ on his role, and Paul Pelliot thought he may have served in the salt monopoly.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The way home, 1292–1295</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="24.914,118.586" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Zaitun</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1292</span><p>Kublai let the Polos go with a wedding party escorting a princess to Persia. The party left from Zaitun in southern China on a fleet of 14 junks.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-0.299,101.271" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a></span> <span class="jr-date">after 1292</span><p>The fleet sailed to the port of Singapore and north to Sumatra, then around the southern tip of India and across the Arabian Sea. The voyage was perilous.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.067,56.460" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Hormuz</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 1293</span><p>Of the six hundred people in the convoy, not counting the crew, only eighteen had survived, including all three Polos. They left the wedding party here and went overland towards the Black Sea.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.005,39.722" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a></span><p>From Hormuz the Polos travelled overland to the port of Trebizond on the Black Sea, now Trabzon.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.012,28.976" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></span> <span class="jr-date">after 1293</span><p>In Persia they had learned that Arghun Khan had died, and Kököchin became a wife of his son Ghazan. After leaving the princess, the Polos travelled overland to Constantinople.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.440,12.332" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1295</span><p>They returned to Venice after 24 years with many riches and treasures, having travelled almost 15,000 miles. Venice was at war with Genoa, and Marco was later captured by the Genoese.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The Polos reached Venice in 1295 after 24 years, with many riches and treasures. Marco dictated his account to Rustichello da Pisa while a prisoner of Genoa, and scholars still debate parts of it, though studies by Morgan, Haw and Vogel largely hold that he did go to China.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Marco Polo&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="mansa-musa" data-kids="1">
<h2>Mansa Musa's Pilgrimage to Mecca</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1324–1325 &#183; <a href="/life/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa of Mali</a> &#183; 7 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Mansa Musa, ruler of the Mali Empire, made the hajj to Mecca between 1324 and 1325, travelling with a very large entourage and a great supply of gold. He left his son Muhammad to rule in his absence.</p>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="29.976,31.133" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Pyramids of Giza</span> <span class="jr-date">July 1324</span><p>Musa and his entourage arrived at the outskirts of Cairo in July 1324 and camped for three days by the Pyramids of Giza.</p></li>
<li data-ll="30.044,31.236" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Cairo</span> <span class="jr-date">19 July 1324</span><p>Musa crossed the Nile into Cairo on 19 July and met the Mamluk sultan al-Nasir Muhammad. He stayed in the Qarafa district for three months and left on 18 October with the official caravan to Mecca.</p></li>
<li data-ll="24.470,39.610" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1324</span><p>Musa gave gifts to fellow pilgrims and the people of Medina as he travelled on towards Mecca.</p></li>
<li data-ll="21.422,39.826" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1324–1325</span><p>In Mecca a conflict broke out between Malian pilgrims and Turkic pilgrims in the Masjid al-Haram. Swords were drawn, but Musa persuaded his men to back down.</p></li>
<li data-ll="29.967,32.533" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Suez</span> <span class="jr-date">1325</span><p>Musa's party returned separately from the main caravan. By the time they reached Suez, many Malian pilgrims had died of cold, starvation or bandit raids, and much of their supplies was lost.</p></li>
<li data-ll="16.267,-0.050" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1325</span><p>According to the Tarikh al-Sudan, Gao submitted to Musa's rule as he travelled through on his return. This is doubted: Ibn Khaldun credits Musa's general Saghmanja with the conquest, and another account places it under Mansa Sakura.</p></li>
<li data-ll="16.773,-3.007" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1325</span><p>The Tarikh al-Sudan says Timbuktu also submitted to Musa on his return, but it is unlikely that a group of pilgrims, even armed, could have conquered a wealthy and powerful city.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Musa returned towards Mali with scholars such as the Andalusi poet Abu Ishaq al-Sahili, after going into debt on the way back. According to the Tarikh al-Sudan, Gao and Timbuktu submitted to his rule as he passed through.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Mansa Musa&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="ibn-battuta" data-kids="1">
<h2>The Travels of Ibn Battuta</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1325–1354 &#183; <a href="/life/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> &#183; 24 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Ibn Battuta, a Maghrebi Muslim scholar from Tangier, set off in 1325 at the age of 21 to make the hajj to Mecca. Over thirty years he visited much of Africa, Asia and the Iberian Peninsula, and the total is put at around 117,000 km.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The first pilgrimage and Iraq, 1325–1327</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="35.769,-5.804" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a></span> <span class="jr-date">14 June 1325</span><p>Ibn Battuta set off from his home town at the age of 21 to perform a hajj to Mecca, a journey that would ordinarily take sixteen months. He did not return to Morocco for 24 years.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.198,29.892" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a></span> <span class="jr-date">early spring 1326</span><p>He arrived at the port, then part of the Bahri Mamluk empire, after a journey of over 3,500 km. There Sheikh Burhanuddin is supposed to have foretold that he would become a world traveller.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.513,36.292" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a></span> <span class="jr-date">August 1326</span><p>He reached Damascus by a side trip from Cairo, a route the Mamluk authorities kept safe for pilgrims because of the holy places along it. He spent Ramadan there, in August, before joining a caravan south to Medina.</p></li>
<li data-ll="21.422,39.826" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a></span> <span class="jr-date">November 1326</span><p>He completed his first pilgrimage and took the honorific status of El-Hajji. Rather than going home, he decided to carry on travelling, choosing the Ilkhanate, a Mongol khanate to the northeast, as his next destination.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.315,44.366" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a></span> <span class="jr-date">June 1327</span><p>After a six-month detour into Iran that took him to Isfahan and Shiraz, he arrived in Baghdad. Parts of the city were still ruined from the damage done by Hulagu Khan's army in 1258.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Arabia and the East African coast, c. 1329–1332</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="12.800,45.033" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. 1329 or 1331</span><p>After the hajj he went to Jeddah and followed the Red Sea coast in small boats, then through Yemen, where he met the Rasulid king at Taiz. He probably went on directly to Aden; his visit to Sanaa is doubtful.</p></li>
<li data-ll="2.039,45.342" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Mogadishu</span> <span class="jr-date">1332</span><p>He sailed from Aden to Zeila and Cape Guardafui, about a week at each. At Mogadishu he found a Somali sultan, Abu Bakr ibn Shaikh 'Umar, and many rich merchants selling fine fabric abroad, including to Egypt.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-8.982,39.517" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani">Kilwa</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1330</span><p>Kilwa was an important transit centre of the gold trade. He described it as one of the finest and most beautifully built towns, with wooden buildings, and wrote favourably of its ruler, Sultan al-Hasan ibn Sulaiman.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Anatolia, Constantinople and Central Asia, 1330–1333</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="9">
<li data-ll="36.908,30.696" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Antalya</span> <span class="jr-date">1330 or 1332</span><p>Having crossed to Anatolia on a Genoese ship from Latakia to Alanya, he went west to Antalya. There he met the fityan associations of young artisans, whose hospitality impressed him. His later route across Anatolia is confused in the Rihla.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.012,28.976" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></span> <span class="jr-date">late 1332 (or 1334)</span><p>He joined the expedition taking Princess Bayalun home to give birth, his first journey beyond the Islamic world. He met the emperor Andronikos III Palaiologos, visited Hagia Sophia and spoke with an Orthodox priest about Jerusalem.</p></li>
<li data-ll="39.655,66.976" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1331–1334</span><p>After a month in Constantinople he returned to Astrakhan and Sarai, then went on to Bukhara and Samarkand. He visited the Chagatai khan Tarmashirin and noted the city walls still ruined from the Mongol invasion of 1220.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">India, the Maldives and China, 1333–1346</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="12">
<li data-ll="28.667,77.217" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a></span> <span class="jr-date">after 12 September 1333</span><p>He crossed the Hindu Kush and reached the Indus on 12 September 1333, then went to Delhi, where Sultan Muhammad bin Tughluq appointed him a qadi. For six years he alternated between favour and suspicion of treason.</p></li>
<li data-ll="11.248,75.780" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Calicut</span> <span class="jr-date">after 1341</span><p>Given charge of an embassy to China in 1341, he was robbed and kidnapped by bandits on the way to the coast. At Calicut he was the guest of the Zamorin, and a storm sank one of the expedition's ships.</p></li>
<li data-ll="4.175,73.508" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Malé</span> <span class="jr-date">after 1341</span><p>The leaders of the recently Islamised islands gave him pearls, gold jewellery and slaves and made leaving by ship impossible. He became chief judge, married into the royal family, and resigned after his strict rulings antagonised the rulers.</p></li>
<li data-ll="5.134,97.201" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Samudra Pasai</span> <span class="jr-date">1345</span><p>After a 40-day voyage he was the guest of the pious sultan Al-Malik Al-Zahir Jamal-ad-Din for about two weeks. No territory east of Samudra Pasai was ruled by a Muslim, and the sultan sent him on to China.</p></li>
<li data-ll="24.914,118.586" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1345</span><p>Muslims called the city Zaitun, but he found no olives. The head of the local Muslim merchants welcomed him with flags, drums and trumpets. Some scholars have questioned whether he reached China at all, suspecting passages borrowed from other authors.</p></li>
<li data-ll="39.904,116.408" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a></span> <span class="jr-date">after 1345</span><p>He is said to have called himself the long-lost ambassador from Delhi and to have been invited to the Yuan court. Scholars point to numerous errors in his account of China, such as confusing the Yellow River with the Grand Canal.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Home by way of the Black Death, Spain and the Sahara, 1348–1354</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="18">
<li data-ll="33.513,36.292" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1348</span><p>He arrived intending to retrace his first hajj and learned that his father had died fifteen years earlier. The Black Death had struck, and he later returned to Damascus in July, when the toll had reached 2,400 victims each day.</p></li>
<li data-ll="35.769,-5.804" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1349</span><p>After one last detour to Sardinia he returned to Tangier by way of Fez, nearly a quarter of a century after leaving. He found that his mother had also died a few months before.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.175,-3.600" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1350</span><p>He joined Muslims leaving Tangier to defend Gibraltar against Alfonso XI of Castile and León. The Black Death had killed Alfonso before he arrived, so he turned the trip into a sight-seeing tour ending in Granada.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.280,-4.280" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a></span> <span class="jr-date">autumn 1351</span><p>He left Fez and made his way to this town on the northern edge of the Sahara. There he bought a number of camels and stayed for four months before setting out with a caravan.</p></li>
<li data-ll="23.000,-4.200" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Taghaza</span> <span class="jr-date">1352</span><p>The caravan reached the salt mines after 25 days. The local buildings were made from slabs of salt cut by the slaves of the Masufa tribe, and he recorded that the place was plagued by flies and the water was brackish.</p></li>
<li data-ll="16.773,-3.007" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">February</span><p>After meeting Mansa Suleyman at the capital of the Mali Empire, he travelled by camel to Timbuktu, at that time a small and relatively unimportant city. After a short stay he went down the Niger to Gao in a canoe.</p></li>
<li data-ll="16.267,-0.050" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1353</span><p>After a month in Gao, then an important commercial centre, he left with a large caravan. A message from the Sultan of Morocco ordered him home, and he set off for Sijilmasa in September 1353.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">He returned to Morocco early in 1354 and, at the suggestion of the Marinid ruler Abu Inan Faris, dictated an account of his travels to Ibn Juzayy. Modern scholars doubt that he visited every place the account describes.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Ibn Battuta&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="zheng-he" data-kids="1">
<h2>Zheng He's Treasure Voyages</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1405–1433 &#183; <a href="/life/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> &#183; 26 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The Yongle Emperor ordered the construction of a treasure fleet in 1403, and Admiral Zheng He was commissioned to command it. It made seven voyages to the South China Sea and the Indian Ocean between 1405 and 1433, six under the Yongle Emperor and the seventh under the Xuande Emperor.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">First voyage, 1405–1407</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="32.061,118.779" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a></span> <span class="jr-date">autumn 1405</span><p>The fleet assembled at Nanjing in the autumn of 1405 and made ready to depart. The Yongle Emperor had held a banquet for the crew the evening before the maiden voyage.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.000,108.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1405</span><p>After waiting at Changle for the northeast monsoon, the fleet left the Fujian coast. Champa was the first foreign destination, and the port of Qui Nhon was always the first the fleet visited.</p></li>
<li data-ll="2.200,102.251" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1405–1407</span><p>The fleet called at Malacca on the way out and stopped there again on its return journey to China.</p></li>
<li data-ll="11.248,75.780" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Calicut</span> <span class="jr-date">December 1406 to April 1407</span><p>On the Malabar Coast, the fleet went as far as Calicut. Mills states that it may have stayed four months there, from December to April 1407.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-2.983,104.764" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1407</span><p>On the return trip Zheng and his associates fought the pirate fleet of Chen Zuyi, who had seized Palembang and dominated the route through the Strait of Malacca. Chen's fleet was defeated.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.061,118.779" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a></span> <span class="jr-date">2 October 1407</span><p>The fleet returned to Nanjing. Foreign envoys from Calicut, Quilon, Semudera, Aru, Malacca and other nations had come with it to present tribute at the Ming court.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Second voyage, 1407–1409</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="-7.312,109.418" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a></span> <span class="jr-date">late 1407 or early 1408</span><p>The fleet settled the enmity between Ming China and Java. In a civil war a King of West Java had killed 170 members of a Chinese embassy, and the Ming court demanded 60,000 liang of gold in compensation.</p></li>
<li data-ll="11.248,75.780" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Calicut</span> <span class="jr-date">late 1407 or early 1408</span><p>The fleet was tasked with the formal investiture of Mana Vikraan as King of Calicut. A tablet was placed in Calicut to commemorate the relationship between China and India.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Third voyage, 1409–1411</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="9">
<li data-ll="6.033,80.216" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/galle">Galle</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1410</span><p>On the outward journey the fleet landed at Galle in Ceylon. There Zheng set up a trilingual inscription in Chinese, Tamil and Persian, praising the Buddha, the local god Tenavarai Nayanar and Allah.</p></li>
<li data-ll="6.900,79.916" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/sri-jayawardenepura-kotte">Kotte</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1411</span><p>On the homeward journey Zheng and 2,000 troops went overland into Kotte, where King Alakeshvara had lured them. The Chinese invaded Kotte, captured its capital and took Alakeshvara and his family captive.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Fourth voyage, 1413–1415</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="11">
<li data-ll="27.067,56.460" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Hormuz</span><p>The fleet sailed beyond Calicut for the first time, through the Maldive and Laccadive Islands to Hormuz.</p></li>
<li data-ll="5.134,97.201" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Semudera</span> <span class="jr-date">1415</span><p>On the way home the fleet stopped in northern Sumatra, where Zheng was ordered to attack the usurper Sekandar. The Ming forces defeated him and pursued his forces to Lambri, where they captured him, his wife and his child.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Fifth voyage, 1417–1419</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="13">
<li data-ll="24.914,118.586" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a></span> <span class="jr-date">31 May 1417</span><p>Zheng may have left the Chinese coast in the autumn of 1417, first making port at Quanzhou to load porcelain and other goods. A Ming tablet there records him burning incense for divine protection for the voyage.</p></li>
<li data-ll="12.800,45.033" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a></span> <span class="jr-date">January 1419</span><p>A Yemeni chronicle reports that Chinese ships reached the Aden coast in January 1419, and that they did not leave the Rasulid capital at Ta'izz before 19 March.</p></li>
<li data-ll="2.039,45.342" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Mogadishu</span> <span class="jr-date">1419</span><p>Mogadishu lay on the most likely route for Arabia and East Africa, which ran by Hormuz, Lasa, Aden, Mogadishu, Brava, Zhubu and Malindi. The fleet returned to China on 8 August 1419.</p></li>
<li data-ll="1.113,44.030" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Brava</span> <span class="jr-date">1419</span><p>The fleet probably called at Brava after Mogadishu on this voyage. Contemporary Chinese porcelain has been excavated at the East African places the fleet visited.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-3.219,40.117" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Malindi</span> <span class="jr-date">1419</span><p>Malindi, on the East African coast, was probably the last place the fleet reached on the fifth voyage before it turned for home.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Sixth voyage, 1421–1422</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="18">
<li data-ll="18.000,54.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhofar">Dhofar</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1421–1422</span><p>After the fleet divided at Semudera, detached squadrons went on to destinations including the three Arabian states of Dhofar, Lasa and Aden.</p></li>
<li data-ll="12.800,45.033" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1421–1422</span><p>The eunuch Zhou, probably Zhou Man, led a detached squadron to Aden. The fleet returned to China on 3 September 1422 with envoys from Siam, Semudera, Aden and other countries.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Seventh voyage, 1431–1433</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="20">
<li data-ll="-7.246,112.738" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Surabaya</span> <span class="jr-date">7 March 1432</span><p>After leaving Nanjing on 19 January 1431, the fleet reached Surabaya in Java and departed on 13 July, according to the Xia Xiyang.</p></li>
<li data-ll="7.000,93.833" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Great Nicobar Island</span> <span class="jr-date">14 November 1432</span><p>Fei Xin writes that the fleet anchored for three days at Cuilanxu, probably Great Nicobar Island, because of unfavourable winds and waves. Local men and women came in log boats to trade coconuts.</p></li>
<li data-ll="11.248,75.780" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Calicut</span> <span class="jr-date">10 December 1432</span><p>The fleet arrived at Calicut on 10 December and left four days later, on 14 December.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.067,56.460" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Hormuz</span> <span class="jr-date">17 January 1433</span><p>The fleet arrived at Hormuz, the farthest west of the eight destinations the Xia Xiyang records for this voyage, and left on 9 March.</p></li>
<li data-ll="21.422,39.826" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1432–1433</span><p>Ma Huan writes that Hong sent seven men from Calicut with a ship bound for Mecca. They took a year to go and return, and bought giraffes, lions and ostriches.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.452,121.104" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Taicang</span> <span class="jr-date">7 July 1433</span><p>On the return the fleet arrived at Taicang. The Xia Xiyang did not record the later stages of the journey to the capital.</p></li>
<li data-ll="39.904,116.408" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a></span> <span class="jr-date">22 July 1433</span><p>The fleet arrived at the capital, Beijing. On 27 July the Xuande Emperor bestowed ceremonial robes and paper money on its personnel.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The seventh voyage returned in 1433 with envoys from 11 countries, including one from Mecca. It is not exactly known why the voyages ended.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Ming treasure voyages&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="nikitin" data-kids="1">
<h2>Afanasy Nikitin's Journey Beyond Three Seas</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1466–1472 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/afanasy-nikitin">Afanasy Nikitin</a> &#183; 14 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Afanasy Nikitin, a merchant from Tver, set out on a trading trip planned for the lands around the Caspian Sea as far as Shirvan. Robbed on the way, he went on through Persia and reached India, where he spent almost three years. The dates are debated: scholars long gave 1466 to 1472, but Leonid Semyonov reconstructed 1468 to 1474.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Down the Volga and the Caspian</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="56.858,35.922" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tver</span> <span class="jr-date">1466 or 1468</span><p>Nikitin, son of Nikita and a merchant of Tver, left his home town on a commercial trip. He planned to trade around the Caspian Sea and go as far as Shirvan.</p></li>
<li data-ll="56.327,44.008" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1466 or 1468</span><p>Nikitin travelled down the Volga, passing Uglich, Kostroma and Plyos, and reached Nizhny Novgorod. There he and his fellow merchants followed the caravan of Hasan Beg, the returning ambassador from Shirvan, further south.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.350,48.035" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1466 or 1468</span><p>Near Astrakhan the party was attacked and robbed by Nogais, whom Nikitin calls Tatars. It was robbed again by Kaitags on the Caspian coast, and some of the Russian merchants were taken prisoner.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.634,48.639" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shemakha</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1466 or 1468</span><p>Nikitin went to the Shirvanshah's camp to plead for help. The ruler freed the captives but refused the means to return home, and the party dispersed, some going back to Rus and some to work at Baku.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.058,48.289" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1466 or 1468</span><p>Hoping to recoup his losses, Nikitin went on to Derbent, which was a familiar market to him.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.367,49.835" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Baku</span> <span class="jr-date">1466 or 1468</span><p>From Baku Nikitin crossed the Caspian Sea into Persia, where he followed a known trade route and made prolonged stops in market towns.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Across Persia and the sea to India</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="27.067,56.460" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Hormuz</span> <span class="jr-date">spring 1471</span><p>After Chapakur, Sari, Amol, Kashan and Yazd, Nikitin reached Hormuz in the Persian Gulf, having spent two years in Persia. Muslim merchants had told him of the riches of India, and he sailed for India from Hormuz.</p></li>
<li data-ll="18.546,72.927" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/chaul">Chaul</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1471</span><p>Nikitin arrived at the port of Chaul six weeks after leaving Hormuz. It was probably after his arrival in India that he began writing his travel notes.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">In India</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="9">
<li data-ll="19.200,73.880" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/junnar">Junnar</a></span><p>Nikitin described the rainy season while living in Junnar, when for four months there was water and mud everywhere, by day and by night.</p></li>
<li data-ll="17.900,77.550" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a></span><p>In the third year of his journey Nikitin wrote that at Bidar, the Bahmani capital, he shed many tears for the Christian faith. He had lost contact with Christianity and lived among Muslims.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The way home</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="11">
<li data-ll="23.614,58.592" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1474</span><p>After almost three years in India, Nikitin decided to return to Russia, concluding that further travel would bring him no great profit. On the way back he visited Muscat.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.005,39.722" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a></span> <span class="jr-date">November 1474</span><p>Nikitin visited Trabzon, also written Trebizond, and in November 1474 sailed from there to Caffa.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.046,35.379" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1474</span><p>Caffa, now Feodosia, had a large Russian settlement. It is most likely that Nikitin composed his narrative here from his travel notes and memories.</p></li>
<li data-ll="54.783,32.045" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a></span> <span class="jr-date">spring 1475</span><p>On his way to Tver Nikitin died not far from Smolensk. Other Russian merchants took his notes to Vasily Mamyrev, secretary to Ivan III, grand prince of Moscow.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Nikitin sailed home by way of Muscat and Trabzon and across the Black Sea to Caffa, where he most likely composed his narrative. He died near Smolensk in the spring of 1475, and other Russian merchants took his notes to Moscow, where they were included in state chronicles.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Afanasy Nikitin&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="dias" data-kids="1">
<h2>Bartolomeu Dias Round the Southern Tip of Africa</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1487–1488 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> &#183; 9 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">A Portuguese mariner, Bartolomeu Dias left Lisbon in or around July 1487 with two caravels and a supply ship, to carry on the exploration of the African coast begun by Diogo Cão. He was also charged with searching for Prester John.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Down the coast to the southern cape</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="38.708,-9.139" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a></span> <span class="jr-date">c. July 1487</span><p>Dias set out with two caravels, the São Cristóvão and São Pantaleão, and a supply ship captained by his brother Diogo. Six Africans kidnapped earlier by Cão were aboard, to be set down along the coast.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-15.800,11.850" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Porto Alexandre</span> <span class="jr-date">1487</span><p>The ships had sailed directly to the Congo and then moved down the coast more carefully. Dias left the supply ship behind here, so that it could re-provision them on their return.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-22.949,14.507" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/walvis-bay">Golfo da Conceição (Walvis Bay)</a></span> <span class="jr-date">8 December 1487</span><p>By December Dias had passed the farthest point reached by Cão. He arrived at the bay he named the Golfo da Conceição, today Walvis Bay in Namibia.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-34.183,22.133" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Mossel Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">4 February 1488</span><p>After progressing along the Namibian coast the two ships turned southwest, away from land, and spent 30 days on the open ocean. They then reached the southern cape of Africa and entered the bay later called Mossel Bay.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.718,26.624" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Kwaaihoek</span> <span class="jr-date">12 March 1488</span><p>Having found that the coast trended northeast, Dias anchored near the mouth of the Boesmans River and set up the Padrão de São Gregório. The crew were restless and urged him to turn back, and the officers unanimously favoured returning.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The return</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="-34.358,18.472" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a></span> <span class="jr-date">May 1488</span><p>Sailing back close to Africa's southwestern coast, the ships met the Cape of Good Hope for the first time. Dias raised the last of their padrões there. Tradition says he named it the Cape of Storms.</p></li>
<li data-ll="1.617,7.400" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Príncipe</span> <span class="jr-date">1488</span><p>After rejoining the supply ship in July, Dias found that six of its nine crew had died in skirmishes with the local people. The worm-eaten ship was burnt on the beach. The fleet then stopped at Príncipe.</p></li>
<li data-ll="5.083,-1.350" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/elmina">São Jorge da Mina</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1488</span><p>The ships called at the Portuguese trading post of São Jorge da Mina, built in the Gulf of Guinea by Diogo de Azambuja's expedition of 1481, in which Dias had taken part.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.708,-9.139" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a></span> <span class="jr-date">December 1488</span><p>Dias returned after an absence of 16 months. His reception at court was muted, with no official proclamations and little recognition.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Dias returned to Lisbon in December 1488 after an absence of 16 months. He had rounded the southern tip of Africa and shown that ships bound south should sail well to the west of the coast.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Bartolomeu Dias&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="columbus" data-kids="0">
<h2>The Voyages of Christopher Columbus</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1492–1504 &#183; <a href="/life/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> &#183; 30 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Between 1492 and 1504 the Genoese navigator Christopher Columbus led four transatlantic expeditions in the name of the Catholic Monarchs of Spain. He sailed in search of a westward route to India, China, Japan and the Spice Islands, and believed at first that he had reached the Indies.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">First voyage, 1492–1493</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="37.228,-6.893" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Palos</span> <span class="jr-date">3 August 1492</span><p>Columbus set out with three ships, the Santa María, the Pinta and the Niña, going down the Rio Tinto into the Atlantic.</p></li>
<li data-ll="28.092,-17.110" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">San Sebastián de La Gomera</span> <span class="jr-date">6 September 1492</span><p>After repairs to the Pinta's rudder in the Canary Islands, the ships met at La Gomera and departed from there for the Atlantic crossing, which lasted five weeks.</p></li>
<li data-ll="24.035,-74.498" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">San Salvador (Guanahani)</span> <span class="jr-date">12 October 1492</span><p>Columbus came ashore on an island he called San Salvador, known to its people as Guanahani. He thought he had reached the Indies, and he met the Lucayan, Taíno and Arawak peoples and took some Arawaks prisoner.</p></li>
<li data-ll="22.000,-79.500" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a></span> <span class="jr-date">28 October 1492</span><p>Columbus landed on the eastern coast of a large island he called Juana and explored its northeast coast. He believed he was near the lands of Asia.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.691,-72.016" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">Fort Navidad</a></span> <span class="jr-date">26 December 1492</span><p>The Santa María ran aground on Hispaniola on 25 December and was abandoned. Columbus began a fort built from its remains and left 39 men there.</p></li>
<li data-ll="36.973,-25.095" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Santa Maria, Azores</span> <span class="jr-date">17 February 1493</span><p>On the way home the Niña anchored off the island, where the cable broke on rocks. The island's captain later held part of the crew prisoner for a time.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.708,-9.139" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a></span> <span class="jr-date">4 March 1493</span><p>A storm forced Columbus into Lisbon. King John II of Portugal met him and said he believed the voyage broke the 1479 Treaty of Alcáçovas.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.228,-6.893" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Palos</span> <span class="jr-date">15 March 1493</span><p>Columbus arrived back at his starting port and later reported to Ferdinand and Isabella in Barcelona, bringing gold samples, plants and some Taíno he had kidnapped.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Second voyage, 1493–1496</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="9">
<li data-ll="36.535,-6.298" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cádiz</span> <span class="jr-date">25 September 1493</span><p>Columbus sailed with a much larger fleet of two carracks and 15 caravels. The stated purpose was to convert the Indigenous Americans to Christianity.</p></li>
<li data-ll="15.417,-61.333" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/dominica">Dominica</a></span> <span class="jr-date">3 November 1493</span><p>The fleet landed on a rugged shore of this island, which Columbus named Dominica. On the same day he landed at Marie-Galante.</p></li>
<li data-ll="18.250,-66.500" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a></span> <span class="jr-date">19 November 1493</span><p>The fleet sighted the island, known to the Taíno as Borinquen, on 17 November and landed at the Bay of Añasco on 19 November. Columbus named it San Juan Bautista and stayed two days.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.691,-72.016" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a></span> <span class="jr-date">27 November 1493</span><p>The fleet signalled the fort and got no answer. Next day the Spaniards found its burnt remains, and Guacanagari's cousin admitted that Caonabo had wiped out the Europeans.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.890,-71.079" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/la-isabela">La Isabela</a></span> <span class="jr-date">2 January 1494</span><p>The fleet reached a plain on the north coast of Hispaniola and founded the settlement of La Isabela. By the end of 1494 disease and famine had claimed two-thirds of the Spanish settlers.</p></li>
<li data-ll="18.180,-77.400" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a></span> <span class="jr-date">5 May 1494</span><p>After leaving Hispaniola, Columbus reached Cuba on 30 April and Discovery Bay on 5 May. He then explored the south coast of Cuba, which he believed to be a peninsula of China.</p></li>
<li data-ll="16.250,-61.500" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Guadeloupe</span> <span class="jr-date">10 April 1496</span><p>Short of food on the way home, Columbus asked the people of Guadeloupe for supplies. They ambushed the Spaniards with arrows, who destroyed some huts and held 13 women and children hostage to force a sale of cassava.</p></li>
<li data-ll="36.494,-6.217" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Bay of Cádiz</span> <span class="jr-date">11 June 1496</span><p>After landing in Portugal near Odemira on 8 June, the fleet returned to Spain through the Bay of Cádiz.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Third voyage, 1498–1500</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="17">
<li data-ll="36.779,-6.354" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Sanlúcar</span> <span class="jr-date">30 May 1498</span><p>Columbus left with six ships. Three went straight to Hispaniola with supplies, while he took the other three to explore what lay to the south.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.461,-61.249" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Trinidad</span> <span class="jr-date">31 July 1498</span><p>The fleet sighted Trinidad from the southeast, sailed along its southern coast and anchored near Soldado Rock, where it met a group of Amerindians in canoes.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.700,-62.500" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Paria Peninsula</span> <span class="jr-date">5 August 1498</span><p>After crossing the Gulf of Paria, the fleet landed on the mainland of South America. Columbus called it another world but still believed it was Asia.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.987,-63.936" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Margarita</span> <span class="jr-date">14 August 1498</span><p>Columbus sailed to the islands of Chacachacare and Margarita. He also sighted Tobago and Grenada.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.000,-71.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a></span> <span class="jr-date">19 August 1498</span><p>Columbus returned in poor health to find many of the settlers in rebellion against his rule. They said he had misled them about the riches they expected.</p></li>
<li data-ll="18.462,-69.936" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a></span> <span class="jr-date">3 February 1500</span><p>Columbus came back to Santo Domingo planning to sail to Spain to answer the rebels' accounts. In 1500 the Crown had him removed as governor and sent to Spain in chains.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.175,-3.600" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a></span> <span class="jr-date">12 December 1500</span><p>The king and queen summoned the Columbus brothers to the Alhambra. With his chains removed, Columbus admitted his faults and begged forgiveness, and their freedom was restored.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Fourth voyage, 1502–1504</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="24">
<li data-ll="36.535,-6.298" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cádiz</span> <span class="jr-date">9 May 1502</span><p>Columbus left with four ships, seeking a westward passage to the Indian Ocean. The king and queen ordered him not to stop at Hispaniola.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.650,-61.015" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Martinique</span> <span class="jr-date">15 June 1502</span><p>After a crossing of twenty days with the trade winds, the ships landed at Carbet on Martinique.</p></li>
<li data-ll="18.462,-69.936" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a></span> <span class="jr-date">29 June 1502</span><p>Columbus expected a hurricane and asked for shelter. He was refused port, and the governor would not heed his warning. A storm then sank many ships, and some of his enemies accused him of conjuring it.</p></li>
<li data-ll="16.472,-85.877" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Guanaja</span> <span class="jr-date">30 July 1502</span><p>Columbus reached the Bay Islands off Honduras. His brother Bartolomeo found native merchants, possibly Mayans, in a large canoe, and the local people introduced Columbus to cacao.</p></li>
<li data-ll="9.300,-82.400" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Almirante Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">16 October 1502</span><p>After two months searching the coasts of Honduras, Nicaragua and Costa Rica for a passage, Columbus arrived in Almirante Bay in Panama.</p></li>
<li data-ll="18.435,-77.202" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">St. Ann's Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">25 June 1503</span><p>Damaged by storms, the ships were beached in Jamaica, where Columbus and his men remained stranded for a year. Help came from Hispaniola on 29 June 1504.</p></li>
<li data-ll="36.779,-6.354" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Sanlúcar de Barrameda</span> <span class="jr-date">7 November 1504</span><p>Columbus and his son Fernando had left Hispaniola in a caravel on 11 September 1504. They arrived at Sanlúcar and travelled on to Seville.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">After the fourth voyage Columbus reached Spain in November 1504 and died in 1506. The Americas were already inhabited by Indigenous peoples, and his voyages led to the Spanish colonization and conquest that followed.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Voyages of Christopher Columbus&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="da-gama" data-kids="0">
<h2>Vasco da Gama's First Voyage to India</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1497–1499 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> &#183; 8 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">King Manuel I of Portugal chose Vasco da Gama to lead an armada of three ships and a supply ship round the Cape of Good Hope to India, carrying out a plan begun under King John II. The Portuguese wanted spices, which were costly to bring overland.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Lisbon round Africa to Calicut</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="38.708,-9.139" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a></span> <span class="jr-date">8 July 1497</span><p>The expedition began on 8 July 1497, on the Lisbon shipping line towards Cabo Verde. The crews numbered up to 170 men, with one ship carrying supplies for three years.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-32.758,18.028" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">St. Helena Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">1497</span><p>Near the bay of St Helena the crews made contact with people who ate sea lions, whales, gazelle meat and herbal roots. They wore fur, carried wooden spears with animal horns, and played flutes in a coordinated manner.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-15.037,40.733" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Mozambique</span><p>The ships crossed into Mozambique, where the crews saw palm trees yielding coconuts. According to Damião de Góis, the São Jorge padrão was set up here to affirm Portuguese sovereignty, so that later explorers could not take the land for themselves.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-3.219,40.117" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Malindi</span><p>From Malindi the Indian Ocean was crossed on a direct passage to Calicut. According to Damião de Góis, the Holy Spirit padrão was set up here, and it is the only one of the five to survive, as the Vasco da Gama Pillar.</p></li>
<li data-ll="11.384,75.719" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Kappakadavu</span> <span class="jr-date">17 May 1498</span><p>The fleet reached Kappakadavu, near Calicut in the present Indian state of Kerala. Scurvy had been felt acutely while crossing the Indian Ocean.</p></li>
<li data-ll="11.248,75.780" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Calicut</span> <span class="jr-date">1498</span><p>Negotiations with the Zamorin were difficult: the gifts were too poor, and Arab merchants resisted competition. Da Gama won an ambiguous letter of trading rights, then left without warning when the Zamorin demanded collateral, leaving a few Portuguese to start a trading post.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The return</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="38.733,-27.317" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Terceira Island</span> <span class="jr-date">1499</span><p>Da Gama fell behind on Terceira Island, preferring to stay with his brother, who was seriously ill, while the caravel Berrio went on to Lisbon.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.708,-9.139" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a></span> <span class="jr-date">12 July 1499</span><p>Nicolau Coelho brought the Berrio into the Tagus on 12 July 1499 with the news that the Portuguese had reached India by sea. Da Gama returned home on 31 August.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Only 55 of the 148 men of the armada survived the voyage, and the São Rafael was burnt. Da Gama reached Lisbon on 31 August 1499 and King Manuel I gave him the title of Don and great rewards.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Portuguese discovery of the sea route to India&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="cabral" data-kids="0">
<h2>Cabral's Fleet to Brazil and India</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1500–1501 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a> &#183; 19 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">King Manuel I of Portugal sent a fleet of 13 ships and 1500 men under Pedro Álvares Cabral to India to secure a treaty with Calicut, find Sofala and bring back spices. On the way the fleet reached the coast of Brazil, where the Tupiniquim lived.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Lisbon to the coast of Brazil</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="38.708,-9.139" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a></span> <span class="jr-date">9 March 1500</span><p>The fleet set out from the Tagus, the river of Lisbon. It was a large, well-armed armada of 13 ships and 1500 men, carrying gifts and letters for the rulers of the east.</p></li>
<li data-ll="16.615,-24.271" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">São Nicolau</span> <span class="jr-date">22 March 1500</span><p>The fleet reached the island in the middle of a storm. The ship of Luís Pires was too damaged by the tempest to go on and returned to Lisbon.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-16.917,-39.267" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Monte Pascoal</span> <span class="jr-date">22 April 1500</span><p>After 44 days from departure the fleet sighted the coast of Brazil and named a hill Monte Pascoal. Tupiniquim people gathered on the beach, but the surf was too strong for a landing.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-16.278,-39.025" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cabrália Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">April 1500</span><p>The fleet sheltered behind the reef at Cabrália Bay, near Porto Seguro. Friar Henrique Soares held a mass before 200 Tupiniquim on 26 April. On 1 May Cabral claimed the land for Portugal as the Ilha de Vera Cruz and left a couple of convicts with the Tupiniquim.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Round the Cape and up the East African coast</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="-34.358,18.472" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a></span> <span class="jr-date">late May 1500</span><p>The fleet met headlong winds for six days at the Cape. Four ships, among them that of Bartolomeu Dias, were lost at sea, which left seven.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.090,39.121" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Primeiras Islands</span> <span class="jr-date">16 June 1500</span><p>Cabral's three-ship squadron chased two merchant ships. One ran aground and the other was captured, then released when it proved to belong to a cousin of the sultan of Malindi.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-15.037,40.733" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mozambique-island">Mozambique Island</a></span> <span class="jr-date">22 June 1500</span><p>The Sultan of Mozambique gave Cabral a warm reception and let him collect water and supplies. Three more ships joined him here, and the fleet went on with six ships.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-8.982,39.517" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani">Kilwa</a></span> <span class="jr-date">26 July 1500</span><p>Cabral met Emir Ibrahim in rowboats in the harbour and offered a treaty from King Manuel I. Ibrahim was suspicious, and Cabral, worried about missing the monsoon, broke off the talks and sailed on.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-3.219,40.117" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Malindi</span> <span class="jr-date">2 August 1500</span><p>After avoiding hostile Mombasa, the fleet was well received by the Sultan of Malindi. Cabral landed the Malindi ambassador, took on two Gujarati pilots and crossed the Indian Ocean from 7 August.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">India: Calicut, war and Cochin</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="10">
<li data-ll="14.757,74.112" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Anjediva Island</span> <span class="jr-date">22 August 1500</span><p>After an uneventful crossing the six ships rested, and were repaired and repainted.</p></li>
<li data-ll="11.248,75.780" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Calicut</span> <span class="jr-date">13 September 1500</span><p>Cabral went ashore only after hostages were exchanged, and met the Zamorin. A trade treaty was agreed and the Portuguese were allowed a factory, where Aires Correia landed with around 70 men.</p></li>
<li data-ll="11.248,75.780" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Calicut</span> <span class="jr-date">17 December 1500</span><p>Cabral seized an Arab merchant ship, and a mob attacked the factory. 53 Portuguese were killed (some sources say 70). Cabral then seized about ten Arab ships, killed their crews, burned them and bombarded the city for a day, with casualties of up to 600.</p></li>
<li data-ll="9.967,76.283" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a></span> <span class="jr-date">24 December 1500</span><p>On the advice of Gaspar da Gama, Cabral sailed to Cochin, a rival of Calicut. He made an alliance with its raja against Calicut, and a Portuguese factory was set up under Gonçalo Gil Barbosa.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.220,76.220" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kodungallur">Cranganore</a></span> <span class="jr-date">January 1501</span><p>Cabral loaded cargo at the declining city and met its Malabari Jewish and Syrian Christian communities. Two Syrian Christian priests asked for passage to Europe.</p></li>
<li data-ll="11.869,75.356" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Cannanore</a></span> <span class="jr-date">January 1501</span><p>After leaving Cochin when news came of a Calicut fleet of around 80 boats, Cabral swung wide of Calicut and was received by the Kolathiri Raja, who sold spices on credit. Cabral paid him all the same.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The way home</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="16">
<li data-ll="-3.219,40.117" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Malindi</span> <span class="jr-date">February 1501</span><p>Vice-admiral Sancho de Tovar ran the spice-laden El Rei aground on the Malindi coast. The ship could not be repaired, so its crew and cargo were reallocated and it was burnt for its iron fittings.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-15.037,40.733" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mozambique-island">Mozambique Island</a></span> <span class="jr-date">spring 1501</span><p>With five ships left and no news of Diogo Dias, Cabral sent the fast Anunciada ahead under Nicolau Coelho and sent Tovar in the São Pedro to find Sofala.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.673,-17.432" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Bezeguiche</span> <span class="jr-date">June 1501</span><p>At the bay of Dakar, called Bezeguiche, Cabral found Diogo Dias and Nicolau Coelho waiting. Pêro de Ataíde and Tovar arrived by the end of June.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.708,-9.139" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a></span> <span class="jr-date">21 July 1501</span><p>Cabral and Miranda arrived in Lisbon a month after Coelho. Tovar and Ataíde followed on 25 July and Dias, with his empty caravel, on 27 July.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Cabral reached Lisbon on 21 July 1501 with the treaty with Calicut unmade and the war with it begun. Of the 13 ships, five were lost, and Portugal gained a factory in Cochin and a claim to Brazil.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;2nd Portuguese India Armada (Cabral, 1500)&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="magellan" data-kids="0">
<h2>Magellan and Elcano's Voyage Round the World</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1519–1522 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a> &#183; 18 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">A fleet of five ships, planned and first led by the Portuguese explorer Ferdinand Magellan for the Spanish crown, left Spain in 1519 with about 270 men. Its purpose was to secure a sea route to the Moluccas, or Spice Islands, in present-day Indonesia.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Across the Atlantic and down South America</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="36.779,-6.354" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Sanlúcar de Barrameda</span> <span class="jr-date">20 September 1519</span><p>The five ships left Seville and went down the Guadalquivir to Sanlúcar de Barrameda at its mouth, where they stayed more than five weeks before setting sail and leaving Spain.</p></li>
<li data-ll="28.269,-16.606" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tenerife</span> <span class="jr-date">26 September 1519</span><p>The fleet stopped in the Canary Islands to take in supplies. Here Magellan received a secret warning that some of the Castilian captains were planning a mutiny.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-22.911,-43.206" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a></span> <span class="jr-date">13 December 1519</span><p>The fleet reached the harbour, which was nominally Portuguese territory but had no permanent settlement then. Over 13 days the crew repaired the ships, took on food and water, and traded with the local people.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-34.883,-56.714" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a></span> <span class="jr-date">11 January 1520</span><p>Magellan believed he had found the strait he sought, but it was the mouth of a river. Juan Serrano, sent to probe it, reported that it was a river, and the fleet found fresh water.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-49.307,-67.730" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Port St. Julian</span> <span class="jr-date">31 March 1520</span><p>Weather had grown so bad that Magellan chose to wait out the winter in a natural harbour. A mutiny led by Juan de Cartagena and others began within a day, and the fleet stayed about five months.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-50.133,-68.350" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Santa Cruz</span> <span class="jr-date">24 August 1520</span><p>After the Santiago found shelter at the Santa Cruz estuary, Magellan moved the fleet there from St. Julian around this date. They spent six weeks there before resuming the search for a strait.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The strait, the Pacific and the Philippines</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="-52.333,-68.354" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cape Virgenes</span> <span class="jr-date">21 October 1520</span><p>The fleet sighted a headland at 52°S and named it Cape Virgenes. A storm drove two ships into a narrow passage beyond it, which proved to be the strait they sought.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-54.000,-71.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Strait of Magellan</span> <span class="jr-date">28 November 1520</span><p>The three remaining ships completed the passage to the Pacific, which Magellan named the Mar Pacifico. The San Antonio had turned back and sailed to Spain.</p></li>
<li data-ll="13.500,144.800" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Guam</span> <span class="jr-date">6 March 1521</span><p>After the Pacific crossing the fleet reached the Mariana Islands and anchored at Guam. Chamorro people took goods from the ships, and Magellan's men later burned Chamorro houses and killed seven men.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.733,125.717" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Homonhon</span> <span class="jr-date">16 March 1521</span><p>The fleet landed on an uninhabited island and traded with people from nearby Suluan. They rested and gathered food and water for nearly two weeks.</p></li>
<li data-ll="9.908,125.075" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Limasawa</span> <span class="jr-date">31 March 1521</span><p>Magellan met Rajah Kolambu and Rajah Siawi here. On Easter Day he and fifty men attended the first Catholic Mass in the Philippines, and he claimed the archipelago for Spain.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.317,123.750" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">7 April 1521</span><p>Magellan persuaded Rajah Humabon to waive the tribute usually asked of visitors. Humabon and his family were baptised on 14 April, and in total 2,200 people were converted.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.300,123.967" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Mactan</span> <span class="jr-date">April 1521</span><p>Lapu-Lapu's people on Mactan resisted conversion, and Magellan decided to make them submit by force. He landed with 49 men against a far larger force and was killed in the battle.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.317,123.750" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1 May 1521</span><p>Humabon invited about thirty men, mostly officers, to a feast. Armed Cebuanos entered the hall and killed twenty-seven of them, and the ships left port.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The Moluccas and home</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="15">
<li data-ll="0.683,127.400" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tidore</span> <span class="jr-date">8 November 1521</span><p>The Trinidad and Victoria reached the Moluccas and were greeted by al-Mansur. A trading post was set up and the men bought large quantities of cloves.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-34.358,18.472" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a></span> <span class="jr-date">6 May 1522</span><p>The Victoria, under Elcano, left Tidore on 21 December 1521 and crossed the Indian Ocean. It rounded the Cape with only rice for rations.</p></li>
<li data-ll="15.300,-23.700" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cape Verde</span> <span class="jr-date">9 July 1522</span><p>Twenty crewmen had died of starvation when Elcano put in for provisions. The Portuguese detained 13 crew members on finding spices aboard, though the Victoria escaped.</p></li>
<li data-ll="36.779,-6.354" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Sanlúcar de Barrameda</span> <span class="jr-date">6 September 1522</span><p>Elcano and the remaining crew arrived aboard the Victoria almost exactly three years after they departed. They then sailed upriver to Seville.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Magellan died in battle in the Philippines in April 1521. Juan Sebastián Elcano brought the Victoria back to Spain on 6 September 1522 with 18 men, the first circumnavigation of the Earth.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Magellan expedition&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="cortes" data-kids="0">
<h2>Cortés's March on Tenochtitlan</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1519–1521 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> &#183; 16 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Hernán Cortés left Cuba in 1519 against the governor's wishes with 11 ships and about 630 men, and with Indigenous allies, mainly Tlaxcalans, marched inland to Tenochtitlan, capital of the Aztec Triple Alliance ruled by Moctezuma II. The campaign ended in the siege and fall of the city in 1521.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Cuba to the Gulf coast</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="21.800,-79.967" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Trinidad, Cuba</span><p>Governor Velázquez came to the dock at Santiago de Cuba and sent orders to hold the fleet and arrest Cortés. Cortés sailed for Trinidad regardless, beginning the expedition with the legal status of a mutineer.</p></li>
<li data-ll="20.417,-86.917" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cozumel</span><p>Cortés spent some time on the island trying to convert the locals to Christianity, with mixed results. He heard of Spaniards living in Yucatán, and the shipwreck survivor Gerónimo de Aguilar joined him as a translator.</p></li>
<li data-ll="18.530,-92.652" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Potonchán</span><p>Cortés defeated the local people in two battles. Their leaders then sued for peace and gave the Spaniards 20 enslaved women, among them the Nahua woman later known as Doña Marina or La Malinche, who became a main translator.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.209,-96.131" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">San Juan de Ulúa</span> <span class="jr-date">21 April 1519</span><p>The expedition landed on the Gulf coast near San Juan de Ulúa. Cortés was welcomed by representatives of Moctezuma II and tried to frighten them with a display of firepower.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.447,-96.404" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Cempoala</span> <span class="jr-date">June 1519</span><p>Cortés persuaded the Totonac chiefs to rebel against the Aztecs, and five of Moctezuma's tax collectors were taken prisoner. The Totonacs helped build Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz, the starting point of the campaign.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Inland to Tenochtitlan</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="19.315,-98.236" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tlaxcala</span> <span class="jr-date">23 September 1519</span><p>The Otomi and Tlaxcalans first fought the Spanish in three battles from 2 to 5 September, then allied with them against the Aztecs. Cortés stayed twenty days, and the Tlaxcalans became his most important allies.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.062,-98.308" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Cholula</span><p>The Spaniards, helped by Tlaxcalans, massacred the people of Cholula and burned the city. Cortés claimed 3,000 dead and Vázquez de Tapia as many as 30,000, though many had fled. Accounts differ on the cause.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.435,-99.131" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a></span> <span class="jr-date">8 November 1519</span><p>Cortés and his forces entered the island capital, where Moctezuma met them on the causeway. By 14 November Moctezuma was Cortés's prisoner, though modern scholars doubt Cortés's own claim about when he took him.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Retreat, return and the siege</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="9">
<li data-ll="19.447,-96.404" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Cempoala</span> <span class="jr-date">May 1520</span><p>Governor Velázquez had sent Pánfilo de Narváez to seize Cortés. Cortés attacked at night, wounded and captured him, and won over most of his men with promises of riches and commands.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.435,-99.131" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a></span> <span class="jr-date">June 1520</span><p>Cortés found that Alvarado had attacked and killed many Aztec nobles at a religious festival in the Great Temple. Alvarado said he feared an attack. The city rose, and Moctezuma died, the Aztecs saying the Spaniards killed him.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.456,-99.186" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tlacopan</span> <span class="jr-date">10 July 1520</span><p>The Spaniards and their allies fled along the causeway at night and were attacked from the causeway and from canoes. Losses were put at 860 Spanish soldiers, 72 other Spaniards and 1,000 Tlaxcalan warriors.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.660,-98.730" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/otumba">Otumba</a></span> <span class="jr-date">14 July 1520</span><p>The Aztecs tried to destroy the retreating Spanish at the Battle of Otumba. The infantry held while the cavalry charged repeatedly, and the Aztecs left the field after Cortés's men killed one of their leaders.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.315,-98.236" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tlaxcala</span> <span class="jr-date">July 1520</span><p>The 440 surviving Spaniards, all wounded and with only 20 horses left, found refuge among the Tlaxcalans. Smallpox struck the Aztecs from September, and killed the new emperor Cuitláhuac on 4 December, perhaps of the disease.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.512,-98.883" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Texcoco</span> <span class="jr-date">February 1521</span><p>Spanish, Tlaxcalan and Texcocan forces made Texcoco their base against Tenochtitlan. In April Tlaxcalan labourers built 13 shallow-bottomed brigantines under Spanish supervision, giving the allies control of Lake Texcoco.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.435,-99.131" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a></span> <span class="jr-date">10 May 1521</span><p>The siege began and cut off the drinking water from Chapultepec. The causeways were cut and the lake was held by armed brigantines. The defenders under Cuauhtémoc refused every offer to end it.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.435,-99.131" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a></span> <span class="jr-date">13 August 1521</span><p>Tenochtitlan and Tlatelolco fell and Cuauhtémoc was captured trying to flee by canoe. Sacking and violence against the survivors followed from 13 to 17 August. The city was almost totally destroyed.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Tenochtitlan and Tlatelolco fell on 13 August 1521 and Cuauhtémoc was captured. The city was largely destroyed and Mexico City was built on its ruins as the capital of New Spain; the surviving Mexica were forbidden to live in Tenochtitlan.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="cabeza-de-vaca" data-kids="0">
<h2>Cabeza de Vaca's Journey from Florida to Mexico City</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1527–1536 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/alvar-nunez-cabeza-de-vaca">Álvar Núñez Cabeza de Vaca</a> &#183; 8 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Álvar Núñez Cabeza de Vaca sailed in 1527 as royal treasurer of Pánfilo de Narváez's expedition to La Florida. After the expedition broke up he was one of four survivors, with Andrés Dorantes de Carranza, Alonso del Castillo Maldonado and Estevanico, an African enslaved by Dorantes. They crossed the Gulf coast and northern Mexico on foot over about eight years.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The Narváez expedition</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="19.000,-71.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1527</span><p>The fleet of five ships, which had sailed from Spain on 17 June 1527 with 600 soldiers and colonists, stopped here for supplies. Narváez lost more than 140 men who chose to stay, and the expedition spent forty-five days on the island.</p></li>
<li data-ll="20.022,-75.829" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Santiago de Cuba</span> <span class="jr-date">1527</span><p>The expedition went on to Cuba hoping to recruit men and buy horses. Narváez anchored here and sent Cabeza de Vaca with two ships further up the coast for provisions.</p></li>
<li data-ll="21.800,-79.967" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Trinidad</span> <span class="jr-date">October 1527</span><p>While Cabeza de Vaca was ashore negotiating for supplies, a hurricane hit the coast. Both of his ships were destroyed, with the loss of sixty men and twenty horses.</p></li>
<li data-ll="22.134,-80.481" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Bay of Jagua</span> <span class="jr-date">November 1527</span><p>Narváez decided to overwinter in Cuba for fear of another storm. The four remaining ships anchored in the Bay of Jagua under Cabeza de Vaca's command until February 1528.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.762,-82.546" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tampa Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">April 1528</span><p>After a storm blew the ships into the Gulf of Mexico, the expedition sighted land and went ashore, probably near Tampa Bay. Narváez then decided to march inland with some 300 men and 42 horses.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Wreck on the Texas coast and the walk to Mexico City</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="29.222,-94.909" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Galveston Island</span> <span class="jr-date">November 1528</span><p>After the rafts built in Florida were scattered, two crafts with about 40 survivors each wrecked on or near Galveston Island. Only 15 of about 80 lived past the winter, and they named the island Malhado.</p></li>
<li data-ll="24.790,-107.388" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Culiacán</span><p>After four years enslaved by nomadic peoples of the upper Gulf coast, four men escaped and walked south and west across Texas and northern Mexico. Near Culiacán Cabeza de Vaca first met other Spaniards.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.354,-99.136" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a></span><p>Cabeza de Vaca and the three other men reached Mexico City, nearly eight years after the wreck on Galveston Island. From there he sailed back to Europe in 1537.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The four men reached Mexico City, and Cabeza de Vaca sailed back to Europe in 1537. He wrote his account in Spain, and it was first published in 1542.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Álvar Núñez Cabeza de Vaca&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="cartier" data-kids="0">
<h2>Jacques Cartier's Voyages to the St. Lawrence</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1534–1542 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a> &#183; 12 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Jacques Cartier, a mariner from Saint-Malo, sailed three times to the Gulf of St. Lawrence and the St. Lawrence River under commissions from Francis I of France. The first voyage hoped for a western passage to the East Indies; the later ones sought the 'Kingdom of Saguenay' and a settlement.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">First voyage, 1534</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="48.649,-2.026" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a></span> <span class="jr-date">20 April 1534</span><p>Cartier sailed under a commission from the king, hoping to find a western passage to the wealthy markets of the East Indies. The commission told him to find islands and lands said to hold gold and other precious things.</p></li>
<li data-ll="47.791,-61.480" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Îles aux Oiseaux</span> <span class="jr-date">1534</span><p>After twenty days across the ocean Cartier explored Newfoundland, the Strait of Belle Isle and the Gulf of St. Lawrence. At the Islands of the Birds his crew killed around 1000 birds, most of them great auks, now extinct.</p></li>
<li data-ll="48.831,-64.484" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Gaspé Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">24 July 1534</span><p>Cartier met a party of St. Lawrence Iroquoians and planted a 10-metre cross reading 'Long Live the King of France', claiming the land for the king. He kidnapped the two sons of their chief, Donnacona, who agreed under pressure on condition that they return with European goods to trade.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Second voyage, 1535 to 1536</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="46.824,-71.243" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/stadacona">Stadacona</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1535</span><p>Cartier set sail on 19 May 1535 with three ships, 110 men and the two Iroquoian captives, and sailed upriver for the first time. He reached Stadacona, the Iroquoian capital, where Chief Donnacona ruled.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.504,-73.575" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Hochelaga</span> <span class="jr-date">2 October 1535</span><p>Using his smallest ship, Cartier arrived at Hochelaga, where a crowd of over a thousand came to the river's edge to greet the French. Rapids blocked the river and he went no further, though he believed it the Northwest Passage.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.824,-71.243" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/stadacona">Stadacona</a></span> <span class="jr-date">11 October 1535</span><p>After two days at Hochelaga, Cartier returned to Stadacona and the French wintered there. The fleet froze in from mid-November to mid-April, and scurvy killed, by Cartier's estimate, 50 Iroquoians; Domagaya told him of a cure, and 85 Frenchmen survived.</p></li>
<li data-ll="48.649,-2.026" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a></span> <span class="jr-date">16 July 1536</span><p>In early May 1536 Cartier kidnapped Chief Donnacona to take him to France, to tell of the 'Kingdom of Saguenay', said to be full of gold, rubies and other treasures. The ships reached Saint-Malo on 16 July.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Third voyage, 1541 to 1542</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="8">
<li data-ll="48.649,-2.026" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a></span> <span class="jr-date">23 May 1541</span><p>Cartier left with five ships, sailing ahead of Roberval, who was named lieutenant general and was waiting for artillery and supplies. The aims were now the 'Kingdom of Saguenay' and a permanent settlement on the St. Lawrence.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.824,-71.243" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/stadacona">Stadacona</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1541</span><p>Cartier anchored at Stadacona and met the Iroquoians again. He found their 'show of joy' and their numbers worrisome and decided not to build his settlement there.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.748,-71.342" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Charlesbourg-Royal</span> <span class="jr-date">1541</span><p>A few kilometres upriver, at the site of present-day Cap-Rouge, Cartier landed the convicts and other colonists, turned the surviving cattle loose and planted a kitchen garden. The fortified settlement was named Charlesbourg-Royal, with a second fort on the cliff above.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.504,-73.575" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Hochelaga</span> <span class="jr-date">September 1541</span><p>On 7 September Cartier left with the longboats to look for Saguenay. He reached Hochelaga, but bad weather and the many rapids stopped him from continuing to the Ottawa River.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.748,-71.342" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Charlesbourg-Royal</span> <span class="jr-date">1541 to 1542</span><p>Back at the fort, by Cartier's account, the Iroquoians were prowling about in a sinister manner. After the winter, from which few records survive, returning sailors said they attacked and killed about 35 settlers. Cartier left for France in early June 1542.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Cartier left Charlesbourg-Royal in early June 1542 and reached France in October, on his last voyage. The settlement was abandoned in 1543, and no permanent European settlement was made in Canada before 1605.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Jacques Cartier&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="de-soto" data-kids="0">
<h2>Hernando de Soto's Expedition through the Southeast</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1539–1543 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a> &#183; 9 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Hernando de Soto, a conquistador who had taken part in the conquest of the Inca Empire, led over 620 men and some 220 horses into La Florida, searching for gold and for a passage to China or the Pacific. The expedition ranged through what is now the south-eastern United States.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Landing and war in Florida</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="27.762,-82.546" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tampa Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">May 1539</span><p>De Soto landed nine ships with over 620 men and 220 horses in an area generally identified as south Tampa Bay, though Robert S. Weddle suggested Charlotte Harbor or San Carlos Bay. He named the land Espíritu Santo.</p></li>
<li data-ll="29.717,-82.050" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Melrose</span> <span class="jr-date">1539</span><p>The army seized stored food, took women as slaves for the soldiers' sexual gratification and forced men and boys to serve as bearers. De Soto struck first at Chief Vitachuco's people and thousands were killed; 900 survivors took refuge in Two-mile Pond.</p></li>
<li data-ll="30.434,-84.273" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/anhaica">Anhaica</a></span> <span class="jr-date">winter 1539</span><p>The expedition spent its first winter at Anhaica, the capital of the Apalachee people, just east of today's Tallahassee. It is one of the few places on the route where archaeologists have found physical traces of the expedition.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Through the Southeast to Mabila</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="31.930,-82.940" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Telfair County, Georgia</span> <span class="jr-date">1540</span><p>Hearing of gold mined toward the sun's rising, the expedition turned north-east through Georgia. Researchers believe, from glass trade beads and metal objects found near the Ocmulgee River in 2009, that it stopped in Telfair County.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.258,-88.703" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tupelo-mississippi">Tupelo</a></span><p>Fearing that word of the losses would reach Spain if his men reached the ships at Mobile Bay, de Soto led them away from the Gulf coast into inland Mississippi, most likely near Tupelo, where they spent the winter. The route beyond Mabila is contested.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">West of the Mississippi and de Soto's death</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="34.400,-93.619" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Caddo Gap</span> <span class="jr-date">October 1541</span><p>Having crossed Arkansas, the expedition went as far inland as the Caddo River, where it clashed with the Tula, whom the Spaniards called the most skilled and dangerous warriors they had met. This may have happened near Caddo Gap.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.332,-91.284" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Guachoya</span> <span class="jr-date">21 May 1542</span><p>De Soto died of a fever in the village of Guachoya; sources disagree whether it lay near Lake Village, Arkansas, or Ferriday, Louisiana. His men hid his death, and according to one source sank his body in the Mississippi.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Down the Mississippi to Mexico</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="8">
<li data-ll="22.050,-98.183" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Pánuco</span><p>After building seven pinnaces on the Mississippi, the survivors set off in July and drifted downriver under attack by war canoes led by the paramount chief Quigualtam; about 11 Spaniards were killed. They kept to the Gulf shore and after about 50 days reached the frontier town of Pánuco.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.354,-99.136" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a></span><p>After a month's rest at Pánuco the survivors reached Mexico City. Viceroy Antonio de Mendoza offered to lead another expedition to La Florida, but few survivors volunteered. Most stayed in the New World.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">At the fortified town of Mabila in 1540 the Spaniards, who had taken the paramount chief Tuskaloosa hostage, burned the town in nine hours of fighting: about 200 Spaniards died, according to Elvas, and an estimated 2,000 to 6,000 Native Americans. De Soto died of a fever on 21 May 1542, and Luis de Moscoso Alvarado took command. Of the recorded 700 participants at the start, between 300 and 350 survived; the expedition had found no gold and founded no colonies.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Hernando de Soto&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="legazpi" data-kids="0">
<h2>Legazpi's Expedition from Mexico to the Philippines</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1564–1572 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> &#183; 11 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">In 1564 the viceroy of New Spain commissioned Miguel López de Legazpi, by order of Philip II, to lead an expedition across the Pacific to find the Spice Islands. Five ships carried 500 soldiers, over half of them Mexicans, and six Augustinian missionaries, with Andrés de Urdaneta as navigator.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Across the Pacific and through the Visayas</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="19.208,-104.681" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Barra de Navidad</span> <span class="jr-date">19 or 20 November 1564</span><p>Five ships with 500 soldiers sailed from this port in New Spain. Other sources give 1 November 1564 and mention four ships and 379 men.</p></li>
<li data-ll="16.700,145.780" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Mariana Islands</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1565</span><p>After sailing the Pacific for 93 days the expedition anchored briefly in the Mariana Islands and took on supplies. There they fought with Chamorro people and burned several huts.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.317,123.750" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">13 February 1565</span><p>Sikatuna, a chief of Bohol, had given Legazpi information about Cebu and joined him as a guide. The ships anchored off the Rajahnate of Cebu but did not land, because people there opposed them.</p></li>
<li data-ll="12.050,125.117" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Samar</span> <span class="jr-date">22 February 1565</span><p>The expedition reached the island of Samar and made a blood compact with its chief, Datu Urrao.</p></li>
<li data-ll="9.908,125.075" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Limasawa</span> <span class="jr-date">1565</span><p>From Samar the Spaniards went on to the island of Limasawa, where Datu Bankaw received them.</p></li>
<li data-ll="9.883,124.217" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/bohol">Bohol</a></span> <span class="jr-date">16 March 1565</span><p>On Bohol the Spaniards befriended Datu Sikatuna and Rajah Sigala. Legazpi made a blood compact with Sikatuna on 16 March.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.317,123.750" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">27 April 1565</span><p>The expedition returned to Cebu and landed. Rajah Tupas challenged the Spaniards but was overpowered, and they founded a colony, the Villa del Santisimo Nombre de Jesús, named after an image of Sto. Niño. In 1568 a Portuguese blockade failed.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Panay, Luzon and Manila</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="8">
<li data-ll="11.150,122.483" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Panay</span> <span class="jr-date">1569</span><p>Food was scarce in Cebu, so Legazpi moved to the town of Panay on the island of Panay. The Spaniards then founded a second settlement, Capiz, on the bank of the Panay River.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.517,120.767" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Manila Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">8 May 1570</span><p>Legazpi sent Martín de Goiti north with 120 Spaniards. Goiti landed in Batangas and on 8 May reached Manila Bay, where he formed an alliance with Rajah Matanda, a vassal of the Sultan of Brunei.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.596,120.977" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a></span> <span class="jr-date">24 June 1571</span><p>Bambalito's fleet of two thousand five hundred fought the Spaniards under Goiti at Bangkusay Channel and lost, and the Spaniards occupied Tondo and Maynila. Legazpi made a pact with Rajah Sulayman and Lakan Dula, founded a settlement and proclaimed Manila the capital.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.596,120.977" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a></span> <span class="jr-date">20 August 1572</span><p>Legazpi governed the Philippines for a year before he died of a stroke in Manila, bankrupt. He was buried in San Agustin Church, Intramuros.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Legazpi proclaimed Manila the capital in 1571 and became the first Spanish governor-general of the Philippines. He died in Manila on 20 August 1572, and the expedition created the trans-Pacific Manila galleon trade.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Miguel López de Legazpi&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="drake" data-kids="0">
<h2>Francis Drake's Voyage Round the World</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1577–1580 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> &#183; 16 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Francis Drake sailed from Plymouth in December 1577 with five ships, secretly commissioned by Queen Elizabeth I to raid the Spanish colonies on the Pacific coast of the Americas. He told his crew they were bound on a trading voyage to the Levant.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Across the Atlantic and round South America</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="50.371,-4.142" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Plymouth</span> <span class="jr-date">13 December 1577</span><p>Drake's fleet had first set out in November but a storm in the Channel damaged two ships and forced it back for repairs lasting nearly a month. It finally left Plymouth on 13 December and sailed south into the Atlantic.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.513,-9.769" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/essaouira">Mogador</a></span> <span class="jr-date">late December 1577</span><p>Poor winds brought the fleet to Mogador, off Morocco, about two weeks after leaving Plymouth. While a pinnace was being assembled there, Moorish raiders took one of Drake's men.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.918,-23.509" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/praia">Praia</a></span> <span class="jr-date">January 1578</span><p>At Santiago, in the Cape Verde Islands, Drake captured the Spanish merchant ship Santa Maria under fire from a fort's guns just off Praia. He took on its Portuguese navigator, Nuno da Silva, and gave Doughty command of the Pelican.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-49.307,-67.730" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">San Julian</span><p>The fleet wintered in this Patagonian harbour, where Magellan had put mutineers to death half a century earlier. Drake tried Thomas Doughty for treachery and incitement to mutiny, and on 2 July had him beheaded.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-54.000,-71.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Strait of Magellan</span> <span class="jr-date">August 1578</span><p>On 20 August 1578 Drake renamed the Pelican the Golden Hind, after his patron Christopher Hatton's crest. He passed through the strait in 16 days. Storms in the Pacific then lost the Marigold, and the Elizabeth turned back for England.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Raids on the Pacific coast and the claim to Nova Albion</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="-38.365,-73.915" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Mocha Island</span><p>Drake's first Pacific stop for food and water. The Mapuche brought sheep, chickens and wheat on the first day. On the second, a landing party was attacked: two men were killed and two captured, and Drake and Diego were wounded by arrows.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.046,-71.620" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a></span> <span class="jr-date">5 December 1578</span><p>Drake assaulted the small Spanish settlement and plundered what he could find, including the church silver. A ship in the harbour carried Chilean wine, 25,000 pesos in gold and charts of the South American coast.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-12.052,-77.139" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a></span> <span class="jr-date">13 February 1579</span><p>Drake took the harbour of Lima with ease and had a dozen vessels there ransacked and run aground. He learned that the treasure galleon Nuestra Señora de la Concepción had sailed north-west, and set off after her the next day.</p></li>
<li data-ll="8.706,-83.879" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Caño Island</span> <span class="jr-date">March 1579</span><p>After taking the Concepción and her treasure, Drake captured a frigate here and had the Golden Hind repaired and careened at a nearby bay. Part of the silver was moved to the frigate, and the crew felt an earthquake.</p></li>
<li data-ll="15.759,-96.123" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Guatulco</span> <span class="jr-date">late March 1579</span><p>The English went ashore against little resistance and sacked the town, taking a gold chain, 7,000 pesos in silver, provisions, silks and maps of the Manila galleon route. Drake released the Spanish captives and Nuno da Silva.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.012,-122.919" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Nova Albion (Drakes Bay)</span><p>Drake met the Coast Miwok, who honoured him with a crown of feathers, and claimed the land for England as Nova Albion. The exact landing place is disputed, and writers have placed it from Alaska to northern Mexico. Drakes Bay is the officially recognised site.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.739,-123.067" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Farallon Islands</span> <span class="jr-date">24 July 1579</span><p>Drake left New Albion on 23 July 1579 and paused the next day at these islands, where the crew hunted seals for meat before the Pacific crossing.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Across the Pacific and home</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="13">
<li data-ll="0.783,127.367" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Ternate</span> <span class="jr-date">November 1579</span><p>Sultan Babullah welcomed the crew in an old Portuguese castle. Drake traded English linen, gold and silver for about ten tonnes of spices, mostly cloves, and declined to join a campaign against the Portuguese fort at Tidore.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-7.733,109.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tjilatjap, Java</span> <span class="jr-date">11 March 1580</span><p>The crew were well received on Java's south coast. Drake exchanged courtesies and silks with the rajahs, and in two weeks the ship took on rice, chickens, yams and dried beef for part of the journey home.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-34.358,18.472" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a></span> <span class="jr-date">15 June 1580</span><p>Drake sailed from Java on 24 March into the Indian Ocean and rounded the Cape, where the crew, expecting severe storms from Portuguese and Spanish accounts, found good weather and a decent wind.</p></li>
<li data-ll="50.371,-4.142" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Plymouth</span> <span class="jr-date">26 September 1580</span><p>Drake entered the English Channel on 26 September and asked fishermen who was on the throne and whether England was at peace. He sailed into Plymouth with 59 remaining crew and his cargo of spices and Spanish treasure.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Drake reached Plymouth on 26 September 1580 in the Golden Hind with 59 crew, a cargo of spices and Spanish treasure, the second leader of a circumnavigation with his ship intact and the first Englishman to complete one. Elizabeth knighted him aboard the ship in 1581.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Francis Drake's circumnavigation&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="hasekura" data-kids="1">
<h2>Hasekura Tsunenaga's Embassy to New Spain, Spain and Rome</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1613–1620 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/hasekura-tsunenaga">Hasekura Tsunenaga</a> &#183; 15 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Hasekura Tsunenaga, a samurai and retainer of Date Masamune, the daimyō of Sendai, headed the Keichō Embassy, a diplomatic mission to Pope Paul V. He went by way of New Spain and visited various ports of call in Europe.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Across the Pacific and New Spain</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="38.434,141.303" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tsukinoura</span> <span class="jr-date">28 October 1613</span><p>The San Juan Bautista, a ship built for the purpose, left for Acapulco with around 180 people on board, among them samurai of the shōgun and of Sendai, merchants, sailors and about 40 Spaniards and Portuguese.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.440,-124.409" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cape Mendocino</span> <span class="jr-date">26 December 1613</span><p>The ship sighted Cape Mendocino and continued along the coast of California.</p></li>
<li data-ll="16.863,-99.890" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a></span> <span class="jr-date">25 January 1614</span><p>After three months at sea the delegation arrived and was received with great ceremony. Fights broke out between the Japanese and the Spaniards, especially Vizcaíno, and orders were issued on 4 and 5 March to restore peace.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.354,-99.136" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a></span> <span class="jr-date">24 March 1614</span><p>The embassy entered the city and was received with great ceremony. Hasekura was lodged next to the Convent of San Francisco and met the Viceroy, and many of his companions were baptised there in April.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.190,-96.153" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/veracruz-city">Veracruz</a></span><p>The embassy went to Veracruz to board the fleet of Don Antonio Oquendo. Hasekura left part of his group behind in Mexico, and the fleet sailed for Europe on 10 June.</p></li>
<li data-ll="23.137,-82.359" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a></span> <span class="jr-date">July 1614</span><p>The embassy stopped in Havana in Cuba and changed ships there. It stayed in the city for six days before going on to Spain.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Spain, France and Rome</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="36.779,-6.354" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Sanlúcar de Barrameda</span> <span class="jr-date">5 October 1614</span><p>The fleet arrived safely after some dangers and storms. The Duke of Medina Sidonia was told of its arrival and sent carriages to bring the ambassador and his gentlemen ashore.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.389,-5.995" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a></span> <span class="jr-date">23 October 1614</span><p>Hasekura entered the city accompanied by 30 Japanese with blades, a captain of the guard, and 12 bowmen and halberdiers with painted lances and ceremonial blades.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.417,-3.703" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a></span> <span class="jr-date">30 January 1615</span><p>Hasekura met King Philip III and gave him a letter from Date Masamune and an offer of a treaty. The king answered that he would do what he could. Hasekura was baptised on 17 February at the Convent of Las Descalzas Reales.</p></li>
<li data-ll="43.270,6.639" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Saint-Tropez</span> <span class="jr-date">1615</span><p>Bad weather forced the embassy, sailing on three Spanish frigates towards Italy, to stay a few days in the French harbour. Local nobility received them, and the town's chronicles recorded their visit.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.893,12.483" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a></span> <span class="jr-date">20 September 1615</span><p>The embassy met Pope Paul V on 3 November and gave him two letters requesting a trade treaty and missionaries. The Pope agreed to send missionaries but left the trade decision to the King of Spain.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The way home</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="12">
<li data-ll="37.389,-5.995" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a></span> <span class="jr-date">June 1616</span><p>After meeting the king again in April 1616, Hasekura found that he declined to sign a trade agreement. The mission left Seville for New Spain in June. Some Japanese may have stayed behind in villages near the city.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.596,120.977" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a></span> <span class="jr-date">April 1618</span><p>The San Juan Bautista reached the Philippines with Hasekura and Luis Sotelo on board. The Spanish government bought the ship, and Hasekura bought goods for Date Masamune and built a ship.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.750,129.880" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a></span> <span class="jr-date">August 1620</span><p>Hasekura finally returned to Japan, reaching the harbour of Nagasaki after his stay in the Philippines.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.268,140.869" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/sendai">Sendai</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1620</span><p>Hasekura reported his travels to Date Masamune and gave him a portrait of Pope Paul V and a portrait of himself in prayer. Two days later an edict against Christians was promulgated in the Sendai fief.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Hasekura returned to Japan in August 1620, reaching the harbour of Nagasaki. European monarchs had refused the trade agreements he sought, and he died of illness a year later.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Hasekura Tsunenaga&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="tasman" data-kids="0">
<h2>Abel Tasman's Voyage of 1642 to 1643</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1642–1643 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/abel-tasman">Abel Tasman</a> &#183; 11 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">In August 1642 the Council of the Indies at Batavia sent the Dutch East India Company's skipper Abel Tasman and the cartographer Frans Jacobszoon Visscher to explore little-charted waters east of the Cape of Good Hope. They sailed with two small ships, the Heemskerck and the Zeehaen.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Batavia to Tasmania</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="-6.175,106.827" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Batavia</a></span> <span class="jr-date">14 August 1642</span><p>The Heemskerck and the Zeehaen left Batavia, the Company's base on Java, on a voyage meant to explore the little-charted regions of the south. One aim was to learn about the Provinces of Beach, a phantom island said to hold plentiful gold.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-20.200,57.500" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a></span> <span class="jr-date">5 September 1642</span><p>The ships reached the island after 22 days, according to the captain's journal. There was fresh water and timber for repairs, and the governor, Adriaan van der Stel, helped Tasman. They left on 8 October for the Roaring Forties.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-42.294,145.359" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">west coast of Tasmania</span> <span class="jr-date">24 November 1642</span><p>After snow and hail on 7 November turned the ships north-east, Tasman sighted the west coast of Tasmania north of Macquarie Harbour. He named it Van Diemen's Land after Antonio van Diemen, Governor-General of the Dutch East Indies.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-43.140,147.530" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Storm Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">late November 1642</span><p>Tasman tried to work the two ships into Adventure Bay on South Bruny Island but a storm blew him out to sea. He named the area Storm Bay.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-42.860,147.861" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Frederick Hendrik Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">1 December 1642</span><p>Tasman anchored north of Cape Frederick Hendrick. On 2 December two boats under the pilot Visscher rowed into Blackman Bay and gathered edible greens, and on 3 December Tasman formally claimed the land.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-40.994,148.349" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Eddystone Point</span> <span class="jr-date">December 1642</span><p>Tasman followed the east coast northward for two more days. Where the land turned north-west at Eddystone Point the Roaring Forties struck the ships, and he turned east to continue his search for the Southern Continent.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">New Zealand</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="-41.746,171.468" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cape Foulwind</span> <span class="jr-date">14 December 1642</span><p>On 13 December the ships sighted the north-west coast of the South Island. Tasman named it Staten Landt and thought it the coast of the unknown Southland. On 14 December they anchored off the coast, and Māori who saw them named a place Tiropahi.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-40.667,172.833" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Golden Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">December 1642</span><p>Māori paddled out in a waka and attacked sailors rowing between the ships, clubbing four to death with patu. As the ships left, the Zeehaen fired on approaching waka. Tasman named the area Murderers' Bay.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-41.229,174.483" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cook Strait</span> <span class="jr-date">December 1642</span><p>The expedition sailed north and sighted Cook Strait, which it mistook for a bight and named Zeehaen's Bight. In the far north it named Cape Maria van Diemen and the Three Kings Islands, names that still stand.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Fiji and the return to Batavia</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="10">
<li data-ll="-16.583,179.183" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/vanua-levu">Vanua Levu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">January 1643</span><p>After coming across the Tongan archipelago on 20 January 1643, the ships nearly wrecked on the reefs of north-eastern Fiji. Tasman charted the eastern tip of Vanua Levu before reaching open sea again.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-6.175,106.827" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Batavia</a></span> <span class="jr-date">15 June 1643</span><p>The expedition turned north-west towards New Guinea and arrived back in Batavia. It had made no significant contact with the native inhabitants and set up no trade.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The ships came back to Batavia on 15 June 1643. The Company was disappointed that Tasman had found neither a promising area for trade nor a useful new shipping route, and for over a century Tasmania and New Zealand were not visited by Europeans.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Abel Tasman&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="bering" data-kids="1">
<h2>Bering and the Great Northern Expedition</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1733–1743 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/vitus-bering">Vitus Bering</a> &#183; 7 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The Great Northern Expedition, conceived by Peter the Great and led by Vitus Bering, was a Russian expedition of roughly 1733 to 1743 that mapped most of the Arctic coast of Siberia and much of the Arctic coast of North America. Bering led one of its two Pacific divisions, with the aim of finding the sea route to North America.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Saint Petersburg to Yakutsk</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="59.950,30.317" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1733</span><p>The two Pacific divisions of the expedition, led by Martin Spanberg and Vitus Bering, left the capital in February and April 1733. The academic group followed on 8 August 1733.</p></li>
<li data-ll="62.027,129.732" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/yakutsk">Yakutsk</a></span> <span class="jr-date">September 1736</span><p>The academic party reached Yakutsk, where almost all members of the two Pacific divisions had already gathered, so that the scholars had trouble finding lodgings. The scholars were to travel on to Kamchatka with Bering.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">To Alaska and Bering Island</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="3">
<li data-ll="53.017,158.650" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Petropavlovsk</span> <span class="jr-date">June 1741</span><p>The St. Peter and the St. Paul set sail from Petropavlovsk. Six days later fog separated them, and both ships kept sailing east.</p></li>
<li data-ll="60.292,-140.931" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Mount Saint Elias</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 16 July 1741</span><p>Bering and the crew of the St. Peter sighted a towering peak on the Alaska mainland. Bering was anxious to return to Russia and turned westward.</p></li>
<li data-ll="59.934,-144.368" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Kayak Island</span> <span class="jr-date">1741</span><p>Bering anchored off the island while crew members went ashore to explore and find water. The naturalist Georg Wilhelm Steller walked around the island and recorded its plants and wildlife.</p></li>
<li data-ll="55.000,166.250" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Bering Island</span> <span class="jr-date">November 1741</span><p>Storms battered the ship, which was wrecked on the shore of the island that many of the crew took for the coast of Kamchatka. Bering fell sick with scurvy and died there on 8 December 1741. Of those left, 28 died over the winter.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The return</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="53.017,158.650" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Petropavlovsk</span> <span class="jr-date">August 1742</span><p>When the weather improved, the 46 survivors built a 40-foot boat from the wreckage and sailed for Petropavlovsk. Bering's crew reached the shore of Kamchatka in 1742 with word of the expedition.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Bering died of scurvy on 8 December 1741 on what later became known as Bering Island. The 46 survivors built a boat from the wreckage and sailed for Petropavlovsk in August 1742, carrying sea otter pelts that sparked Russian settlement in Alaska.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Great Northern Expedition&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="bougainville" data-kids="1">
<h2>Bougainville's Voyage Round the World</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1766–1769 &#183; Louis Antoine de Bougainville &#183; 8 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">In 1766 Louis XV gave Bougainville permission to circumnavigate the globe. He sailed with two ships, the Boudeuse and the Étoile. It was the first expedition to circumnavigate the globe with professional naturalists and geographers aboard.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Nantes to Tahiti</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="47.217,-1.554" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a></span> <span class="jr-date">15 November 1766</span><p>Bougainville left Nantes with two ships, the Boudeuse under Nicolas Pierre Duclos-Guyot and the Étoile under François Chenard de la Giraudais. The crews numbered 214 aboard the Boudeuse and 116 aboard the Étoile.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.867,-148.067" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Mehetia</span><p>After sighting islands of the Tuamotu group on 22 March, Bougainville saw the peak of Mehetia on 2 April. He then went on to Tahiti.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.667,-149.467" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tahiti</span><p>Bougainville visited Tahiti, where the British captain Samuel Wallis had made a visit and claim less than a year before. He claimed the island for France and named it New Cythera. Jeanne Barret, Commerçon's valet, was found to be a woman and confessed.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Westward to Batavia and home</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="-15.417,166.900" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-santo">Espiritu Santo</a></span><p>The expedition left Tahiti and sailed west past southern Samoa and the New Hebrides. On sighting Espiritu Santo it turned west, still looking for the Southern Continent.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-6.244,155.384" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/bougainville-island">Bougainville Island</a></span><p>The ships sailed through the Solomon Islands but, because of the hostility of the people there, did not land. Bougainville named Bougainville Island for himself.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-3.330,152.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">New Ireland</span><p>People from New Ireland attacked the expedition, and the French then made for the Moluccas.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-6.175,106.827" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Batavia</a></span><p>At Batavia the expedition received news that Wallis and Carteret had reached Tahiti before Bougainville.</p></li>
<li data-ll="48.649,-2.026" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a></span> <span class="jr-date">16 March 1769</span><p>The expedition completed its circumnavigation here. It had lost only seven of its 340 crew. Bougainville brought with him Ahutoru, a Tahitian who had volunteered to come.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The expedition completed its circumnavigation at Saint-Malo on 16 March 1769, having lost only seven of its 340 crew. Bougainville published his journal in 1771, and its account of Tahitian society was a sensation.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Louis Antoine de Bougainville&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="cook" data-kids="0">
<h2>James Cook's First Voyage in HMS Endeavour</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1768–1771 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> &#183; 19 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The first voyage of James Cook was a combined Royal Navy and Royal Society expedition to the south Pacific aboard HMS Endeavour. Its aims were to observe the 1769 transit of Venus from Tahiti and to seek evidence of the postulated Terra Australis Incognita.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">To Tahiti and the Society Islands</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="50.371,-4.142" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Plymouth</span> <span class="jr-date">26 August 1768</span><p>Cook left Plymouth Dockyard with 94 people and 18 months of provisions.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-22.911,-43.206" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a></span> <span class="jr-date">15 November 1768</span><p>Endeavour stayed at Rio until 2 December to re-provision and make repairs. The Viceroy suspected the voyage had other aims, and Cook took offence at his suspicion.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-55.980,-67.289" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-horn">Cape Horn</a></span><p>After leaving Rio de Janeiro the ship rounded Cape Horn and continued westward across the Pacific, reaching Tahiti in April 1769.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.500,-149.500" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Matavai Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">13 April 1769</span><p>Endeavour arrived at Matavai Bay in Tahiti, where Cook commissioned a small fort and observatory at Point Venus. The transit was observed on 3 June.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-16.733,-151.033" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/huahine">Huahine</a></span> <span class="jr-date">16 July 1769</span><p>Endeavour entered the harbour at Huahine. Tupaia, a local priest and mariner, had joined the ship as pilot, interpreter and intermediary.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-16.820,-151.440" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Raiatea</span> <span class="jr-date">20 July 1769</span><p>Cook raised the flag and claimed Raiatea-Tahaa and the nearby islands for Great Britain, naming them the Society Islands.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">New Zealand</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="-38.716,177.991" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Poverty Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">7 October 1769</span><p>Cook and a landing party went ashore in the north-east of the North Island. In the first two days the encounters with Māori resulted in the death of four or five locals.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-39.643,177.096" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cape Kidnappers</span> <span class="jr-date">15 October 1769</span><p>Three more Māori were killed in an encounter off Cape Kidnappers. Cook's journal shows regret that he had failed his instructions to avoid hostilities.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-36.810,175.743" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Mercury Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">9 November 1769</span><p>Cook observed the transit of Mercury here and formally claimed possession of the bay for Great Britain. One local was killed in a trading dispute, the last recorded death of a Māori at the hands of Cook's crew.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-41.250,174.016" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Queen Charlotte Sound</span> <span class="jr-date">31 January 1770</span><p>Cook claimed possession of the sound and the adjacent lands in the name of King George III. From Arapawa Island he had seen the strait he named Cook Strait.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The coast of New Holland</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="11">
<li data-ll="-38.000,148.883" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Point Hicks</span> <span class="jr-date">19 April 1770</span><p>Land was sighted at 6 a.m. and Cook named it Point Hicks. His party became the first recorded Europeans to encounter Australia's eastern coastline.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.983,151.183" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Botany Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">29 April 1770</span><p>Cook's party landed at Silver Beach on Botany Bay (Kamay). Two Gweagal men opposed the landing, and Cook fired small shot and wounded one of them in the leg.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-24.169,151.882" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Seventeen Seventy</span> <span class="jr-date">23 May 1770</span><p>Endeavour stopped at Bustard Bay. On 24 May Cook and others went ashore, found a freshwater stream and saw fires and shells showing Aboriginal occupation.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-15.458,145.233" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Endeavour River</span> <span class="jr-date">16 June 1770</span><p>After running aground on the Great Barrier Reef on 11 June, Cook got Endeavour into the river mouth on 16 June. Repairs delayed the voyage almost seven weeks, and encounters with the Guugu Yimidhirr were mainly peaceable.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-10.727,142.397" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Possession Island</span> <span class="jr-date">22 August 1770</span><p>Cook named Cape York and then went to Possession Island, where he claimed the whole eastern coastline he had explored as British territory.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Home by the East Indies</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="16">
<li data-ll="-6.135,106.813" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Batavia</span> <span class="jr-date">October 1770</span><p>Endeavour came into Batavia, the capital of the Dutch East Indies, for repairs. Seven of the expedition died there, among them Tupaia, and more died of illness on the passage home.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-34.358,18.472" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a></span> <span class="jr-date">13 March 1771</span><p>Endeavour rounded the Cape of Good Hope after leaving Batavia on 26 December.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-15.965,-5.715" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a></span><p>After rounding the Cape of Good Hope, Cook stopped at Saint Helena. England was sighted again on 10 July 1771.</p></li>
<li data-ll="51.223,1.401" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Deal</span> <span class="jr-date">12 July 1771</span><p>Endeavour anchored in the Downs that afternoon and Cook went ashore at Deal, Kent. His return was unexpected, as newspapers had reported fears that the ship was lost.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Endeavour reached the English port of Deal on 12 July 1771, and the voyage lasted almost three years. Seven of the expedition died in Batavia and a further 23 on the passage from there to England.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;First voyage of James Cook&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="cook-second" data-kids="0">
<h2>Cook's Second Voyage: The Resolution and the Adventure</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1772–1775 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> &#183; 24 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">James Cook sailed in the Resolution, with the Adventure under Tobias Furneaux, on a voyage commissioned by the British government with advice from the Royal Society. It was designed to circumnavigate the globe as far south as possible and to settle whether a great southern landmass, Terra Australis, existed.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">To the Cape and round to New Zealand</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="50.343,-4.143" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/plymouth-sound">Plymouth Sound</a></span> <span class="jr-date">13 July 1772</span><p>The Resolution and the Adventure left Plymouth Sound on a Monday. The Resolution carried a crew of 112, among them the astronomer William Wales, the artist William Hodges and the Royal Society scientists Johann Reinhold Forster and his son Georg.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.650,-16.917" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Funchal</span> <span class="jr-date">1 August 1772</span><p>The first port of call was Funchal in the Madeira Islands. From Funchal Roads Cook reported to the Admiralty on the sailing qualities of the Resolution before sailing on after a short stay.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.918,-23.509" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/praia">Porto Praya</a></span> <span class="jr-date">12 August 1772</span><p>The ships took on water at Porto Praya on San Tiago, in the Cape Verde Islands. Cook and Wales surveyed the bay, the Forsters collected plants, and hogs, goats, poultry and fruit were plentiful.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.850,18.450" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a></span> <span class="jr-date">30 October 1772</span><p>Both ships anchored near Cape Town. The Swedish naturalist Anders Sparrman joined the expedition there as an assistant naturalist at his own expense, and Wales and Bayly checked the chronometers.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-45.766,166.593" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Dusky Sound</span><p>After the Antarctic Circle was first crossed on 17 January 1773 and the ships were separated in fog on 8 February, the Resolution sighted New Zealand and Cook entered Dusky Sound. The crew recovered with fresh water, wood and fish, and met cautious but curious Māori.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-41.093,174.239" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Ship Cove</span> <span class="jr-date">May 1773</span><p>The Resolution anchored in Ship Cove, in Queen Charlotte Sound, and was rejoined by the Adventure, which had arrived on 7 April. Cook planned to use the winter to explore the uncharted seas to the east and north.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The Pacific in the southern winter and spring</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="-17.747,-149.161" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tautira</span> <span class="jr-date">August 1773</span><p>Both ships reached Tahiti and stopped at Vaitepiha Bay so that the Adventure's sick could go ashore for fresh food and rest. Tahiti had recently been through a civil war, and the hog breeding Cook relied on had been disrupted. Cook left Tautira on 24 August.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.500,-149.500" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Matavai Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">26 August 1773</span><p>Cook met the chief Maritata, who promised to introduce him to Prince Otoo. Cook and Furneaux visited Otoo the next day, and Omai joined the Adventure and Porio the Resolution when the ships left.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-16.733,-151.033" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/huahine">Huahine</a></span> <span class="jr-date">September 1773</span><p>The chief Ori, who had hosted Cook before, welcomed the ships, and hogs were bought in numbers. Sparrman, collecting plants alone, was attacked by two local men who stripped him of his clothes and knife and chased him along the beach.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-21.211,-175.153" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tongatapu">Tongatapu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">October 1773</span><p>The people of Tongatapu welcomed the visitors and brought out canoes of provisions. After thefts, Cook ordered muskets fired at one man and others tied up and flogged. He later called Eua, Tongatapu and Nomuka the Friendly Archipelago.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-41.093,174.239" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Ship Cove</span> <span class="jr-date">3 November 1773</span><p>The Resolution reached Queen Charlotte Sound after a gale off Cook Strait, but the Adventure, parted from her on 30 October, was not there. Cook left a message buried under a marked tree and sailed on 25 November.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-41.250,174.016" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Grass Cove</span> <span class="jr-date">December 1773</span><p>Furneaux's cutter, sent for greens, did not return. Burney's search party found shoes, a hand identified as Thomas Hill's and dismembered bodies at Grass Cove, and fired on the Māori there.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The second Antarctic crossing and the islands of the south-west Pacific</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="13">
<li data-ll="-27.120,-109.350" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a></span> <span class="jr-date">11 March 1774</span><p>After crossing the Antarctic Circle for the third time on 26 January and turning back from the ice at 71° S, the Resolution sighted Easter Island. Cook recognised it by its location and the moai, and Cook himself had been dangerously ill.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-9.938,-139.111" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Vaitahu Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">April 1774</span><p>Cook sailed for the port Mendaña had used in the Marquesas, on Tahuata. Canoes came out to trade, but theft by islanders brought a violent response from the crew, and a local man died. Trade resumed later.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.500,-149.500" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Matavai Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">22 April 1774</span><p>The Resolution anchored in Tahiti after passing through the Tuamotu atolls. The crew's scurvy had gone, supplies were restocked and Wales checked the chronometer.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-20.250,-174.800" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Nomuka</span><p>The English met hostility and theft, and Cook used force to recover stolen goods. Relations then improved and regular trade began. Cook declined the offer of a young woman, which upset her family.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-18.800,169.067" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Eromanga</span><p>In the New Hebrides, a conflict broke out during an attempted landing at Eromanga, with injuries on both sides. The English found the Eromangans different from the Malekulans in appearance and language.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-19.500,169.333" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tanna</span><p>An active volcano drew scientific interest. The English met resistance at first, then traded. Islanders blocked them from parts of the island, which they later realised were sacred. They stayed more than two weeks.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-21.250,165.300" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">New Caledonia</span><p>The Resolution reached New Caledonia, and Cook gave the island that name. The English noted that the tall, strong islanders were open with them and that there was no theft.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-29.033,167.950" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/norfolk-island">Norfolk Island</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1774</span><p>The Resolution sighted the Isle of Pines and Norfolk Island, both valued for their timber, then returned to New Zealand. There the Māori assured Cook that all was well, though the Adventure had gone.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Homeward by Cape Horn and the Atlantic</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="21">
<li data-ll="-54.317,-36.650" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">South Georgia</span> <span class="jr-date">January 1775</span><p>After rounding Cape Horn at the end of December 1774, the Resolution sighted land in icy conditions: no continent, but an island of seals, penguins and birds, which Johann Forster named South Georgia. The ship left on 20 January 1775.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.925,18.424" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a></span> <span class="jr-date">March 1775</span><p>The Resolution anchored for repairs after a long voyage, and Sparrman left to continue his botanical studies. At sea Cook had heard from passing ships that Furneaux's crew had suffered casualties in New Zealand.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-15.965,-5.715" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">St Helena</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1775</span><p>After five weeks of repairs the Resolution sailed with the Dutton to St Helena, where Cook met an old acquaintance, John Skottowe, and wrote a favourable account of the island.</p></li>
<li data-ll="50.751,-1.137" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Spithead</span> <span class="jr-date">30 July 1775</span><p>The Resolution anchored at Spithead, ending more than three years of exploration. Cook was promoted to captain and made a Fellow of the Royal Society.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The Resolution anchored at Spithead on 30 July 1775. Cook's reports put to rest the myth of Terra Australis, and the Larcum Kendall K1 chronometer had let him fix his longitude with much greater accuracy.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Second voyage of James Cook&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="cook-third" data-kids="0">
<h2>James Cook's Third Voyage</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1776–1780 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> &#183; 23 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">James Cook sailed from Plymouth in 1776 in HMS Resolution, with HMS Discovery under Charles Clerke, on a British attempt to find the Northwest Passage. The stated purpose was to return Omai, a young man from Raiatea, to his homeland.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Plymouth to New Zealand</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="50.371,-4.142" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Plymouth</span> <span class="jr-date">1776</span><p>The Resolution under Cook and the Discovery under Clerke were prepared for the voyage, which started from Plymouth. Clerke was in prison for debt until the end of July and joined the ships afterwards.</p></li>
<li data-ll="28.269,-16.606" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tenerife</span> <span class="jr-date">4 August 1776</span><p>Cook stopped here rather than at Madeira to obtain better feed for the livestock. He met French and Spanish naval officers, and left on 4 August after three days.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-34.358,18.472" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a></span> <span class="jr-date">17 October 1776</span><p>Cook reached the Cape and anchored in Table Bay the next day, arriving twelve days sooner than on his previous voyage. The Discovery arrived on 10 November, and the ships sailed together on 1 December.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-42.000,147.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Van Diemen's Land</span> <span class="jr-date">26 January 1777</span><p>Driven by strong westerly winds, the ships reached the island now called Tasmania. They took on water and wood and had brief contact with the Aboriginal people living there.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-41.250,174.016" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Queen Charlotte Sound</span> <span class="jr-date">12 February 1777</span><p>Cook kept armed guards with shore parties after earlier violence at Grass Cove. The local people expected utu, or revenge, for Grass Cove, but Cook refused to act, saying he would remain a friend unless provoked again.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The Society Islands and Christmas Island</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="-17.667,-149.467" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tahiti</span> <span class="jr-date">12 August 1777</span><p>After weeks at Tonga and a sighting of Tubuai, where he chose not to land, Cook reached Tahiti. Omai's return created a difficult situation, as his countrymen showed more interest in his treasures than in him.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.535,-149.835" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/moorea">Moorea</a></span><p>After a goat was stolen, Cook led an armed group across the island, burning homes, canoes and crops. Cook wrote that the affair was regretted by the islanders no more than by himself, and some officers were uncomfortable at what they saw.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-16.733,-151.033" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/huahine">Huahine</a></span> <span class="jr-date">October 1777</span><p>Omai wished to reclaim his father's land on Raiatea, but Cook arranged with the Huahine chiefs for land on which to build him a house instead. Cook left Omai livestock and some powder for his firearms.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-16.820,-151.440" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Raiatea</span> <span class="jr-date">3 November 1777</span><p>Chief Orio and his family greeted the ships. Two groups of men deserted during the stay and were recovered; Shaw was flogged and Mouat punished and imprisoned.</p></li>
<li data-ll="1.850,-157.400" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Christmas Island</span> <span class="jr-date">24 December 1777</span><p>The ships sighted an atoll and anchored. The men fished and took 300 turtles, observed a solar eclipse on 30 December, and left on 2 January 1778. Cook left a sealed bottle claiming the island for Britain.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Hawaii and the North Pacific coast</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="11">
<li data-ll="21.957,-159.669" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Waimea Bay, Kauai</span> <span class="jr-date">19 January 1778</span><p>The ships approached Kauai on 19 January. During a scouting mission a local man was shot and killed, which was not at once reported to Cook. At Waimea Bay the islanders greeted him by prostrating themselves. The ships left the group on 2 February.</p></li>
<li data-ll="44.772,-124.076" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cape Foulweather</span> <span class="jr-date">7 March 1778</span><p>Cook sighted the coast of present-day Oregon and named this promontory for the severe weather. Storms then drove the ships south, costing over two degrees of latitude by 13 March.</p></li>
<li data-ll="49.783,-126.633" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Nootka Sound</span> <span class="jr-date">29 March 1778</span><p>The ships found an inlet suitable for repairs on the western coast of what is now Vancouver Island. Local people eager to trade greeted them with song. The ships left on 26 April and a five-day hurricane followed.</p></li>
<li data-ll="60.615,-147.168" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Prince William Sound</span> <span class="jr-date">20 May 1778</span><p>Against his orders to avoid inlets until 65°N, Cook turned into the Gulf of Alaska at 61°N to examine a bay. He met people marked with tattoos and piercings, then left the inlet doubting a passage existed.</p></li>
<li data-ll="65.640,-168.120" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cape Prince of Wales</span> <span class="jr-date">9 August 1778</span><p>The fog lifted at 65°N. Cook anchored in Lawrence Bay to trade with the Chukchi people, crossed the Bering Strait two days later, and on 17 August met ice at 70°N that ended the search.</p></li>
<li data-ll="53.874,-166.537" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Unalaska</span> <span class="jr-date">27 October 1778</span><p>Cook spent his fiftieth birthday at sea near Unalaska. He corrected Russian maps, replenished supplies and met Russian officials, and planned to winter at the Sandwich Islands rather than in Kamchatka.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Kealakekua Bay and Cook's death</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="17">
<li data-ll="19.475,-155.929" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kealakekua-bay">Kealakekua Bay</a></span> <span class="jr-date">17 January 1779</span><p>After sailing round Hawaii island, Cook brought both ships into the bay, unaware that his arrival coincided with the Makahiki, a harvest festival of worship of the god Lono. The ships stayed about a month.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.475,-155.929" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kealakekua-bay">Kaawaloa</a></span><p>After thefts and quarrels, including the theft of the Discovery's cutter, Cook went ashore to take King Terreeoboo hostage until it was returned. A chief, Kalimu, was killed by fire from the ships, a crowd attacked, and Cook was killed. At least seventeen Hawaiians died, and several marines.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The voyage home</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="19">
<li data-ll="52.933,158.600" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Awatska Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">29 April 1779</span><p>The ships entered the bay to deliver letters and buy supplies. A party under Gore, King and Webber travelled to Bolcheretsk, where Major Behm promised help, and the English doctors treated local people for scurvy.</p></li>
<li data-ll="52.933,158.600" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Awatska Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">22 August 1779</span><p>After a second failed attempt through the ice, Clerke, gravely ill, died just before the ships reached the harbour. Gore took command of the Resolution and King of the Discovery.</p></li>
<li data-ll="22.190,113.538" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macao</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1 December 1779</span><p>The ships left the bay on 9 October and reached Macao. The crew sold furs, earning about £2,000 in goods and money, and many sailors wanted to return to collect more.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-34.193,18.436" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Simon's Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">11 April 1780</span><p>After leaving Macao on 13 January 1780, the ships stopped at Prince's Island for water and then anchored in Simon's Bay, leaving on 9 May with fresh supplies.</p></li>
<li data-ll="51.496,0.882" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">The Nore</span> <span class="jr-date">4 October 1780</span><p>Ireland was sighted on 12 August, but weather forced the ships north to Stromness. They reached the Nore after an absence of over four years.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Cook was killed at Kealakekua Bay on 14 February 1779 in a violent exchange with Hawaiians. Clerke took command and died of tuberculosis, and under John Gore the crews reached the Nore in October 1780 without having found a passage.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Third voyage of James Cook&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="la-perouse" data-kids="0">
<h2>The Lapérouse Expedition</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1785–1788 &#183; Jean-François de Galaup, comte de Lapérouse &#183; 17 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Lapérouse was appointed in 1785 by Louis XVI to lead a scientific expedition around the world with two ships, L'Astrolabe and La Boussole. It was to complete James Cook's Pacific discoveries and correct his maps, and also to establish trade contacts and open new maritime routes.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Brest to Botany Bay</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="48.391,-4.486" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Brest</span> <span class="jr-date">1 August 1785</span><p>Lapérouse and his 220 men left Brest on 1 August 1785 in L'Astrolabe and La Boussole, and rounded Cape Horn.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-27.120,-109.350" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a></span> <span class="jr-date">9 April 1786</span><p>After investigating the Spanish colonial government in Chile, the expedition arrived at Easter Island.</p></li>
<li data-ll="20.770,-156.268" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1786</span><p>Lapérouse sailed to the Hawaiian Islands, where he and his crew became the first Europeans to set foot on Maui. James Cook had charted the island in 1778 and traded with the people from his ships.</p></li>
<li data-ll="58.637,-137.573" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Lituya Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">13 July 1786</span><p>Lapérouse landed near Mount Saint Elias in late June and explored. On 13 July a barge and two longboats carrying 21 men were lost in the heavy currents of the bay he called Port des Français. The men had visited the Tlingit people.</p></li>
<li data-ll="36.600,-121.895" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/monterey-california">Monterey</a></span> <span class="jr-date">14 September 1786</span><p>Lapérouse reached the Presidio of Monterey in Spanish Las Californias and examined the Spanish settlements and missions. He wrote critically of the missionaries' treatment of the California indigenous peoples and likened conditions at a mission to a slave plantation.</p></li>
<li data-ll="22.190,113.538" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1786 or 1787</span><p>The expedition crossed the Pacific again in 100 days and arrived at Macau, where Lapérouse sold the furs acquired in Alaska and divided the profits among his men.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.596,120.977" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a></span> <span class="jr-date">9 April 1787</span><p>After a visit to Manila, the expedition set out on 9 April 1787 for the coasts of northeast Asia.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.380,126.550" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/jeju-island">Quelpart</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1787</span><p>Lapérouse saw the island of Quelpart, present-day Jeju. Europeans had visited it only once before, when Dutchmen were shipwrecked there in 1635. He then visited the Asian mainland coasts of Korea.</p></li>
<li data-ll="50.500,143.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Oku-Yeso Island (Sakhalin)</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1787</span><p>On Sakhalin the Ainu people drew Lapérouse a map showing Hokkaidō and the coasts of Tartary. He tried to sail north through the Strait of Tartary and failed, then turned south and sailed east through the strait that now bears his name.</p></li>
<li data-ll="53.017,158.650" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Petropavlovsk</span> <span class="jr-date">7 September 1787</span><p>The expedition rested at Petropavlovsk on Kamchatka and enjoyed Russian hospitality. Letters from Paris ordered Lapérouse to investigate the British settlement in New South Wales. Barthélemy de Lesseps left here to carry the logs, charts and letters to France.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-14.300,-170.722" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tutuila">Navigator Islands (Samoa)</a></span> <span class="jr-date">6 December 1787</span><p>The expedition stopped in the Navigator Islands (Samoa). Just before it left, Samoans attacked a group of his men at Massacre Bay, killing twelve, among them Lamanon and de Langle, commander of L'Astrolabe. Twenty men were wounded.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.983,151.183" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Botany Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">24 January 1788</span><p>The expedition arrived off Botany Bay and met the British First Fleet under Arthur Phillip, which moved to Port Jackson on 26 January. The French spent six weeks there, built an observatory and a garden, and sent journals and letters to Europe. Father Receveur died on 17 February.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.983,151.183" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Botany Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">10 March 1788</span><p>After taking on wood and fresh water, the expedition left for New Caledonia, Santa Cruz, the Solomons, the Louisiades and the coasts of Australia. It was the last recorded landfall: no one from the expedition was seen again by Europeans.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The search and the wrecks</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="14">
<li data-ll="-11.617,166.967" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Vanikoro</span> <span class="jr-date">May 1793</span><p>Entrecasteaux, sent from Brest in 1791 to search for Lapérouse, sighted Santa Cruz and an uncharted island that was Vanikoro. His ships did not approach it and sailed away.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-12.296,168.832" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tikopia</span> <span class="jr-date">1826</span><p>The Irish sea captain Peter Dillon bought swords he had reason to believe had belonged to Lapérouse or his officers. The islanders said they came from nearby Vanikoro, where two big ships had broken up years earlier.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-11.617,166.967" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Vanikoro</span> <span class="jr-date">1826</span><p>Dillon sailed to Vanikoro and found cannonballs, anchors and other remains of ships in the water between coral reefs. A Tikopian named Pu Ratia showed him the direction to sail.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-11.617,166.967" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Vanikoro</span> <span class="jr-date">May 2005</span><p>The wreck examined in 1964 was formally identified as La Boussole. Both ships had been wrecked on the reefs, Boussole first. A group of men, probably survivors of Boussole, were massacred by the local inhabitants, according to the islanders.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">After leaving Botany Bay on 10 March 1788, neither Lapérouse nor any member of his expedition was seen again by Europeans. Both ships were wrecked on the reefs of Vanikoro, and Peter Dillon found the evidence in 1826.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Lapérouse expedition&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="first-fleet" data-kids="0">
<h2>The First Fleet to Botany Bay and Sydney Cove</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1787–1788 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/arthur-phillip">Arthur Phillip and the First Fleet</a> &#183; 6 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The First Fleet was eleven British ships under Captain Arthur Phillip, carrying over 1,400 convicts, marines, sailors and colonial officials. Britain sent it to found a penal colony in New South Wales, after the end of transportation of criminals to North America following the American Revolution.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From England to the Cape</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="50.806,-1.087" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Portsmouth</span> <span class="jr-date">13 May 1787</span><p>Phillip tried to sail on 10 May, but sailors of the Fishburn refused to leave until a dispute about pay was settled. The fleet left on 13 May, and the convicts were allowed on deck in fine weather.</p></li>
<li data-ll="28.467,-16.250" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Santa Cruz, Tenerife</span> <span class="jr-date">3 June 1787</span><p>The fleet anchored here, and fresh water, vegetables and meat were brought on board. The local governor entertained Phillip and the chief officers, and one convict tried unsuccessfully to escape.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-22.911,-43.206" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a></span> <span class="jr-date">5 August 1787</span><p>The fleet stayed for a month. The ships were cleaned and repaired, water was taken on board, and Phillip ordered large quantities of food. The women convicts' infested clothing was burnt and they were given new clothes made from rice sacks.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.850,18.450" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a></span> <span class="jr-date">13 October 1787</span><p>Leaving Rio on 4 September, the fleet reached Table Bay, its last port of call. There it took on plants, seeds and livestock for the new colony, including seven cows, 44 sheep and 32 pigs.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Botany Bay and Sydney Cove</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="-33.983,151.183" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Botany Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">18 January 1788</span><p>Supply arrived first and the other ships within two days. The bay was open, the water shallow and the soil poor. The Cadigal people witnessed the arrival, and the Eora, first met here, seemed curious but suspicious.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.859,151.212" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/sydney-cove">Sydney Cove</a></span> <span class="jr-date">26 January 1788</span><p>Phillip had found Port Jackson a better site and moved the Fleet there. The anchorage had deep water, shelter and a small stream, and he named it Sydney Cove after Lord Sydney. The British flag was hoisted that day. Conflict with the Eora, including the Bidjigal clan, followed.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The Fleet reached Botany Bay on 18 January 1788 and moved to Port Jackson, where Phillip named Sydney Cove on 26 January 1788. The Cadigal and Eora people were already living in the area, and conflict began soon after, with Pemulwuy of the Bidjigal clan leading attacks on the colonists between 1790 and 1810.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;First Fleet&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="bounty" data-kids="0">
<h2>HMS Bounty: Tahiti, the Mutiny and the Two Ways Home</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1787–1790 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/hms-bounty">William Bligh and Fletcher Christian</a> &#183; 17 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">HMS Bounty left England in 1787 to collect breadfruit plants in Tahiti for the British colonies in the West Indies, under Lieutenant William Bligh. On 28 April 1789 acting-Lieutenant Fletcher Christian and others seized the ship and set Bligh and 18 loyalists adrift in the launch. The journey then divides: Bligh's open-boat voyage to Timor, and the mutineers' search for a refuge.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From England to Tahiti</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="51.478,-0.026" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Deptford</span> <span class="jr-date">15 October 1787</span><p>Bounty had been refitted at Deptford Dockyard under Joseph Banks's supervision, its great cabin turned into a greenhouse for over a thousand breadfruit plants. It left Deptford for Spithead to await final sailing orders.</p></li>
<li data-ll="50.751,-1.137" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Spithead</span> <span class="jr-date">23 December 1787</span><p>The Admiralty delayed Bligh's orders for three weeks. Bounty sailed on 28 November but was held by contrary winds and could not clear Spithead until 23 December.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-34.217,18.633" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">False Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">24 May 1788</span><p>Stormy weather off Cape Horn had driven Bounty back, and on 17 April Bligh turned for the Cape of Good Hope. The ship anchored in False Bay, where five weeks went on repairs and provisions.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-43.367,147.333" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Adventure Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">21 August 1788</span><p>Bligh sighted Mewstone Rock on 19 August and anchored in Adventure Bay two days later. The crew rested, fished, filled water casks and felled timber, with peaceful encounters with the native population.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.500,-149.500" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Matavai Bay, Tahiti</span> <span class="jr-date">26 October 1788</span><p>Bounty anchored after a journey of 27,086 nautical miles. Over five months many of the men lived ashore, Christian formed a close relationship with a Polynesian woman named Mauatua, and Bligh's punishments grew harsher.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Homeward, and the mutiny</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="-20.250,-174.800" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Nomuka</span> <span class="jr-date">22 April 1789</span><p>Bounty left Tahiti on 5 April with the breadfruit plants. At Nomuka Christian led a watering party that Bligh had ordered to leave its arms in the boat, and Bligh cursed him when the party returned with the task incomplete.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-19.750,-175.067" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">South of Tofua</span> <span class="jr-date">28 April 1789</span><p>Early in the morning Christian, with the help of men he had approached, took the ship and had Bligh tied and brought to the quarterdeck. Bligh and 18 loyalists were put into the launch, which was left with seven inches of freeboard.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Bligh's open-boat voyage</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="8">
<li data-ll="-19.750,-175.067" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tofua</span> <span class="jr-date">2 May 1789</span><p>Bligh hoped to find water and food ashore. The people of Tofua grew menacing, and on 2 May they seized the launch's stern rope. The quartermaster John Norton leapt into the water and was stoned to death.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-12.620,143.447" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Restoration Island</span> <span class="jr-date">28 May 1789</span><p>Having decided to sail for Kupang, the launch passed through the Fiji Islands without stopping, for fear of cannibalism. On 28 May Bligh found a gap in the Great Barrier Reef and landed on an island he named Restoration Island, where the men ate oysters and berries.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-10.687,142.531" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cape York</span> <span class="jr-date">2 June 1789</span><p>The launch cleared the northern point of the Australian continent and steered through shoals and reefs. That evening it reached the Arafura Sea, still 1,100 nautical miles from Kupang.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-10.163,123.578" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kupang">Kupang</a></span> <span class="jr-date">14 June 1789</span><p>The coast of Timor was sighted on 12 June, and the launch sailed into Kupang harbour under a makeshift Union Jack after about 3,500 nautical miles. Bligh reported the mutiny, and the botanist Nelson soon died of the climate.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Christian's way: Tubuai, Tahiti and Pitcairn</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="12">
<li data-ll="-23.370,-149.480" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tubuai</span> <span class="jr-date">28 May 1789</span><p>Christian threw the breadfruit plants into the sea and made for Tubuai, which he thought easy to defend. The islanders were hostile, and when war canoes came towards the ship Christian fired a four-pounder gun, killing at least a dozen warriors.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.667,-149.467" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tahiti</span> <span class="jr-date">6 June 1789</span><p>To settle on Tubuai the mutineers needed labour and women. Christian told the Tahitian chiefs a false story that he, Bligh and Cook were founding a settlement at Aitutaki, and on 16 June sailed with nearly thirty Tahitian men and women, some taken by deception.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-23.370,-149.480" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tubuai</span> <span class="jr-date">June 1789</span><p>The mutineers began building a moated enclosure called Fort George. Clashes with the islanders, mainly over property and women, ended in a pitched battle in which 66 islanders were killed. Sixteen of the party then chose to return to Tahiti.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.667,-149.467" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tahiti</span> <span class="jr-date">22 September 1789</span><p>Christian landed 15 of the 16 men who had voted to stay. That evening he cut the anchor rope and sailed with the Tahitians who were aboard, mainly women. He put six elderly women ashore on Mo'orea and kept 20 Polynesians, 14 of them women.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-25.071,-130.104" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Pitcairn Island</span> <span class="jr-date">15 January 1790</span><p>After months of searching, Christian found Pitcairn, uninhabited and almost inaccessible, 188 nautical miles east of its recorded position. The ship was stripped and burned on 23 January. Christian settled with Mauatua, and a son was born.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-25.071,-130.104" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Pitcairn Island</span> <span class="jr-date">September 1793</span><p>Tensions grew over the mutineers treating the Tahitians, especially the women, as their property. Tahitians killed several mutineers in a series of murders. By 1794 the six Tahitian men were all dead, killed by the mutineers' widows or by each other.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Bligh and his men reached Kupang on 14 June 1789 and Bligh landed in England on 14 March 1790. Christian's party settled on Pitcairn Island, where several mutineers and all six Tahitian men died in violence by 1794; the community was found by an American sealer in February 1808.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Mutiny on the Bounty&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="macartney" data-kids="1">
<h2>The Macartney Embassy to the Qianlong Emperor</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1792–1794 &#183; George Macartney, 1st Earl Macartney &#183; 17 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Viscount Macartney led the first British diplomatic mission to China, with about a hundred people. It sailed from Britain in 1792 to ask for new ports for British trade, a permanent embassy in Beijing, a small island for British use and fewer restrictions on trade at Canton.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The voyage out</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="50.806,-1.087" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Portsmouth</span> <span class="jr-date">26 September 1792</span><p>The delegation sailed from Portsmouth in three ships: HMS Lion, the East Indiaman Hindostan and the brig Jackall. Captain Sir Erasmus Gower commanded the Lion, which led the mission.</p></li>
<li data-ll="50.452,-3.557" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Torbay</span> <span class="jr-date">1792</span><p>A storm struck the squadron soon after it left and forced it to stop at Torbay for repairs. The Jackall went missing in the storm, and the Lion and Hindostan went on without her.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.750,-17.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a></span> <span class="jr-date">October 1792</span><p>The squadron stopped at Madeira in early October, and at the Canary Islands later in the same month.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.918,-23.509" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/praia">Porto Praya</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1 November 1792</span><p>The ships arrived at Porto Praya in Cape Verde and waited five days for the Jackall. The crews of the Lion and Hindostan believed she was wrecked, and the squadron sailed on without her.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-22.911,-43.206" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a></span> <span class="jr-date">November 1792</span><p>The trade winds off the coast of Africa forced the ships to sail west to Rio de Janeiro, which they reached at the end of November. Macartney had an attack of gout there that lasted a month.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-34.358,18.472" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a></span> <span class="jr-date">7 January 1793</span><p>The expedition left Rio de Janeiro on 17 December, sailed east again and rounded the Cape of Good Hope.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-6.175,106.827" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Batavia</a></span> <span class="jr-date">6 March 1793</span><p>The squadron passed Java in February and reached Batavia. There the embassy bought a French brig and named her Clarence, and the Jackall, which had turned back for repairs, rejoined the squadron.</p></li>
<li data-ll="16.069,108.210" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/da-nang">Tourane</a></span> <span class="jr-date">26 May 1793</span><p>The embassy anchored at Tourane until 16 June and visited Tây Sơn-ruled Cochinchina, where it received gifts from the emperor Cảnh Thịnh. Local people arrested the master of the Lion and seven other men and released them a week later.</p></li>
<li data-ll="22.190,113.538" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a></span> <span class="jr-date">19 June 1793</span><p>The full squadron arrived at Macau. George Staunton went ashore to meet East India Company officials, and three of the four Chinese Catholic priests left the mission, leaving one Chinese interpreter.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">North along the coast and up the Hai River</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="10">
<li data-ll="29.989,122.205" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Zhoushan</span> <span class="jr-date">June 1793</span><p>After leaving Macao on 23 June the embassy stopped at Zhoushan, where Staunton went ashore to meet the military governor of Dinghai. The governor supplied pilots, as the Emperor had ordered every port to do.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.977,117.705" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Dagu</span> <span class="jr-date">6 August 1793</span><p>Macartney and his group went on to Dagu in the smallest ships. There he and Staunton met Liang Kentang, Viceroy of Zhili, who told them the Emperor would receive them at Rehe (Jehol) rather than in Beijing.</p></li>
<li data-ll="39.147,117.206" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a></span> <span class="jr-date">11 August 1793</span><p>Macartney and Staunton attended a banquet with Viceroy Liang and the Manchu legate Zhengrui. Macartney persuaded the viceroy to let some of the gifts stay in Beijing to avoid damage on the journey to Rehe.</p></li>
<li data-ll="39.909,116.651" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tongzhou</span> <span class="jr-date">16 August 1793</span><p>The mission went up the Hai River in small boats pulled by men along the shore with ropes and harnesses, and landed at Tongzhou.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Beijing, the Great Wall and Chengde</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="14">
<li data-ll="39.904,116.408" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a></span> <span class="jr-date">21 August 1793</span><p>The embassy was escorted to a residence near the Old Summer Palace and not permitted to leave it. The gifts were stored in the throne room there, and the planetarium took 18 days to assemble.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.690,117.157" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Gubeikou</span> <span class="jr-date">September 1793</span><p>About seventy members of the mission, forty of them soldiers, left Beijing on 2 September and crossed the Great Wall at Gubeikou. Lieutenant Parish surveyed the Wall's fortifications, which aroused suspicion among their hosts.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.974,117.932" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Chengde</span> <span class="jr-date">8 September 1793</span><p>The entourage reached the outskirts of Chengde, where the Qianlong Emperor awaited them. The same day an edict allowed Macartney a single prostration in place of the usual nine.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.974,117.932" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Chengde</span> <span class="jr-date">14 September 1793</span><p>Macartney met the Qianlong Emperor in the imperial tent. After a dispute over the kowtow, it was agreed that he would genuflect on one knee, and he presented King George III's letter and exchanged gifts with the Emperor.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The Qianlong Emperor rejected all of the mission's requests. The embassy returned to London without obtaining any concession from China, but it brought back detailed observations of the empire.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Macartney Embassy&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="mungo-park" data-kids="0">
<h2>Mungo Park's Journeys to the Niger</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1795–1806 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/mungo-park">Mungo Park</a> &#183; 12 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Mungo Park, a Scottish surgeon, went to West Africa for the African Association to find the course of the Niger River. He made two journeys there, the first in 1795 to 1797 and the second, for the government, from 1805.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">First journey, 1795 to 1797</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="50.806,-1.087" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Portsmouth</span> <span class="jr-date">22 May 1795</span><p>Park left England on the brig Endeavour, a vessel bound for the Gambia to trade for beeswax and ivory.</p></li>
<li data-ll="13.567,-14.567" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Pisania</span> <span class="jr-date">2 December 1795</span><p>Park reached the Gambia River on 21 June and ascended it 200 miles to the British trading station of Pisania. On 2 December he set out for the interior with two local guides.</p></li>
<li data-ll="13.450,-6.267" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/segou">Ségou</a></span> <span class="jr-date">21 July 1796</span><p>Park had been held for four months by a Moorish chief at Ludamar. He escaped alone on 1 July with his horse and a pocket compass, and reached the Niger at Ségou.</p></li>
<li data-ll="12.646,-7.992" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a></span> <span class="jr-date">29 July 1796</span><p>Park began the return on 29 July by a route further south, keeping close to the Niger as far as Bamako. He traced the river's course for some 300 miles.</p></li>
<li data-ll="13.567,-14.567" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Pisania</span> <span class="jr-date">10 June 1797</span><p>Park came back to the trading station on the Gambia, where he had set out.</p></li>
<li data-ll="17.085,-61.800" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Antigua</span><p>Unable to book a passage to England from Bathurst, Park boarded a slave ship bound for Charleston and served as doctor to the enslaved people aboard, many of whom died on the way. The ship was forced to dock in Antigua, and he returned to Britain on 22 December.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Second journey, 1805 to 1806</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="50.806,-1.087" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Portsmouth</span> <span class="jr-date">31 January 1805</span><p>Park sailed for the Gambia as a captain, head of a government expedition. His brother-in-law Alexander Anderson was second-in-command.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.667,-17.398" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Gorée</span><p>At Gorée, then in British occupation, Park was joined by Lieutenant Martyn, thirty-five privates and two seamen.</p></li>
<li data-ll="12.646,-7.992" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a></span> <span class="jr-date">mid-August 1805</span><p>The party started late into the rainy season and did not reach the Niger until mid-August. Only eleven Europeans were then alive; the rest had died of fever or dysentery. From Bamako they went on to Ségou by canoe.</p></li>
<li data-ll="13.725,-6.004" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Sansanding</span> <span class="jr-date">19 November 1805</span><p>Mansong Diarra, the local ruler, gave permission to proceed. Anderson died at Sansanding on 28 October. Park turned two canoes into one boat, named it the Joliba, and set sail downstream on 19 November.</p></li>
<li data-ll="11.396,5.486" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Yauri</span><p>According to Amadi Fatouma, the guide who went downstream with Park, canoes chased them at several places and Park's party drove them off with firearms, killing many people. At Yauri Amadi landed.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.317,4.600" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Bussa</span><p>At the Bussa rapids the boat stuck on a rock. Hostile people on the bank attacked with bows and spears, and Park, Martyn and the two remaining soldiers jumped into the river and drowned. One of the slaves survived.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Park died in 1806 at the Bussa rapids, near Bussa in present-day Nigeria, while travelling down the Niger. His journal was never recovered, and the mystery of the Niger's course was solved in 1830 by Richard Lander and his brother.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Mungo Park (explorer)&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="humboldt" data-kids="1">
<h2>Humboldt and Bonpland in the Spanish Americas</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1799–1804 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a> &#183; 19 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Alexander von Humboldt and the French botanist Aimé Bonpland sailed to Spain's American realms in 1799 with authorisation from the Spanish crown and Humboldt's own funds. They travelled through Venezuela, Cuba, the Andes and New Spain, and returned by way of the United States.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Venezuela</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="43.371,-8.396" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">La Coruña</span> <span class="jr-date">5 June 1799</span><p>Humboldt and Bonpland, armed with the King of Spain's authorisation, sailed from La Coruña on the ship Pizarro.</p></li>
<li data-ll="28.269,-16.606" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tenerife</span><p>The Pizarro stopped for six days on Tenerife, and Humboldt climbed the volcano Teide.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.450,-64.167" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cumana">Cumaná</a></span> <span class="jr-date">16 July 1799</span><p>The Pizarro landed at Cumaná. An outbreak of typhoid on board had made the captain change course from Havana to northern South America.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.506,-66.914" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a></span> <span class="jr-date">February 1800</span><p>At the invitation of the captain general, Manuel de Guevara y Vasconcelos, Humboldt stayed in Caracas for about two months. He then began his researches in the valley of Aragua.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.172,-63.505" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Caripe</span><p>Humboldt visited the mission at Caripe and explored the Guácharo cavern, where he found the oilbird, which he made known to science as Steatornis caripensis.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Cuba</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="23.137,-82.359" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a></span> <span class="jr-date">19 December 1800</span><p>They sailed from Venezuela on 24 November 1800 and landed in Cuba on 19 December. In Havana Humboldt surveyed the city and the nearby towns, and met the plant collector John Fraser.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The Andes</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="4.610,-74.082" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Bogotá</span> <span class="jr-date">6 July 1801</span><p>From Cartagena de Indias they went up the swollen Magdalena River to Honda and reached Bogotá. There they met José Celestino Mutis and stayed until 8 September 1801.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-0.220,-78.513" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a></span> <span class="jr-date">6 January 1802</span><p>Humboldt and Bonpland crossed the frozen ridges of the Cordillera Real on a tedious and difficult journey and reached Quito. They hoped to meet the French expedition of Baudin.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-1.469,-78.817" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/chimborazo">Chimborazo</a></span><p>Humboldt, Bonpland, Carlos Montúfar and a number of Indians climbed the extinct volcano Chimborazo to 19,286 feet (5,878 m), a record for a westerner, about 1,000 feet short of the summit.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-12.052,-77.139" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a></span> <span class="jr-date">9 November 1802</span><p>At Callao, the main port for Peru, Humboldt observed the transit of Mercury on 9 November and studied guano, whose later introduction into Europe was due mainly to his writings.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">New Spain</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="11">
<li data-ll="16.863,-99.890" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a></span> <span class="jr-date">15 February 1803</span><p>Unable to join a voyage to the Pacific, they left Guayaquil and landed at Acapulco. Humboldt surveyed the bay to fix its longitude, as the ship's captain had reckoned it wrongly.</p></li>
<li data-ll="18.555,-99.606" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Taxco</span><p>From Acapulco they went to Taxco, a silver-mining town in modern Guerrero. The route was suitable only for mule trains, and Humboldt measured elevations all along it.</p></li>
<li data-ll="18.919,-99.234" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Cuernavaca</span> <span class="jr-date">April 1803</span><p>Humboldt visited Cuernavaca and, impressed by its climate, nicknamed it the City of Eternal Spring.</p></li>
<li data-ll="19.354,-99.136" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a></span><p>Welcomed by a letter from Viceroy José de Iturrigaray, Humboldt received a special passport and access to crown records, mines and estates. He found it the largest city in the Americas.</p></li>
<li data-ll="21.018,-101.257" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a></span><p>Humboldt spent time at the Valenciana silver mine, then the most important in the Spanish empire. He studied the whole mining complex and its output statistics.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The United States and home</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="16">
<li data-ll="19.190,-96.153" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/veracruz-city">Veracruz</a></span><p>Humboldt left New Spain from the port of Veracruz, taking elevation measurements on the road from Mexico City. He reached Cuba on 7 January 1804 and stayed until 29 April 1804.</p></li>
<li data-ll="39.953,-75.164" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a></span><p>Leaving Cuba, Humboldt made an unplanned visit to the United States. In Philadelphia he met Caspar Wistar, Benjamin Smith Barton and Benjamin Rush.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.895,-77.037" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a></span><p>Humboldt held long discussions with President Thomas Jefferson on science and on New Spain, and wrote him a two-page report on where the border of Louisiana lay.</p></li>
<li data-ll="44.838,-0.579" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Bordeaux</span> <span class="jr-date">3 August 1804</span><p>After six weeks in the United States, Humboldt set sail from the mouth of the Delaware and landed at Bordeaux.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Humboldt set sail for Europe from the mouth of the Delaware and landed at Bordeaux on 3 August 1804. He spent the next two decades publishing the results, and the work made his name in scientific circles.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Alexander von Humboldt&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="lewis-and-clark" data-kids="1">
<h2>The Lewis and Clark Expedition</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1804–1806 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/lewis-and-clark-expedition">Meriwether Lewis and William Clark</a> &#183; 16 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">After the Louisiana Purchase, President Thomas Jefferson commissioned the Corps of Discovery under Captain Meriwether Lewis and Second Lieutenant William Clark. It was to find a practical water route across the continent, map the land and establish relations with the Native American nations along the Missouri River.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Up the Missouri</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="38.803,-90.101" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Camp Dubois</span> <span class="jr-date">14 May 1804</span><p>Under Clark's command the Corps set out at 4 pm up the Missouri in a keelboat and two pirogues. It may have had as many as 45 members, among them York, a Black man enslaved by Clark.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.789,-90.512" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">St. Charles</span> <span class="jr-date">21 May 1804</span><p>Lewis joined the party at St. Charles, Missouri, six days after it left Camp Dubois. The expedition set out again the next afternoon, 21 May, and followed the Missouri upriver.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.496,-96.405" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Floyd's Bluff, Sioux City</span> <span class="jr-date">20 August 1804</span><p>Sergeant Charles Floyd died, apparently of acute appendicitis, the only member of the expedition to die. He was buried on a bluff by the river with a cedar post carrying his name and the day of his death.</p></li>
<li data-ll="44.359,-100.376" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">The Bad River, near Fort Pierre</span> <span class="jr-date">25 September 1804</span><p>The expedition met the Lakota, whom it called Sioux, under chiefs including Black Buffalo and the Partisan. The chiefs demanded tribute for passage, warriors seized a boat, and Lewis and Clark made a show of force until Black Buffalo ordered his men back.</p></li>
<li data-ll="47.296,-101.286" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Mandan</span> <span class="jr-date">winter 1804–1805</span><p>The party wintered in the Mandan nation's territory, where it met the trapper Toussaint Charbonneau and his young Shoshone wife Sacagawea. Peace with the Mandan chiefs was made by sharing a ceremonial pipe, and the keelboat went back to St. Louis on 7 April 1805.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Over the mountains to the Pacific</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="47.930,-110.491" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Marias River</span> <span class="jr-date">16 June 1805</span><p>Sacagawea became dangerously ill at the Marias River. She found relief by drinking mineral water from the sulphur spring that fed into it.</p></li>
<li data-ll="44.974,-113.445" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Lemhi Pass</span> <span class="jr-date">1805</span><p>The expedition followed the Missouri to its headwaters and crossed the Continental Divide near Lemhi Pass. Lewis and Clark held a mistaken belief that boats could easily be carried from the Missouri's headwaters to the Columbia.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.635,-114.580" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Lolo Pass</span> <span class="jr-date">1805</span><p>The party went north to Traveler's Rest and crossed the Bitterroot Mountains at Lolo Pass, then came down on foot to the Clearwater River.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.649,-120.978" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Celilo Falls</span> <span class="jr-date">1805</span><p>The party went down the Clearwater, Snake and Columbia rivers in canoes, past Celilo Falls and what is now Portland. The sight of Mount Hood showed that they had almost reached the Pacific.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.250,-123.863" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Dismal Nitch</span> <span class="jr-date">November 1805</span><p>The expedition sighted the Pacific Ocean on 7 November 1805. It then camped in a storm-wracked place Clark called Dismal Nitch, short of food because the elk had retreated and the party had too little to buy from neighbouring peoples.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.134,-123.880" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Clatsop</span> <span class="jr-date">24 November 1805</span><p>The majority voted to move camp to the south side of the Columbia, and Sacagawea and York took part in the vote. The party built Fort Clatsop on the Netul River and spent the winter there, many of the men ill with colds and influenza.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The return</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="12">
<li data-ll="46.134,-123.880" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Clatsop</span> <span class="jr-date">23 March 1806</span><p>The journey home began on 23 March 1806, first by canoe up the Columbia and later overland. Before leaving, Clark gave the Chinook a letter for the next ship captain to call, Captain Hill, which reached Jefferson before the Corps did.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.222,-116.010" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Camp Chopunnish</span> <span class="jr-date">April 1806</span><p>At Camp Chopunnish on the Clearwater the party gathered 65 horses but could not cross the snowbound Bitterroot Mountains. When Native Americans stole Lewis's dog Seaman, he warned the chief that further wrongdoing would mean instant death.</p></li>
<li data-ll="48.467,-112.467" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Marias River</span> <span class="jr-date">3 July 1806</span><p>The Corps split in two so Lewis could explore the Marias. His group of four met Blackfeet men, who tried to steal their weapons in the night, and the soldiers killed two of them. Lewis and three others fled over 100 miles in a day.</p></li>
<li data-ll="47.985,-103.989" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Confluence of the Yellowstone and Missouri</span> <span class="jr-date">11 August 1806</span><p>The two groups met again. Clark had passed through Crow territory, where half his horses vanished in the night. As they reunited, the hunter Pierre Cruzatte mistook Lewis for an elk and shot him in the thigh.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.626,-90.199" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">St. Louis</span> <span class="jr-date">23 September 1806</span><p>The Corps returned quickly down the Missouri and reached St. Louis on 23 September 1806. It then reported its findings to Jefferson through maps, sketches and various journals.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The Corps reached St. Louis on 23 September 1806. It had reached the Pacific but found no continuous waterway to it, and it had relied on the help of about two dozen Native American nations. The expedition also served to assert American sovereignty over lands those nations already inhabited.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Lewis and Clark Expedition&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="napoleon-russia" data-kids="0">
<h2>Napoleon's Grande Armée in Russia</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1812 &#183; <a href="/life/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> &#183; 13 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Napoleon set out to force the Russian Empire to comply with the continental blockade of the United Kingdom. On 24 June 1812 the multinational Grande Armée, of nearly half a million men, crossed the Neman from the Duchy of Warsaw into Russia and advanced towards Moscow.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The advance to Moscow</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="54.900,23.933" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a></span> <span class="jr-date">23 June 1812</span><p>Napoleon arrived near Kaunas on 23 June, and the army crossed the Neman the next day. He spent a night and the following day in Kaunas, admitting only his guards to the city.</p></li>
<li data-ll="54.687,25.280" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a></span> <span class="jr-date">28 June 1812</span><p>Napoleon reached Vilnius with only light skirmishing, leaving more than 5,000 dead horses behind him. For lack of horses he had to abandon up to 100 guns and up to 500 artillery wagons.</p></li>
<li data-ll="55.183,30.167" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Vitebsk</span><p>After the battle of Vitebsk the Russians slipped away during the night. The city and the palace of Alexander of Württemberg became Napoleon's base for the next two weeks.</p></li>
<li data-ll="54.783,32.045" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a></span> <span class="jr-date">16 to 18 August 1812</span><p>Napoleon attacked the Smolensk Kremlin in the first real confrontation of significant forces. Barclay de Tolly withdrew his troops from the burning city in the middle of the night, and the Russians left to the east.</p></li>
<li data-ll="55.517,35.817" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Borodino</span> <span class="jr-date">7 September 1812</span><p>The largest battle of the invasion was fought near Borodino. The Grande Armée captured the main positions but failed to destroy the Russian army, and about a third of Napoleon's soldiers were killed or wounded.</p></li>
<li data-ll="55.751,37.617" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a></span> <span class="jr-date">14 September 1812</span><p>Napoleon moved into Moscow with his roughly 100,000 men and received no delegation from the city. Within 48 hours three quarters of Moscow, which had about 270,000 people before the evacuation order, was reduced to ashes by arson.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The retreat</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="55.751,37.617" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a></span> <span class="jr-date">19 October 1812</span><p>After five weeks of occupation the French Army left Moscow, still 108,000 men but with its cavalry nearly destroyed. Napoleon first took the Old Kaluga road, hoping to avoid the path his army had devastated on the way east.</p></li>
<li data-ll="55.000,36.467" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Maloyaroslavets</span><p>Napoleon lost the battle at Maloyaroslavets and had to return to the Smolensk road he had travelled eastward. Cossack bands under Platov, Orlov-Denisov and Davydov then attacked isolated French foraging units.</p></li>
<li data-ll="55.210,34.285" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/vyazma">Vyazma</a></span><p>Elements of the Grande Armée were defeated at Vyazma. Starvation and disease plagued the troops, and nearly all the surviving horses died of hunger or were eaten by starving soldiers.</p></li>
<li data-ll="54.560,31.430" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Krasny</span><p>Short of cavalry and artillery in snow and ice, Napoleon sent the Old Guard against Miloradovich, who blocked the road to Krasny. Davout broke through, while Eugene de Beauharnais and Ney had to take a detour.</p></li>
<li data-ll="54.224,28.512" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Borisov</span><p>The French corps went on to Borisov, where the Russian army had destroyed the bridge over the Berezina. Two Russian armies inflicted heavy casualties on the remnants of the Grande Armée during the crossing.</p></li>
<li data-ll="54.484,26.400" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Smorgonie</span> <span class="jr-date">5 December 1812</span><p>Napoleon left the army at Smorgonie and returned to Paris by sled, leaving the sick Murat in command. Within a few days a further 20,000 people died of the cold and of diseases carried by lice.</p></li>
<li data-ll="54.687,25.280" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a></span><p>Murat and Ney took command and pressed on, leaving over 20,000 men in the hospitals of Vilnius. The remnants of the principal armies then crossed the frozen Neman and the Bug.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The army left Russian territory on 14 December 1812. Figures vary: Richard Riehn gives 685,000 men marching in and fewer than 70,000 known survivors, while Adam Zamoyski estimated that as many as 400,000 of 550,000 to 600,000 died, counting prisoners who died in captivity.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;French invasion of Russia&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="caillie" data-kids="1">
<h2>René Caillié to Djenné, Timbuktu and Morocco</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1827–1828 &#183; René Caillié &#183; 9 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">René Caillié, a Frenchman who had spent months among the Brakna Moors learning Arabic and the customs of Islam, set out alone dressed as a Muslim. He wished to reach Timbuktu and describe it for a prize of 9,000 francs offered by the Société de Géographie in Paris.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From the Rio Nuñez to Djenné</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="10.931,-14.289" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Kakondy</span> <span class="jr-date">19 April 1827</span><p>Caillié had come by ship from Sierra Leone to Boké on the Rio Nuñez. From Kakondy, near Boké, he set off east along the hills of Fouta Djallon.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.650,-9.883" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Kouroussa</span><p>Beyond the head streams of the Senegal River, Caillié crossed the Upper Niger at Kurussa, now Kouroussa.</p></li>
<li data-ll="10.383,-9.300" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Kankan</span> <span class="jr-date">17 June 1827</span><p>He arrived with a caravan carrying kola nuts and stayed a month in this commercial centre. Warned against going north because Kankan was fighting for the Bouré gold area, he left eastwards.</p></li>
<li data-ll="9.533,-7.333" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tiémé</span> <span class="jr-date">3 August 1827</span><p>In the Kong highlands Caillié was held at the village of Tiémé by illness for five months. He resumed his journey in January 1828 and went north-east.</p></li>
<li data-ll="13.900,-4.550" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a></span> <span class="jr-date">11 March 1828</span><p>Caillié stayed in Djenné from 11 to 23 March. He confused the Bani River with the Niger, which he called the Dhioliba, and left for Timbuktu on a boat carrying merchandise.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Timbuktu and across the Sahara</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="16.773,-3.007" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">20 April 1828</span><p>Caillié arrived by river and stayed about two weeks. He found the city a mass of earth houses, smaller and less busy than he had expected, and less commercial than Djenné.</p></li>
<li data-ll="18.905,-3.528" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Araouane</span> <span class="jr-date">19 May 1828</span><p>Caillié left Timbuktu on 4 May with a caravan of 600 camels. After six days it reached Araouane, a village that served the trans-Saharan trade. On 19 May it left with 1,400 camels and 400 men.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.043,-5.003" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fez</span> <span class="jr-date">12 August 1828</span><p>The caravan carried slaves, gold, ivory, gum, ostrich feathers and cloth across the Sahara. Caillié reached Fez on 12 August.</p></li>
<li data-ll="35.769,-5.804" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a></span><p>From Tangier, Caillié returned to France by frigate, landing at Toulon. Back in France he was awarded the prize of 9,000 francs by the Société de Géographie.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Caillié was the first European to return alive from Timbuktu. The Société de Géographie awarded him the prize, and in 1830 its gold medal.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;René Caillié&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="beagle" data-kids="1">
<h2>The Second Voyage of HMS Beagle</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1831–1836 &#183; <a href="/life/charles-darwin">Robert FitzRoy and Charles Darwin</a> &#183; 24 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">HMS Beagle left Plymouth on 27 December 1831 on a survey expedition under Robert FitzRoy, who had sought a naturalist to investigate geology. Charles Darwin, aged 22, accepted the place. The ship surveyed the coasts of southern South America and returned by Tahiti and Australia, circling the Earth.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Across the Atlantic to the Río de la Plata</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="50.343,-4.143" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/plymouth-sound">Plymouth Sound</a></span> <span class="jr-date">27 December 1831</span><p>After delays from westerly gales, Beagle left its anchorage in the Barn Pool on the morning of 27 December 1831 and set out on its surveying expedition.</p></li>
<li data-ll="28.269,-16.606" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tenerife</span> <span class="jr-date">6 January 1832</span><p>Beagle reached Tenerife in the Canary Islands but was quarantined because of cholera in England. Darwin was denied a landing, to his intense disappointment, and the ship sailed on.</p></li>
<li data-ll="14.918,-23.509" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/praia">Praia, Santiago</a></span> <span class="jr-date">January 1832</span><p>Beagle made its first landing at Praia on Santiago in the Cape Verde Islands. FitzRoy set up an observatory on Quail Island, and Darwin studied its geology and first thought of writing a book on the subject.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-12.983,-38.493" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Bahia (Salvador)</span> <span class="jr-date">28 February 1832</span><p>Beagle reached the port of Bahia in Brazil to rate the chronometers and take on water. Darwin found slavery offensive, and when he doubted FitzRoy's account of slaves' answers to their master, FitzRoy banned him from his company, then apologised within hours.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-22.911,-43.206" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a></span> <span class="jr-date">4 April 1832</span><p>Beagle entered Rio de Janeiro harbour, where FitzRoy took longitude observations from Villegagnon Island. Darwin took lodgings at Botafogo and set off with local estate owners on a riding excursion to Rio Macaé.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-38.880,-62.075" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Punta Alta</span> <span class="jr-date">22 September 1832</span><p>During a cruise round Bahía Blanca, Darwin and FitzRoy stopped at Punta Alta. In the low cliffs Darwin found fossilised teeth and bones of gigantic extinct mammals and collected them over several days.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Tierra del Fuego and Patagonia</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="-54.000,-70.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tierra-del-fuego">Tierra del Fuego</a></span> <span class="jr-date">18 December 1832</span><p>Beagle reached Tierra del Fuego with the three Fuegians taken to England on the earlier voyage. Darwin met the Yaghan people for the first time and judged them harshly, in terms he later used in The Descent of Man.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-49.307,-67.730" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Port St Julian</span> <span class="jr-date">9 January 1834</span><p>Exploring the geology of the cliffs near the harbour, Darwin found pieces of a spine and a hind leg. He took them for a large animal such as a mastodon; Richard Owen later named the creature Macrauchenia.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-54.000,-70.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tierra-del-fuego">Tierra del Fuego, the mission site</a></span> <span class="jr-date">5 March 1834</span><p>Beagle returned to visit the missionaries and found their huts deserted. Jemmy Button, one of the three Fuegians, came out in a canoe, having lost his possessions and settled into the native ways with a wife.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-50.133,-68.350" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Santa Cruz River</span> <span class="jr-date">19 April 1834</span><p>An expedition including FitzRoy and Darwin took boats up the Santa Cruz river, with everyone taking turns hauling them upstream. Darwin read the terraces of shingle as old shores that had gradually risen. They turned back near the Andes.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The west coast of South America</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="11">
<li data-ll="-42.600,-73.950" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé Island</a></span> <span class="jr-date">28 June 1834</span><p>Beagle and Adventure reached Chiloé in the wet, heavily wooded archipelago and then spent six months surveying the coast and islands southwards. Darwin found fragments of black lignite and petrified wood there.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.046,-71.620" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a></span> <span class="jr-date">23 July 1834</span><p>Beagle arrived at Valparaíso. Darwin set off up the volcanic Andes on 14 August with a companion, then fell ill on his way back from Santiago and spent a month in bed.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-39.814,-73.246" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/valdivia">Valdivia</a></span> <span class="jr-date">8 February 1835</span><p>Twelve days after arriving, Darwin was on shore when a severe earthquake struck. He returned to find the port town badly damaged.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-36.827,-73.050" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/concepcion-chile">Concepción</a></span> <span class="jr-date">4 March 1835</span><p>Beagle found Concepción devastated by the same earthquake and a tsunami. FitzRoy found mussel beds now above high tide, evidence that the ground had risen about 9 feet.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-12.060,-77.037" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a></span> <span class="jr-date">9 September 1835</span><p>Beagle sailed to Lima, where an armed insurrection kept Darwin with the ship. While writing up his notes he reached the idea that coral atolls formed as volcanic islands sank. FitzRoy joined them on 9 September.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Across the Pacific and home</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="16">
<li data-ll="-0.800,-89.400" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Chatham Island, Galápagos</span> <span class="jr-date">16 September 1835</span><p>Beagle anchored at Chatham Island in the Galápagos. Darwin spent his first hour ashore at what is now called Frigatebird Hill and began to collect plants, reptiles, birds and insects across the islands.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.667,-149.467" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tahiti</span> <span class="jr-date">15 November 1835</span><p>Beagle reached Tahiti, where Darwin found the vegetation luxuriant and the people pleasant and intelligent. He thought they showed the benefits of Christianity and doubted what he had read about tyrannical missionaries.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.868,151.210" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/sydney">Sydney</a></span> <span class="jr-date">12 January 1836</span><p>Darwin first sighted Australia on 12 January 1836 and admired Sydney. Inland he met Aboriginal people who gave a spear-throwing display for a shilling, and he reflected sadly that their numbers were falling.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-42.883,147.317" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1836</span><p>At Hobart, Darwin noted that the Aboriginal people of Tasmania had been removed and kept in effect as prisoners on a promontory. He wrote that the settlers' misconduct had first led to this step.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-12.117,96.895" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Keeling Islands</span> <span class="jr-date">1 April 1836</span><p>The Admiralty had asked for a survey of a coral atoll. On arrival the whole crew set to work, and FitzRoy's soundings supported Darwin's theory that encircling reefs become atolls as an island sinks.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.925,18.424" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a></span> <span class="jr-date">31 May 1836</span><p>At the Cape, Darwin learned that extracts from his letters to Henslow had been printed as a pamphlet. Around 15 June he and FitzRoy visited the astronomer Sir John Herschel.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-15.965,-5.715" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">St Helena</a></span> <span class="jr-date">8 July 1836</span><p>Beagle stopped on St Helena for six days. Darwin lodged near Napoleon's tomb, studied the island's strata and volcanic dykes, and noted that goats and hogs introduced in 1502 had destroyed much of the woodland.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-7.950,-14.350" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Ascension Island</span> <span class="jr-date">19 July 1836</span><p>At Ascension Darwin received letters from his sisters telling him of Sedgwick's praise for the collection he had sent home. He also climbed over the volcanic rocks with his geological hammer.</p></li>
<li data-ll="50.150,-5.067" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Falmouth</span> <span class="jr-date">2 October 1836</span><p>After a stormy passage with a stop at the Azores, Beagle anchored at Falmouth. Darwin left that night by the Royal Mail coach and reached his family home at Shrewsbury late on 4 October.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Beagle reached Falmouth on 2 October 1836. The voyage had been offered to Darwin as a two-year trip and lasted almost five years. Darwin spent most of it exploring on land, and his journal later made him widely known as a writer.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Second voyage of HMS Beagle&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="trail-of-tears" data-kids="0">
<h2>The Trail of Tears: The Removal of the Choctaw, Chickasaw and Cherokee</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1831–1839 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/trail-of-tears">The Choctaw, Chickasaw and Cherokee nations</a> &#183; 10 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The United States government, with state militias, forcibly removed the Cherokee from their homeland in the south-eastern United States to Indian Territory, in modern Oklahoma, after the Treaty of New Echota of 1835. Principal Chief John Ross did not sign that treaty and most Cherokee opposed it. The Choctaw and Chickasaw removals are given here as shorter legs; the Muscogee (Creek) and Seminole removals were forced west as well, but the route of neither is told stop by stop.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The Choctaw removal, 1831</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="35.146,-90.052" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Memphis</span> <span class="jr-date">1831</span><p>The first Choctaw groups met here and at Vicksburg, where a harsh winter brought flash floods, sleet and snow. Alexis de Tocqueville witnessed the removal at Memphis in 1831 and described the Choctaw as tranquil but sombre.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.017,-91.345" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Arkansas Post</span><p>The Memphis group travelled about 60 miles up the Arkansas to this place. It stayed below freezing for almost a week and ice clogged the rivers, so they could not travel for weeks. Daily rations were a handful of boiled corn, one turnip and two cups of heated water.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.744,-92.288" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Little Rock</span><p>Forty government wagons carried the Choctaw from Arkansas Post to Little Rock. On reaching it, a Choctaw chief called their trek a trail of tears and death. Nearly 17,000 Choctaws made the move, and about 2,500 to 6,000 died.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The Chickasaw removal, 1837</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="35.146,-90.052" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Memphis</span> <span class="jr-date">4 July 1837</span><p>The first Chickasaw group, led by John M. Millard, gathered here with its belongings, livestock and slaves. It crossed the Mississippi and followed routes the Choctaw and Creek had used. In Indian Territory the Chickasaw merged with the Choctaw nation.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The Cherokee removal, 1838</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="35.171,-84.871" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Cleveland, Tennessee</span><p>Martin Van Buren allowed an armed force of 7,000 militiamen, army regulars and volunteers under General Winfield Scott to relocate about 13,000 Cherokees to Cleveland, Tennessee. The Cherokee were held in camps in eastern Tennessee, and their former lands were immediately opened to settlement.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.993,-84.947" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Red Clay</span> <span class="jr-date">winter 1838</span><p>The march of 1,000 miles began at Red Clay, the last Eastern capital of the Cherokee Nation. Most walked with scant clothing and without shoes or moccasins. Because of disease they were not allowed into towns or villages and often had to go far around them.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.353,-88.428" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Mantle Rock</span><p>After crossing Tennessee and Kentucky, the Cherokee were forced to shelter under this bluff on the Kentucky side of the Ohio River while they waited to cross. Many died huddled together there.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.362,-88.487" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Golconda</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 3 December 1838</span><p>The starving Cherokee reached the Ohio River across from Golconda about 3 December 1838. Berry's Ferry charged them a dollar a head, against its usual twelve cents, and they were not allowed to cross until it had served everyone else.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.416,-88.894" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Vienna</span><p>Several Cherokee were murdered by locals. The Cherokee filed a lawsuit through the courthouse in Vienna, suing the government for $35 a head to bury them. Crossing the 60 miles to the Mississippi took almost three months, and most Cherokee deaths came in southern Illinois.</p></li>
<li data-ll="35.913,-94.971" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tahlequah">Tahlequah</a></span><p>The removed Cherokee initially settled near Tahlequah in Indian Territory. Major Ridge had said on signing the Treaty of New Echota that he had signed his death warrant, and Major Ridge, John Ridge and Elias Boudinot were killed.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The removed Cherokee initially settled near Tahlequah. About 4,000 Cherokee died on the trek to Oklahoma, and most deaths were caused by disease, malnutrition and exposure. Some Cherokee evaded removal, and the Eastern Band of the Cherokee Nation descends from those who stayed in North Carolina.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Trail of Tears&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="great-trek" data-kids="0">
<h2>The Great Trek of the Voortrekkers</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1834–1838 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/great-trek">The Voortrekkers</a> &#183; 9 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">From 1836 onwards Dutch-speaking settlers left the Cape Colony by wagon train for the interior of southern Africa, seeking to live beyond British colonial administration. Their reasons included British opposition to their ownership of slaves, the abolition of slavery in 1834, and a wish for more pasture to the east.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The exploring party to Natal</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="-33.300,26.533" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Grahamstown</span> <span class="jr-date">8 September 1834</span><p>A Kommissietrek of 40 men and one woman, with a retinue of coloured servants and 14 wagons, set off for Natal to judge it as a homeland for Boers discontented with British rule. Petrus Lafras Uys led it.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-29.858,31.025" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/durban">Port Natal</a></span> <span class="jr-date">February 1835</span><p>The party reached the bay after about 650 kilometres, welcomed by the few British hunters and ivory traders there. The Boers made a laager in the area of the present Greyville Racecourse, and Dick King, who spoke Zulu, was sent to King Dingane at uMgungundlovu.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.300,26.533" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Grahamstown</span> <span class="jr-date">October 1835</span><p>The party left Port Natal in early June with a stash of ivory and returned by much the same route. Jan Bantjes drew up the report on Natal, which inspired Boers at the Cape to set the Great Trek in motion.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">North across the Vaal</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="-23.865,35.383" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/inhambane">Inhambane</a></span> <span class="jr-date">late July 1836</span><p>Hans van Rensburg's party, which had left the Cape in September 1835 and parted from Louis Tregardt's, was massacred here by an impi of Manukosi. Only two children, saved by a Zulu warrior, survived of its 49.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-26.000,32.750" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a></span><p>Those of Tregardt's party who had settled around Soutpansberg moved on to Delagoa Bay. Most of them, Tregardt among them, died of fever.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-27.477,27.914" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Vegkop</span> <span class="jr-date">20 October 1836</span><p>Hendrik Potgieter's party was attacked by an army of 4,600 Ndebele warriors. Thirty-five armed trekkers held their laager at the cost of two men and almost all their cattle.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Into Natal and the war with the Zulu</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="-28.436,31.268" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">uMgungundlovu</span> <span class="jr-date">October 1837</span><p>Piet Retief met King Dingane to negotiate a treaty for land in present-day KwaZulu-Natal. Dingane, suspicious because of earlier Voortrekker influxes from across the Drakensberg, had Retief and seventy of his followers killed.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-28.854,30.073" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Weenen</span><p>After Retief's party was killed, a Zulu army of 7,000 attacked Voortrekker camps in the Drakensberg foothills. In the Weenen massacre 532 people died, among them 282 Voortrekkers, of whom 185 were children, and 250 Khoikhoi and Basuto.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-28.105,30.542" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Blood River</span> <span class="jr-date">16 December 1838</span><p>Andries Pretorius had arrived in November to help in the defence. The trekkers fought Zulu impis here and won. Dingane's half-brother Mpande then allied with them to overthrow the king, and the Voortrekkers proclaimed the Natalia Republic.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The trek led to the founding of the South African Republic, the Orange Free State and the Natalia Republic. It also displaced the Northern Ndebele and led to conflicts with the Zulu that contributed to the decline and collapse of the Zulu Kingdom.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Great Trek&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="oregon-trail" data-kids="1">
<h2>The Oregon Trail</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1843 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/oregon-trail">The emigrants of the Great Migration of 1843</a> &#183; 15 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The Oregon Trail was a wagon route of 2,170 miles (3,490 km) from the Missouri River to valleys in Oregon Territory. In the Great Migration of 1843, an estimated 700 to 1,000 emigrants set out for Oregon, led at first by John Gantt, and later by Marcus Whitman.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From the Missouri to South Pass</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="39.093,-94.414" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Independence</span><p>Independence was the main jumping off point, the common head of the Santa Fe Trail and the Oregon Trail. Travellers had to ferry across the Missouri River, then followed the Santa Fe Trail to near present-day Topeka, Kansas.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.650,-99.000" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Kearny</span><p>The army fort, established in 1848, lay about 200 miles (320 km) from the Missouri River. Nearly all the trail's offshoots converged near it along the Platte, and it was the first place to buy supplies, make repairs, get medical aid or mail a letter.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.704,-103.348" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Chimney Rock</span><p>Travellers on the Platte passed landmarks in Nebraska including Courthouse and Jail Rocks, Chimney Rock and Scotts Bluff. Until about 1870 hundreds of thousands of bison migrated through Nebraska, and most travellers killed several for fresh meat.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.203,-104.546" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Laramie</span><p>Fort Laramie was a former fur trading outpost, first named Fort John, bought by the U.S. Army in 1848 to protect travellers on the trails. Most cholera outbreaks on the Platte ended here.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.494,-107.133" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Independence Rock</span><p>Where the North Platte veered south, the trail crossed it to the Sweetwater River valley, which heads almost due west. Independence Rock stands on the Sweetwater, which had to be crossed up to nine times.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.370,-108.914" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">South Pass</span><p>The trail crossed the Continental Divide at South Pass. It then ran southwest over Big Sandy Creek to the Green River, a dangerous crossing at high water in July and August, where three to five ferries worked at peak times.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Fort Bridger to Fort Boise</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="41.318,-110.392" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Bridger</span><p>Beyond the Green River the main trail met the Blacks Fork and Fort Bridger. In 1847 Brigham Young and the Mormon pioneers left the trail here for Utah, while the main trail veered northwest to the Bear River valley.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.658,-111.596" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Soda Springs</span><p>The trail followed the Bear River northwest to Soda Springs, a favourite attraction of the pioneers, who marvelled at the hot carbonated water. West of it the main trail turned northwest along the Portneuf River valley.</p></li>
<li data-ll="43.020,-112.635" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Hall</span><p>Fort Hall was an old fur trading post on the Snake River, founded by Nathaniel Jarvis Wyeth and company and sold in 1837 to the Hudson's Bay Company. Nearly all travellers were given some aid and supplies there if available.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.941,-115.318" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Three Island Crossing</span><p>Near present-day Glenns Ferry, most emigrants crossed the swift Snake River here by ferry, or by driving or floating wagons and swimming teams. Hidden holes in the bed made it treacherous, and before ferries there were several drownings nearly every year.</p></li>
<li data-ll="43.824,-117.020" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Boise</span><p>The north side of the Snake had better water and grass, and the Boise River valley was a welcome relief. The last Snake crossing, near Old Fort Boise, was made on bull boats, and young Native American boys were often hired to drive and ride the stock across.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From the Blue Mountains to Oregon City</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="12">
<li data-ll="45.327,-118.093" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">La Grande valley</span><p>Across what became Oregon the trail passed the Malheur River, Farewell Bend and the Burnt River canyon to the La Grande valley, below the Blue Mountains. In 1843 settlers cut a wagon road over them, passable to wagons for the first time.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.040,-118.465" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Whitman Mission</span><p>The trail went to the Whitman Mission near Fort Nez Perce in Washington. There some travellers built rafts or hired boats for the Columbia, while others went on by wagon. After 1847 the trail bypassed the closed mission.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.601,-121.183" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">The Dalles</span><p>At the Columbia, stopped by the Cascade Mountains and Mount Hood, some gave up their wagons or floated them downriver on boats or rafts. Livestock were driven around Mount Hood, and the Barlow Road, a toll road, was cut from here in 1846.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.357,-122.607" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/oregon-city-oregon">Oregon City</a></span><p>Travellers on the Columbia made a portage of 1.6 miles (2.6 km) around the Cascade Rapids before reaching the Willamette River near Oregon City, the trail's nominal end. Many settlers branched off or stopped short of it.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Nearly all the settlers of the 1843 wagon trains reached the Willamette Valley by early October, and a passable wagon trail now existed from the Missouri River to The Dalles. The Barlow Road, completed in 1846, made the whole way passable for wagons.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Oregon Trail&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="franklin" data-kids="0">
<h2>Franklin's Lost Expedition</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1845–1848 &#183; Sir John Franklin &#183; 12 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">HMS Erebus and HMS Terror left England in 1845 under Captain Sir John Franklin, with 129 officers and men, to traverse the last unnavigated sections of the Northwest Passage in the Canadian Arctic. Both ships became icebound in Victoria Strait near King William Island.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Out from England</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="51.450,0.282" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Greenhithe</span> <span class="jr-date">19 May 1845</span><p>The expedition sailed from Greenhithe, Kent, on the morning of 19 May 1845, with a crew of 24 officers and 110 men aboard Erebus and Terror.</p></li>
<li data-ll="58.961,-3.299" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Stromness</span><p>The ships stopped briefly at Stromness in the Orkney Islands to take on fresh water. From there they sailed to Greenland with HMS Rattler and the transport Barretto Junior, a passage of 30 days.</p></li>
<li data-ll="69.000,-52.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Whalefish Islands</span><p>At the Whalefish Islands in Disko Bay ten oxen carried on Barretto Junior were slaughtered for fresh meat, and the crews wrote their last letters home. Five men were sent home sick, which left 129 men.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Into the ice and the march south</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="74.717,-91.850" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Beechey Island</span> <span class="jr-date">winter 1845–46</span><p>Franklin's men spent the winter of 1845–46 on Beechey Island. Three crew members died there and were buried.</p></li>
<li data-ll="69.167,-97.417" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">King William Island</span> <span class="jr-date">September 1846</span><p>After travelling down Peel Sound through the summer, Terror and Erebus became trapped in ice off King William Island. They are thought never to have sailed again. Franklin died on 11 June 1847, according to the Victory Point Note.</p></li>
<li data-ll="69.167,-97.417" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Victory Point</span> <span class="jr-date">25 April 1848</span><p>The ships were deserted on 22 April 1848. Crozier and Fitzjames recorded that 105 men had landed, that 9 officers and 15 men had died, and that they would start for the Back River on 26 April.</p></li>
<li data-ll="68.100,-97.800" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Starvation Cove</span><p>From archaeological finds it is believed that all the remaining crew died on the 400 km march to the Back River, most on the island. Thirty or forty men reached the northern coast of the mainland before dying.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">What searchers found</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="8">
<li data-ll="68.533,-89.827" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Pelly Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">21 April 1854</span><p>John Rae met an Inuk near Pelly Bay, who told him of 35 to 40 white men who had died of starvation near the mouth of the Back River. Other Inuit confirmed this, with reports of cannibalism among the dying sailors.</p></li>
<li data-ll="69.167,-97.417" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Victory Point</span> <span class="jr-date">5 May 1859</span><p>Lieutenant William Hobson's sled party found the Victory Point Note in a cairn on the north-western coast of King William Island. It told of the abandonment of the ships, Franklin's death and the march south.</p></li>
<li data-ll="69.167,-97.417" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">NgLj-2, western shore of King William Island</span> <span class="jr-date">1993</span><p>Archaeologists excavated nearly 400 bones and fragments on the island's western shore. Many bore cut marks consistent with de-fleshing, and it is now generally accepted that some of Franklin's men resorted to cannibalism.</p></li>
<li data-ll="68.033,-99.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">O'Reilly Island</span> <span class="jr-date">7 September 2014</span><p>A Canadian search team located Erebus in about 11 m of water in Wilmot and Crampton Bay, west of O'Reilly Island. The ship was preserved in good condition.</p></li>
<li data-ll="68.900,-98.950" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Terror Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">12 September 2016</span><p>The Arctic Research Foundation announced that it had found the wreck of Terror in Terror Bay, south of King William Island, at a depth of 24 m.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The ships were abandoned in April 1848 and the survivors, led by Francis Crozier, set out for the Canadian mainland and disappeared. Later searchers, Inuit testimony and the finding of the wrecks in 2014 and 2016 told what became of them.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Franklin's lost expedition&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="mormon-trail" data-kids="1">
<h2>The Mormon Pioneers' Trek to the Salt Lake Valley</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1846–1847 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/brigham-young">Brigham Young and the Mormon pioneers</a> &#183; 7 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Members of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints left Nauvoo, Illinois, in 1846, after the death of their leader Joseph Smith made it clear the group could not remain there. In 1847 a vanguard company led by Brigham Young broke the trail west from Winter Quarters to the Great Basin.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Nauvoo to Winter Quarters</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="40.550,-91.385" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Nauvoo</span> <span class="jr-date">4 February 1846</span><p>The exodus from Nauvoo began on this day, after the killing of Joseph Smith in 1844 made it clear the Latter-day Saints could not stay. Advance parties were sent out by church leaders in March 1846.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.338,-95.961" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Winter Quarters</span> <span class="jr-date">5 April 1847</span><p>Leaders planned the migration here during the winter of 1846–1847. On 5 April 1847 a vanguard of 143 men, three women and two children set out west with 73 wagons.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Across the plains and the Rockies</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="3">
<li data-ll="42.203,-104.546" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Laramie</span> <span class="jr-date">1 June 1847</span><p>The first segment took six weeks. The company halted to repair wagons and reshoe the animals, and was joined by sick members of the Mormon Battalion and by church members from Mississippi.</p></li>
<li data-ll="42.370,-108.914" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">South Pass</span><p>The company took the established Oregon Trail and pushed through South Pass. Brigham Young had met the mountain man Jim Bridger on 28 June and discussed routes into the Salt Lake Valley.</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.318,-110.392" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Bridger</span> <span class="jr-date">7 July 1847</span><p>Another thirteen sick members of the Mormon Battalion joined the company here. Young chose to follow the trail the Donner–Reed party had used the year before, and he fell ill with mountain fever.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The Salt Lake Valley</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="40.684,-111.978" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/salt-lake-valley">Salt Lake Valley</a></span> <span class="jr-date">21 July 1847</span><p>The scouts Erastus Snow and Orson Pratt entered the valley, and the first camp was set up the next day. On 24 July Young, ill in a wagon, is reported to have said &quot;This is the right place, drive on.&quot;</p></li>
<li data-ll="41.338,-95.961" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Winter Quarters</span> <span class="jr-date">August 1847</span><p>Young and other chosen members of the vanguard company went back east to gather and organise the companies due in the following years.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The first company reached the Salt Lake Valley in July 1847, then part of Mexico, where the Shoshone, Utes and Goshutes held sovereignty. By December 1847 more than two thousand Mormons had completed the journey, and migration continued in organised companies until 1869.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Mormon pioneers&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="livingstone" data-kids="0">
<h2>David Livingstone Across Africa</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1852–1873 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> &#183; 13 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">David Livingstone, a Scottish doctor and missionary with the London Missionary Society, travelled north from Linyanti with Kololo men lent by their chief Sekeletu to look for a trade route to the coast. He crossed south-central Africa from the Atlantic to the Indian Ocean, then returned for a Zambezi expedition and a last search for the sources of the Nile.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Linyanti to the Atlantic and the Indian Ocean</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="-8.815,13.230" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a></span> <span class="jr-date">May 1854</span><p>The party reached the Portuguese city of Luanda on the Atlantic after great difficulties, and Livingstone nearly died of fever on the way. He judged the route too hard for future traders.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.925,25.858" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Mosi-oa-Tunya (Victoria Falls)</span><p>Going east down the Zambezi, Livingstone became the first European to see the waterfall called Mosi-oa-Tunya, &quot;the smoke that thunders&quot;. He named it Victoria Falls after Queen Victoria.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.876,36.887" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/quelimane">Quelimane</a></span><p>Livingstone reached Quelimane on the Indian Ocean, having mapped most of the course of the Zambezi. He was hailed as the first European to cross south-central Africa at that latitude.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The Zambezi expedition</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="8.487,-13.236" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a></span> <span class="jr-date">10 March 1858</span><p>The expedition left Britain on the steamer Pearl with the prefabricated river steamer Ma Robert. At Freetown they took on 12 Kru seafarers to man the river steamer.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-18.039,35.611" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Shupanga</span><p>The Zambezi proved impassable to boats past the Cahora Bassa rapids. The experts were stuck at Shupanga and quarrelled, and the artist Thomas Baines was dismissed from the expedition.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The last journeys</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="-6.165,39.199" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Zanzibar</span> <span class="jr-date">January 1866</span><p>Livingstone returned to Africa at Zanzibar, where the Sultan provided a house for his use while he prepared an expedition to seek the source of the Nile.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-12.183,34.367" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a></span> <span class="jr-date">6 August 1866</span><p>His assistants had gradually deserted him. He reached Lake Malawi after most of his supplies, including all his medicines, had been stolen.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-9.167,28.500" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lake-mweru">Lake Mweru</a></span> <span class="jr-date">8 November 1867</span><p>Livingstone, forced by ill health to travel with slave traders, arrived at Lake Mweru. He went on south to become the first European to see Lake Bangweulu.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-4.217,26.183" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyangwe">Nyangwe</a></span> <span class="jr-date">15 July 1871</span><p>At the Nyangwe market on the Lualaba River, Livingstone watched Arab slavers massacre around 400 Africans, standing beside the trader Dugumbe. The killing horrified him and left him too shattered to continue his search.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-4.917,29.683" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/ujiji">Ujiji</a></span> <span class="jr-date">23 October 1871</span><p>Livingstone travelled 240 miles (390 km) back from Nyangwe to Ujiji, an Arab settlement on the eastern shore of Lake Tanganyika, violently ill most of the way.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-4.917,29.683" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/ujiji">Ujiji</a></span> <span class="jr-date">10 November 1871</span><p>Henry Morton Stanley, sent by the New York Herald, found Livingstone at Ujiji. Despite Stanley's urgings, Livingstone would not leave Africa until his mission was complete.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-12.300,30.317" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Chitambo's village, Chipundu</span> <span class="jr-date">1 May 1873</span><p>Livingstone died in Chief Chitambo's village from malaria and internal bleeding due to dysentery. Chuma and Susi removed his heart and buried it under a tree near where he died.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-6.433,38.900" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Bagamoyo</span><p>Chuma and Susi's expedition carried his remains, his last journal and his belongings over more than 1,000 miles to the coast. Seventy-nine followers completed the journey, and his remains went by ship to Britain.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Livingstone died on 1 May 1873 in Chief Chitambo's village at Chipundu. His attendants Chuma and Susi buried his heart there and carried his body and papers to Bagamoyo on the coast, from where it went by ship to Britain.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;David Livingstone&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="speke" data-kids="1">
<h2>Speke's Journeys in Search of the Source of the Nile</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1857–1863 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke">John Hanning Speke</a> &#183; 10 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">John Hanning Speke, an English army officer, went to East Africa with Richard Burton to find the Great Lakes rumoured to lie in the centre of the continent, in the hope of locating the source of the Nile. He later returned with James Augustus Grant, and most of the day to day work was done by a large labour force of porters, soldiers, guides and cooks.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">With Burton to Lake Tanganyika and Lake Victoria, 1857 to 1859</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="-5.900,39.300" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Zanzibar</span> <span class="jr-date">June 1857</span><p>Speke and Burton began their inland journey from Zanzibar Island in June 1857. They stayed at the residence of Atkins Hamerton, the British consul.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-5.017,32.800" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Kazeh</a></span> <span class="jr-date">7 November 1857</span><p>By 7 November 1857 they had travelled over 600 miles on foot and donkey and reached Kazeh, a settlement of Arab slave traders. There they rested; Burton became gravely ill and Speke went temporarily blind as they went on west.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-4.917,29.683" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/ujiji">Ujiji</a></span> <span class="jr-date">February 1858</span><p>The two arrived at Ujiji in February 1858 and became the first Europeans to reach Lake Tanganyika, though Speke was partly blind and could not properly see it. Burton was too ill to explore the lake, so Speke crossed it with a small crew and canoes.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-5.017,32.800" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Kazeh</a></span> <span class="jr-date">May 1858</span><p>The caravan halted again at Kazeh on the way back. Burton was too weak to go on to a second lake, so he stayed in base camp while Speke made a 47-day side trip with 34 men, Bombay and Mabruki as his captains.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-5.017,32.800" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Kazeh</a></span> <span class="jr-date">26 September 1858</span><p>After Speke's side trip, which reached Lake Victoria on 30 July 1858, the return journey to the coast began from Kazeh on 26 September 1858 with 152 porters. Both men were often ill and some of the way were carried in a litter.</p></li>
<li data-ll="12.800,45.033" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a></span><p>From the coast they went by ship to Zanzibar and then to Aden. Burton was not granted a medical certificate to travel, so Speke left alone on HMS Furious and reached England on 8 May 1859.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">With Grant to Buganda and down the Nile, 1860 to 1863</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="-5.900,39.300" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Zanzibar</span> <span class="jr-date">October 1860</span><p>Speke and James Augustus Grant left Portsmouth on 27 April 1860 and set out from Zanzibar in October 1860. Locals often fled or fought, taking them for slavers, and demands for gifts and passage fees delayed them and used up their supplies.</p></li>
<li data-ll="0.434,33.194" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Ripon Falls</span><p>After reaching Lake Victoria on 28 July 1862, Speke went round its western side and, on the north side, found the Nile flowing out of it at the Ripon Falls. He had also stayed at the court of Muteesa I, Kabaka of Buganda.</p></li>
<li data-ll="4.900,31.667" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Gondokoro</span> <span class="jr-date">January 1863</span><p>Speke, reunited with Grant, travelled down the Nile but could not map all of its flow from the lake. They reached Gondokoro in January 1863, where he met Samuel Baker. The Pethericks, sent by the Royal Geographical Society to meet them, were away trading ivory.</p></li>
<li data-ll="15.603,32.527" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a></span><p>Speke continued to Khartoum by Baker's ship. From there he sent a telegram to London reading &quot;The Nile is settled.&quot; Burton replied that Speke had not followed the river from the lake to Gondokoro, so could not be sure it was the same river.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Speke and Grant reached Gondokoro in January 1863 and arrived in England in June 1863. Burton disputed that the Nile had been traced, and Speke died on 15 September 1864, the day before a planned debate with him.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;John Hanning Speke&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="burke-wills" data-kids="0">
<h2>The Burke and Wills Expedition</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1860–1861 &#183; Robert O'Hara Burke and William John Wills &#183; 11 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The Victorian Exploring Expedition, organised by the Royal Society of Victoria, set out from Melbourne under Robert O'Hara Burke with William John Wills as deputy. Its aim was to cross Australia to the Gulf of Carpentaria, a distance of around 3,250 kilometres, through country most of which had not been explored by non-Indigenous people.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Melbourne to Menindee</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="-37.814,144.963" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a></span> <span class="jr-date">20 August 1860</span><p>The nineteen men left Royal Park at about 4pm, watched by around 15,000 spectators, with twenty-three horses, six wagons and twenty-six camels. Four of the men were camel drivers from the Indian subcontinent. One wagon broke down before leaving the park.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-37.267,144.717" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Lancefield</span> <span class="jr-date">23 August 1860</span><p>Heavy rains and bad roads made travel through Victoria slow, and more wagons broke down. The party reached Lancefield on 23 August and set up their fourth camp.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-35.333,143.550" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Swan Hill</span> <span class="jr-date">6 September 1860</span><p>The expedition reached Swan Hill, where Charles Gray joined it. Heavy rains and bad roads had made travel through Victoria slow.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-34.638,143.560" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Balranald</span> <span class="jr-date">15 September 1860</span><p>To lighten the load, the expedition left behind its sugar, lime juice and some guns and ammunition. Burke dismissed several members, among them the foreman Charles Ferguson, citing lack of funds.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-32.392,142.418" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Menindee</span> <span class="jr-date">12 October 1860</span><p>The expedition reached Menindee on the Darling River after two months, having covered 750 km. Burke divided the party here and left on 19 October with seven men, guided by a Paakantyi man named Dick Barkinji, William Wright and another local Aboriginal man.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Cooper Creek and the Gulf</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="-27.624,141.076" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Cooper Creek</span> <span class="jr-date">11 November 1860</span><p>Burke's party, using the pathways of the Aboriginal residents they met at the Bulloo, reached Cooper Creek and formed a depot, later moved to Camp 65, where they built a stockade. On 16 December Burke, Wills, King and Gray set off for the Gulf, leaving William Brahe in charge.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.676,141.084" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Camp 119</span> <span class="jr-date">c. 2 February 1861</span><p>Rain made water easy to find, and the Aboriginal people were peaceful. Wills found the Bynoe River salty and tidal. Burke and Wills went on through swamps, where Kukatj men pointed them in the best direction. They may have viewed the Gulf on 11 February, though no document shows it.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The return and the deaths</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="8">
<li data-ll="-22.900,139.900" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Burke River, south of Boulia</span> <span class="jr-date">25 March 1861</span><p>The monsoon rains had begun and the party was eating portulaca and a python. Burke beat Gray for stealing porridge. Gray, weakened by dysentery, died on 17 April at a place they called Polygonum Swamp, whose location is unknown.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-25.899,139.352" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Diamantina River, south of Birdsville</span> <span class="jr-date">10 April 1861</span><p>With the pack animals dwindling and equipment abandoned, the party shot their only horse, Billy, on the Diamantina River. Three camels had already been shot and eaten, and another abandoned.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-27.624,141.076" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Camp 65, the Dig Tree</span> <span class="jr-date">21 April 1861</span><p>Burke, Wills and King found the depot deserted nine hours after Brahe left and dug up the cache. On 23 April they set off for Mount Hopeless. The Yandruwandha gave them fish, padlu, nardoo cakes and shelter. Burke fired over Yandruwandha heads and knocked fish from their hands.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-27.624,141.076" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Cooper Creek, the Dig Tree</span> <span class="jr-date">11 September 1861</span><p>Alfred Howitt's rescue party reached the Dig Tree. King, found on 15 September, had lived about a month with a Yandruwandha clan, to whom he gave birds. Howitt buried the remains of Burke and Wills and gave the Yandruwandha tomahawks, knives, sugar and flour.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Burke and Wills died on or about 30 June 1861 after the depot at Cooper Creek was abandoned hours before they returned to it. Seven men died in all, and John King, who had been cared for by the Yandruwandha, was the only man who crossed the continent with the expedition and came back alive to Melbourne.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Burke and Wills expedition&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="long-walk" data-kids="0">
<h2>The Long Walk of the Navajo</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1864–1868 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/long-walk-of-the-navajo">The Navajo (Diné)</a> &#183; 6 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The United States Army removed the Navajo from their homeland in what is now Arizona and New Mexico to Bosque Redondo, Hwéeldi in Navajo, at Fort Sumner. It has been called a deportation and an ethnic cleansing, and it was carried out in some 53 forced marches between August 1864 and the end of 1866.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The homeland, the forts and the campaign</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="35.742,-109.067" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Defiance</span> <span class="jr-date">August 1851</span><p>Colonel Edwin Vose Sumner set up the fort for the United States government on Navajo land. In April 1860 Manuelito and Barboncito led about 1,000 Navajo warriors against it and almost took control.</p></li>
<li data-ll="35.468,-108.541" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Fauntleroy</span> <span class="jr-date">9 August 1861</span><p>Lt. Col. Manuel Antonio Chaves took command of the garrison here. After a disputed horse race with Manuelito in September, soldiers killed 15 Navajos, mostly women and children, and wounded or captured many more.</p></li>
<li data-ll="35.085,-107.518" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Cubero</span><p>With Confederate troops moving into southern New Mexico, General Canby sent an agent to persuade friendly Navajo to gather at a central encampment near Cubero. Those who refused were to be treated as enemies, and many came.</p></li>
<li data-ll="35.742,-109.067" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Canby</span> <span class="jr-date">January 1864</span><p>Kit Carson, with Ute allies, burned hogans, killed livestock and destroyed irrigated fields. Navajo who surrendered were taken to Fort Canby, and those who resisted were killed. Some Navajo escaped to places such as Navajo Mountain and the Grand Canyon.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">To Bosque Redondo and back</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="34.402,-104.195" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Sumner (Bosque Redondo)</span> <span class="jr-date">spring 1864</span><p>Bands led by the Army were marched to Bosque Redondo in the Pecos River valley, told nothing of where they were going or why. At least 200 died on the trek, and the camp held 9,022 people by spring 1865.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.402,-104.195" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Sumner (Treaty of Bosque Redondo)</span> <span class="jr-date">1 June 1868</span><p>Barboncito, Manuelito, Ganado Mucho and other Navajo leaders signed the treaty with General Sherman. It set up a reservation and arranged the return of the Navajo to it, ending the experiment at Bosque Redondo.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">In 1868 the Navajo were allowed to return to their ancestral homeland under the Treaty of Bosque Redondo. Up to 3,500 people died from starvation, disease and executions over the four years.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Long Walk of the Navajo&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="sherman" data-kids="0">
<h2>Sherman's March to the Sea</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1864 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/william-tecumseh-sherman">William T. Sherman</a> &#183; 8 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Major general William T. Sherman led about 62,000 Union soldiers out of Atlanta on 15 November 1864 on a march of roughly 300 miles (480 km) through Georgia. The army lived off the land and destroyed railroads, industry and civilian property as it went.</p>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="33.754,-84.390" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Atlanta</span> <span class="jr-date">15 November 1864</span><p>Sherman's 62,000 men, in two columns, began the march from the recently captured city. Sherman later recalled Atlanta behind him smouldering and in ruins, and the troops singing &quot;John Brown's Body&quot;.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.444,-84.315" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Lovejoy's Station</span><p>Howard's wing, led by Kilpatrick's cavalry, marched south along the railroad. The defenders conducted a fighting retreat to Macon, and the cavalry captured two Confederate guns.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.871,-83.489" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Griswoldville</span> <span class="jr-date">22 November 1864</span><p>Howard's wing met the first real resistance. After several hours of badly coordinated Confederate attacks, the Confederates retreated with about 1,100 casualties, about 600 of them prisoners, against about 100 Union casualties.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.080,-83.232" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Milledgeville</span> <span class="jr-date">23 November 1864</span><p>Governor Joseph Brown and the state legislature left in haste as Slocum's wing approached. Slocum's troops captured the city and held a mock legislative session in the capitol, jokingly voting Georgia back into the Union.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.982,-82.810" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Sandersville</span> <span class="jr-date">25–26 November 1864</span><p>Wheeler's Confederate cavalry struck at the advance guard of Slocum's wing here. Several small actions followed along the march, among them Ball's Ferry on 24 and 25 November.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.091,-82.015" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Waynesboro</span> <span class="jr-date">4 December 1864</span><p>Kilpatrick's cavalry routed Wheeler's cavalry in the Battle of Waynesboro, after earlier clashes at Sylvan Grove and Buck Head Creek in which Kilpatrick had been driven back and nearly captured.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.891,-81.196" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort McAllister</span> <span class="jr-date">13 December 1864</span><p>The army reached the outskirts of Savannah on 10 December, but Hardee had entrenched 10,000 men and flooded the rice fields. Hazen's division stormed this fort within 15 minutes, with 134 Union casualties, some caused by torpedoes, and Sherman gained contact with the Navy.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.051,-81.104" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Savannah</span> <span class="jr-date">21 December 1864</span><p>Hardee led his men across the Savannah River on a pontoon bridge on 20 December. Mayor Richard Dennis Arnold offered the city's surrender, and Geary's division of the XX Corps occupied it. Sherman then offered Savannah to Lincoln as a Christmas gift.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The march ended with the capture of Savannah on 21 December 1864. Sherman estimated the destruction at $100 million, and a Confederate officer estimated that 10,000 liberated slaves followed the army, of whom hundreds died of hunger, disease or exposure.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Sherman's March to the Sea&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="iwakura" data-kids="1">
<h2>The Iwakura Mission</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1871–1873 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/iwakura-mission">Iwakura Tomomi</a> &#183; 12 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The newly established Meiji government sent about 50 officials, led by Iwakura Tomomi, to the United States and Europe. The mission was to gain recognition for the imperial dynasty, renegotiate the unequal treaties and study modern industrial, political, military and educational systems.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Across the Pacific and the United States</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="35.450,139.634" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Yokohama</span> <span class="jr-date">December 1871</span><p>The mission began its voyage here, bound for San Francisco aboard the SS America. About 100 Japanese leaders, officials and students sailed.</p></li>
<li data-ll="37.775,-122.419" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a></span> <span class="jr-date">January 1872</span><p>The party arrived in California and went on by train, to study the political, social and economic developments of the Western nations.</p></li>
<li data-ll="38.895,-77.037" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a></span> <span class="jr-date">29 February 1872</span><p>The mission stayed about seven months. Struggles with President Ulysses S. Grant and Secretary of State Hamilton Fish over amending the treaties went unresolved, and the party left for the United Kingdom in August 1872.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The United Kingdom</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="53.407,-2.992" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a></span> <span class="jr-date">17 August 1872</span><p>The mission arrived on the Cunard steamer Olympus, then travelled to London via Manchester.</p></li>
<li data-ll="51.507,-0.128" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></span><p>The party spent much of late August and early September in and around the capital, inspecting political, academic and military institutions, visiting the British Museum and travelling on the new London Underground.</p></li>
<li data-ll="53.479,-2.245" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Manchester</span> <span class="jr-date">7 October 1872</span><p>Iwakura Tomomi led the Manchester-Liverpool delegation. A civic reception and banquet on 7 October had toasts to the region's leading role in manufacturing, technology and municipal administration.</p></li>
<li data-ll="55.861,-4.250" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a></span><p>As guests of Lord Blantyre, the delegation stayed at Erskine House and toured shipbuilding and steel fabrication facilities on the banks of the River Clyde.</p></li>
<li data-ll="54.978,-1.613" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle upon Tyne</a></span> <span class="jr-date">21 October 1872</span><p>The group stayed at the Royal Station Hotel and met the industrialist Sir William Armstrong. It visited the Elswick works and the Gosforth Colliery, and new export orders for ships and armaments followed.</p></li>
<li data-ll="53.837,-1.790" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Saltaire</span><p>Accompanied by the British envoy Sir Harry Smith Parkes, the group inspected textile manufacturing at Salts Mill, and toured villages, schools, almshouses, hospitals and parks provided by Sir Titus Salt and Sir John Crossley for their workers.</p></li>
<li data-ll="53.381,-1.470" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Sheffield</span> <span class="jr-date">28 October 1872</span><p>Members arrived for four days and stayed at Banner Cross Hall, owned by George Wilson of Charles Cammell and Company. They visited factories and workshops.</p></li>
<li data-ll="51.484,-0.604" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Windsor Castle</span> <span class="jr-date">5 December 1872</span><p>The delegation was presented to Queen Victoria at an official audience at Windsor Castle.</p></li>
<li data-ll="52.830,0.514" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Sandringham</span> <span class="jr-date">9 December 1872</span><p>The delegation had an audience with the Prince of Wales. The party left for France on 16 December.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The treaties were not revised, which prolonged the mission by almost four months. Its members were nonetheless impressed by the industrial modernisation they saw and took from the tour an impetus to lead similar initiatives at home.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Iwakura Mission&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="nez-perce" data-kids="0">
<h2>The Flight of the Nez Perce</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1877 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/nez-perce-war">The non-treaty Nez Perce (Nimíipuu) bands</a> &#183; 18 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">In 1877 several non-treaty bands of the Nez Perce refused to give up their ancestral lands and move to a reservation in Idaho Territory. Led by a coalition that included Ollokot, White Bird, Toohoolhoolzote, Looking Glass and Chief Joseph, they fought a retreat of 1,170 miles (1,880 km) while pursued by the United States Army, first to seek help from the Crow and then to reach Sitting Bull's camp in Canada.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From the homeland to the Clearwater country</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="46.450,-116.817" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Lapwai</span><p>After General Howard ordered the non-treaty bands onto the reservation within 30 days, Joseph and the other leaders judged resistance futile. They agreed to the move and reported to Fort Lapwai as ordered.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.916,-116.236" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tolo Lake</span> <span class="jr-date">14 June 1877</span><p>At White Bird's camp at Tolo Lake, three young men set out on a mission of revenge for the earlier killing of a relative by a white man. They killed four white men and wounded another, and about sixteen more young men then rode out to raid the settlements.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.761,-116.302" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">White Bird Canyon</span> <span class="jr-date">16 June 1877</span><p>The bands camped in the canyon and decided to avoid war but fight if forced. Cavalry under Captain Perry approached, and after shots were fired at a truce party the Nez Perce routed them, killing 34 soldiers while three Nez Perce warriors were wounded.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.052,-116.351" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Cottonwood</span> <span class="jr-date">3–5 July 1877</span><p>After crossing and recrossing the Salmon River, the warriors rebuffed the army in a series of skirmishes near Cottonwood.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Across the Bitterroots and Montana</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="46.635,-114.580" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Lolo Pass</span><p>After Howard's force attacked their Clearwater camp, about 250 warriors and 500 women and children, with more than 2,000 horses and other livestock, crossed from Idaho over Lolo Pass into Montana Territory.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.746,-114.172" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Fizzle</span><p>Forces tried to halt the Nez Perce at a barricade here, but the Nez Perce bypassed it.</p></li>
<li data-ll="45.638,-113.644" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Big Hole</span><p>The two-day battle in southwestern Montana Territory left heavy casualties on both sides, including many Nez Perce women and children. Afterwards the war grew fiercer, and the Crow refused the Nez Perce refuge, so they decided to try to reach Canada.</p></li>
<li data-ll="44.353,-111.885" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Camas Meadows</span> <span class="jr-date">20 August 1877</span><p>While Howard's men were camped at Camas Meadows, the Nez Perce raided at night and stole horses and mules. They held off the cavalry that pursued them and continued into Yellowstone National Park.</p></li>
<li data-ll="44.600,-110.500" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Yellowstone National Park</span><p>The Nez Perce travelled along what was later named Nez Perce Creek across the Central Plateau and Hayden Valley. They crossed the Yellowstone River at Nez Perce Ford and left by a difficult route across the Absaroka Mountains.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.806,-112.264" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Canyon Creek</span> <span class="jr-date">13 September 1877</span><p>The army caught up with them in the canyon of Canyon Creek, west of Billings. The warriors took positions in the hills and fought a delaying action before escaping over the hills.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The Bear's Paw Mountains and exile</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="11">
<li data-ll="48.377,-109.207" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Bear's Paw Mountains</span> <span class="jr-date">30 September 1877</span><p>The Nez Perce camped on the prairie beside Snake Creek, only 40 miles from the border, believing they had shaken off their pursuers. General Miles's force made a surprise attack on the camp in the morning.</p></li>
<li data-ll="48.377,-109.207" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Bears Paw Mountains (surrender)</span> <span class="jr-date">5 October 1877</span><p>After a three-day standoff Howard arrived on 3 October. Chief Joseph surrendered on 5 October, saying he would fight no more forever. White Bird escaped with an undetermined number of his band to Sitting Bull's camp in Canada.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.376,-105.883" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tongue River Cantonment</span> <span class="jr-date">23 October 1877</span><p>Miles marched his captives 265 miles (426 km) to the Tongue River Cantonment in southeast Montana Territory, where they were held until 31 October.</p></li>
<li data-ll="47.986,-104.001" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Buford</span> <span class="jr-date">1 November 1877</span><p>The able-bodied warriors were marched to Fort Buford. On 1 November the women, children, the ill and the wounded set out for it in fourteen Mackinaw boats.</p></li>
<li data-ll="46.808,-100.784" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Bismarck</span> <span class="jr-date">23 November 1877</span><p>Most of Bismarck's citizens turned out to welcome the prisoners with a buffet. On 23 November they were loaded into eleven rail coaches for the journey to Fort Leavenworth.</p></li>
<li data-ll="39.355,-94.921" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Fort Leavenworth</span><p>Over the protests of the fort's commander, the Nez Perce were forced to live in a swampy bottomland. Howard and Miles had promised a return to Idaho, but General Sherman overruled them.</p></li>
<li data-ll="36.680,-97.309" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tonkawa</span><p>After Joseph pleaded in Washington in January 1879, the government refused to let the Nez Perce return to Idaho because of fierce opposition there. They were sent to Oklahoma and settled on a small reservation near Tonkawa.</p></li>
<li data-ll="48.230,-118.873" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Colville Indian Reservation</span> <span class="jr-date">1885</span><p>Joseph and 268 surviving Nez Perce were allowed to return to the Pacific Northwest. Joseph was not permitted to go back to the Nez Perce reservation and settled at Colville, where he died in 1904.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The war ended at the Bear's Paw Mountains, 40 miles (64 km) from the border, where 418 Nez Perce surrendered and were sent to Fort Leavenworth, Kansas. White Bird escaped with an undetermined number of his band to Sitting Bull's camp in Canada.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Nez Perce War&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="fram" data-kids="1">
<h2>Nansen's Fram Expedition</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1893–1896 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/fridtjof-nansen">Fridtjof Nansen</a> &#183; 12 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The Norwegian explorer Fridtjof Nansen planned to reach the North Pole by freezing his ship Fram into the pack ice near the New Siberian Islands and letting the drift carry her towards the pole. After 18 months he left the ship with Hjalmar Johansen to go on by sledge.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Christiania to the Siberian coast</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="59.913,10.739" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Christiania</a></span> <span class="jr-date">24 June 1893</span><p>Fram left Christiania, now Oslo, with a cannon salute from the fort and the cheers of thousands of well-wishers. Nansen planned to reach the New Siberian Islands by the North-East Passage along the northern coast of Siberia.</p></li>
<li data-ll="60.392,5.323" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1 July 1893</span><p>Fram reached Bergen on 1 July, where a great banquet was held in Nansen's honour. It was one of a series of farewells as the ship sailed round the coast and moved northward.</p></li>
<li data-ll="63.440,10.400" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a></span> <span class="jr-date">5 July 1893</span><p>Fram called at Trondheim on 5 July, another port of farewell on her way north along the Norwegian coast.</p></li>
<li data-ll="69.652,18.956" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tromsø</span><p>Fram reached Tromsø, north of the Arctic Circle, a week after Trondheim. Bernt Bentzen joined the expedition here at very short notice.</p></li>
<li data-ll="70.371,31.110" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Vardø</span> <span class="jr-date">18 July 1893</span><p>Vardø was the last Norwegian port of call. After the final provisions were taken on board, Nansen, Sverdrup, Hansen and Blessing spent their last hours ashore in a sauna.</p></li>
<li data-ll="77.717,104.300" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cape Chelyuskin</span> <span class="jr-date">10 September 1893</span><p>After heavy ice had slowed the ship, a stretch of open water allowed Fram to round Cape Chelyuskin, the most northerly point of the Eurasian mainland, and enter the Laptev Sea. Only Nordenskiöld's Vega had done so before.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Nansen and Johansen in Franz Josef Land</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="81.217,56.333" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Jackson Island</span> <span class="jr-date">28 September 1895</span><p>Nansen turned back on 7 April, his farthest north camp at 86°13.6′N, and made for Franz Josef Land. They wintered on what is now Jackson Island in a shelter called The Hole, ready on 28 September, and left on 19 May 1896.</p></li>
<li data-ll="79.987,50.968" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cape Flora</span> <span class="jr-date">17 June 1896</span><p>Nansen heard a dog bark and then voices, and met Frederick Jackson, whose expedition had its headquarters at Cape Flora. Nansen and Johansen were taken to the base, and Nansen named the island of their winter quarters after Jackson.</p></li>
<li data-ll="70.371,31.110" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Vardø</span> <span class="jr-date">13 August 1896</span><p>The supply ship Windward arrived at Cape Flora on 26 July and sailed south on 7 August with Nansen and Johansen aboard. She reached Vardø on 13 August, and telegrams told the world of Nansen's safe return.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Home to Christiania</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="10">
<li data-ll="70.663,23.682" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Hammerfest</span> <span class="jr-date">18 August 1896</span><p>Nansen and Johansen waited at Vardø for the weekly mail steamer south and reached Hammerfest, where they were given an enthusiastic reception.</p></li>
<li data-ll="69.652,18.956" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tromsø</span> <span class="jr-date">21 August 1896</span><p>Nansen learned that Sverdrup had brought Fram to Skjervøy and was continuing to Tromsø. Nansen and Johansen sailed into Tromsø the next day and joined their comrades in an emotional reunion.</p></li>
<li data-ll="59.913,10.739" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Christiania</a></span> <span class="jr-date">9 September 1896</span><p>Fram left Tromsø on 26 August and arrived in Christiania, escorted into the harbour by a squadron of warships and welcomed by thousands. Nansen and his crew were received by King Oscar.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Nansen and Johansen reached a record Farthest North of 86°13.6′N but turned back, wintered in Franz Josef Land and were found there by Frederick Jackson in 1896. Fram emerged from the ice near Spitsbergen on 13 August 1896, and the ship and her crew reached Christiania together.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Nansen's Fram expedition&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="tibet-1904" data-kids="0">
<h2>The British Expedition to Tibet</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1903–1904 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/francis-younghusband">Francis Younghusband</a> &#183; 9 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The British expedition to Tibet, also called the Younghusband expedition, was effectively a temporary invasion by British Indian armed forces under the Tibet Frontier Commission. Its purported mission was to establish diplomatic relations and resolve the dispute over the Tibet–Sikkim border, and it was intended to counter perceived Russian ambitions.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Sikkim to the Tibetan plateau</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="27.330,88.620" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/gangtok">Gangtok</a></span> <span class="jr-date">19 July 1903</span><p>Younghusband arrived in the capital of the princely state of Sikkim to prepare his mission. John Claude White, the political officer there, was unhappy with his secondment to the force.</p></li>
<li data-ll="28.217,88.474" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Khampa Dzong</span> <span class="jr-date">August 1903</span><p>Curzon had asked China and Tibet for talks here. The Dalai Lama refused to attend, and from August 1903 Younghusband and his escort commander, Lieutenant Colonel Brander, tried to provoke the Tibetans into a confrontation.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.485,88.907" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tuna</span><p>When no Tibetan or Chinese officials met the British at Khampa Dzong, Younghusband advanced to Tuna with some 1,150 soldiers, porters and labourers. After months of waiting in vain for negotiators, the expedition was ordered to continue toward Lhasa.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The fighting road to Gyantse</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="28.559,89.679" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Guru (Chumik Shenko)</span> <span class="jr-date">31 March 1904</span><p>A Tibetan force of 3,000 with matchlock muskets blocked the road behind a rock wall. After scuffles, the British, Sikh and Gurkha troops fired from three sides with Maxim guns. The Tibetans left between 600 and 700 dead and 168 wounded; British casualties were 12 wounded.</p></li>
<li data-ll="28.920,89.600" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Gyantse</span> <span class="jr-date">11 April 1904</span><p>The force reached the town after fighting at Red Idol Gorge on 9 April, where the Tibetans left 200 dead. The garrison had already left and the gates were opened. Younghusband's staff made Changlo Manor the Mission headquarters.</p></li>
<li data-ll="28.918,89.599" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Gyantse Dzong</span> <span class="jr-date">6 July 1904</span><p>After a breach was made in the Tibetan walls, Gurkhas and Royal Fusiliers took the fortress after eleven hours. Soldiers then pillaged Palkor Chode, Dongtse and other monasteries, and some of the pillaging was officially approved, according to Major Beynon.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">To Lhasa and the treaty</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="28.971,90.398" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Nakartse</span><p>After marching east from Gyantse on 14 July, the force passed along the shores of Yamdok Tso. It reached the fortress of Nakartse, empty except for a party of delegates from Lhasa. Younghusband would negotiate only at Lhasa.</p></li>
<li data-ll="29.654,91.118" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a></span> <span class="jr-date">3 August 1904</span><p>The force found that the 13th Dalai Lama had fled to Urga, the capital of Outer Mongolia. The Amban escorted the British into the city but said he had no authority to negotiate.</p></li>
<li data-ll="29.658,91.117" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Potala Palace</span> <span class="jr-date">7 September 1904</span><p>Younghusband intimidated the regent, Ganden Tri Rinpoche, and the Tsongdu into signing the Convention of Lhasa, which he had drafted. It was signed at his insistence at the Potala. It set an indemnity of 7,500,000 rupees and put the Chumbi Valley under British occupation until it was paid.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The Commission forced the Tibetan regent and National Assembly to sign the Convention of Lhasa on 7 September 1904, and the mission left in late September. Tibetan casualties have been estimated at between 2,000 and 3,000 killed or fatally wounded, and the British lost 202 men killed in action.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;British expedition to Tibet&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="terra-nova" data-kids="1">
<h2>Scott's Terra Nova Expedition to the South Pole</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1910–1913 &#183; Robert Falcon Scott &#183; 10 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Captain Robert Falcon Scott led the Terra Nova Expedition to Antarctica with scientific and geographical aims, and wanted to be the first to reach the geographic South Pole. The expedition was named after its supply ship and was a private venture financed by public contributions and a government grant.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The voyage out and the base</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="51.482,-3.179" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a></span> <span class="jr-date">15 June 1910</span><p>Terra Nova sailed from Cardiff. Scott, detained by expedition business, sailed later on a faster passenger liner and joined the ship in South Africa.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-45.818,170.619" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Port Chalmers</span> <span class="jr-date">29 November 1910</span><p>Scott rejoined the ship in New Zealand, where 34 dogs, 19 Siberian ponies and three motorised sledges were taken aboard. The heavily overloaded ship finally left Port Chalmers on this day.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-77.633,166.400" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cape Evans</span> <span class="jr-date">4 January 1911</span><p>Terra Nova arrived off Ross Island and Scott chose a cape about 15 miles north of his 1902 base at Hut Point, renaming it Cape Evans after his second-in-command. A prefabricated hut was erected there and made habitable by 18 January.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-81.500,-175.000" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">One Ton Depot</span><p>A party of 12 men laid depots across the Barrier for the polar journey. Scott, worried that the ponies would not get back, laid the largest depot at 79°29′S, more than 30 miles short of its intended place.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">To the Pole and back</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="-77.633,166.400" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Cape Evans</span> <span class="jr-date">1 November 1911</span><p>Scott's main party left the base with the dogs and ponies. A motor party had set out on 24 October, but both its motor sledges failed after little more than 50 miles.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-83.750,171.000" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Beardmore Glacier</span> <span class="jr-date">4 December 1911</span><p>The expedition reached the Gateway, the route from the Barrier on to the Beardmore Glacier. A blizzard kept the men in camp until 9 December, after which the remaining ponies were shot and the dogs turned back on 11 December.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-89.998,139.273" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">South Pole</span> <span class="jr-date">17 January 1912</span><p>Scott, Wilson, Oates, Bowers and Edgar Evans spotted Amundsen's black flag some 15 miles from the Pole and knew they had been forestalled. They reached the Pole on 17 January, and found Amundsen's tent the next day.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-83.750,171.000" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Beardmore Glacier</span> <span class="jr-date">7 February 1912</span><p>On the way home the party began its descent on 7 February and had serious difficulty finding a depot. Edgar Evans, badly frostbitten and weakened by falls, collapsed near the bottom of the glacier and died on 17 February.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-81.500,-175.000" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">One Ton Depot</span> <span class="jr-date">29 March 1912</span><p>The dog teams did not come, and temperatures fell sharply. On 16 March Oates left the tent and walked to his death. Scott, Wilson and Bowers were halted by a blizzard 11 miles south of One Ton Depot on 20 March, and Scott's last diary entry is dated 29 March.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-81.500,-175.000" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">One Ton Depot</span> <span class="jr-date">12 November 1912</span><p>A search party under Atkinson found the tent with the frozen bodies of Scott, Wilson and Bowers, 11 miles south of One Ton Depot. Atkinson read Scott's diaries, and the party raised a cairn of snow over the tent.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Scott and four companions reached the Pole on 17 January 1912 and found that a Norwegian team led by Roald Amundsen had preceded them by 34 days. All five died on the return journey, and a search party found some of their bodies, journals and photographs eight months later.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Terra Nova Expedition&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="amundsen" data-kids="1">
<h2>Amundsen's South Pole Expedition</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1910–1912 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/roald-amundsen">Roald Amundsen</a> &#183; 8 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The Norwegian explorer Roald Amundsen had planned an Arctic drift in Nansen's ship Fram. After Cook and Peary each claimed the North Pole in 1909, he changed his plan to the South Pole and kept the new aim secret, revealing it to his crew at Madeira.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Norway to the Great Ice Barrier</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="58.147,7.996" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Kristiansand</span> <span class="jr-date">late July 1910</span><p>Fram took the dogs on board and made final preparations here. Just before sailing on 9 August, Amundsen told only the two junior officers, Prestrud and Gjertsen, the true destination.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.650,-16.917" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Funchal</span> <span class="jr-date">6 September 1910</span><p>Three days after arriving at Madeira, Amundsen told the crew he meant to make a detour to the South Pole. Each man was asked if he would go on, and all said yes. He cabled Scott before leaving on 9 September.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-78.500,-164.333" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Bay of Whales</span> <span class="jr-date">14 January 1911</span><p>Fram sailed south for four months. The first icebergs were sighted on New Year's Day 1911, the Barrier came into view on 11 January, and the ship was in the Bay of Whales on 14 January.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The base and the march to the pole</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="-78.500,-164.000" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Framheim</span><p>The main hut stood 2.2 nautical miles from the ship, with its door facing west away from the prevailing wind. The base was named Framheim, the home of Fram. Depot journeys and the winter were spent here.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-85.417,-163.000" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Axel Heiberg Glacier</span> <span class="jr-date">21 November 1911</span><p>The five men left Framheim on 19 October with four sledges and fifty-two dogs. Having found a steep glacier about 30 nautical miles long, Amundsen named it after one of his backers. On 21 November they climbed 5,000 feet.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-90.000,0.000" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">South Pole</span> <span class="jr-date">14 December 1911</span><p>At around 3 pm the party reached the vicinity of the pole and planted the Norwegian flag. They spent three days fixing the position and pitched a tent, Polheim, leaving equipment and a letter for Scott.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Home to Framheim and on to Hobart</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="7">
<li data-ll="-78.500,-164.000" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Framheim</span> <span class="jr-date">25 January 1912</span><p>The party left the pole on 18 December and reached Framheim at 4 am on 25 January. Eleven of the 52 dogs that started had survived. The round trip took 99 days and covered about 1,860 nautical miles.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-42.883,147.317" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a></span> <span class="jr-date">7 March 1912</span><p>Fram left the Bay of Whales late on 30 January. At Hobart Amundsen telegraphed his brother Leon, Nansen and King Haakon, and the next day cabled the first full account to the Daily Chronicle.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Amundsen and four companions reached the Geographic South Pole on 14 December 1911, five weeks ahead of Scott's party, and returned safely to Framheim. Fram carried the news to Hobart, from where Amundsen sent his telegrams.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Amundsen's South Pole expedition&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="endurance" data-kids="1">
<h2>Shackleton's Imperial Trans-Antarctic Expedition</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1914–1917 &#183; Ernest Shackleton &#183; 12 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Ernest Shackleton planned the first land crossing of the Antarctic continent, from the Weddell Sea to the Ross Sea. His ship Endurance became trapped in the ice of the Weddell Sea before the party could land, and the expedition became one of survival.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">To the ice and the loss of the ship</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="50.371,-4.142" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Plymouth</span> <span class="jr-date">8 August 1914</span><p>Endurance left Plymouth without Shackleton, who was detained in England by expedition business, and headed first for Buenos Aires.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-34.600,-58.382" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a></span> <span class="jr-date">26 October 1914</span><p>Shackleton, who had travelled on a faster ship, rejoined the expedition here. The photographer Hurley, Bakewell and the stowaway Blackborow came aboard, and several others left or were discharged. The ship sailed for the South Atlantic on 26 October.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-54.281,-36.508" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Grytviken</span> <span class="jr-date">5 December 1914</span><p>Endurance reached South Georgia on 5 November and halted for a month at the Grytviken whaling station. She sailed south on 5 December and met pack ice two days later, as far north as 57° 26′S.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From the floes to Elephant Island</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="-61.140,-55.120" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Elephant Island</span> <span class="jr-date">15 April 1916</span><p>Endurance was held fast in the Weddell Sea ice, drifted north through the winter of 1915 and was abandoned on 27 October 1915. After months on the floes the three lifeboats reached the island, and the James Caird found a narrow shingle beach on its northern shore.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-61.100,-54.867" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Cape Wild</span> <span class="jr-date">16 April 1916</span><p>The beach would be covered at high tide, so Wild and a crew explored the coast in the Stancomb Wills and found a long spit of land seven miles to the west. The men moved the boats there and later named it Cape Wild.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The James Caird and the crossing of South Georgia</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="-54.150,-37.317" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">King Haakon Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">10 May 1916</span><p>The James Caird was launched from Elephant Island on 24 April 1916 with Shackleton, Worsley, Crean, McNish, Vincent and McCarthy. South Georgia was sighted on 8 May, and two days later the party struggled ashore here.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-54.150,-36.683" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Stromness</span> <span class="jr-date">20 May 1916</span><p>Shackleton, Worsley and Crean crossed the unexplored interior of the island on a march of 36 hours and reached the whaling station. The whalers met them with shock and disbelief, and the factory manager Thoralf Sørlle gave them food, clothes and shelter.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Rescue from Elephant Island</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="8">
<li data-ll="-51.694,-57.850" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/stanley-falkland-islands">Port Stanley</a></span><p>Shackleton left South Georgia on the whaler Southern Sky, but pack ice blocked the way about 70 miles from Elephant Island and the ship retreated here. He cabled London for a ship and was told that none was available before October.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-53.163,-70.908" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Punta Arenas</span> <span class="jr-date">12 July 1916</span><p>After the trawler Instituto de Pesca No. 1 failed to get through the pack, Shackleton, Worsley and Crean came here and met Allan MacDonald, who equipped the schooner Emma. She sailed on 12 July and was also defeated by the ice.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-61.140,-55.120" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Elephant Island</span> <span class="jr-date">30 August 1916</span><p>The Chilean tug Yelcho, captained by Luis Pardo, left on 25 August and found open seas. The fog lifted at 11:40 a.m. on 30 August and the camp was spotted. Within an hour all the men were aboard.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-53.163,-70.908" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Punta Arenas</span><p>Yelcho returned here with all the men from Elephant Island and received a triumphal welcome after its successful mission.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-33.046,-71.620" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a></span><p>The rescued men were moved to the port of Valparaíso, where they again had a warm welcome, and from there they were repatriated.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Shackleton, after three failed attempts, brought all the men off Elephant Island on 30 August 1916 without loss of life. The wreck of Endurance was found on the seafloor in 2022.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Imperial Trans-Antarctic Expedition&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="salt-march" data-kids="1">
<h2>The Salt March to Dandi and Dharasana</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1930 &#183; <a href="/life/mahatma-gandhi">Mohandas Gandhi</a> &#183; 11 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Mohandas Gandhi, leader of the Indian National Congress's campaign of nonviolent civil disobedience, set out on foot from the Sabarmati Ashram with 78 satyagrahis to break the British salt monopoly at the coastal village of Dandi. The march of about 24 days passed through 4 districts and 48 villages.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Sabarmati to the sea</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="23.060,72.581" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Sabarmati Ashram</span> <span class="jr-date">12 March 1930</span><p>Gandhi and 78 satyagrahis set out on foot from the ashram for Dandi, 385 km away. On the eve of the march thousands came to hear him at the evening prayer.</p></li>
<li data-ll="22.700,72.870" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Nadiad</span> <span class="jr-date">15 March 1930</span><p>The marchers reached Nadiad from Matar on the fourth day. In each village crowds greeted them with drums and cymbals, and Gandhi attacked the salt tax as inhuman.</p></li>
<li data-ll="22.556,72.951" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Anand</span> <span class="jr-date">17 March 1930</span><p>The marchers arrived from Nadiad on 16 March and rested here for a day. Each night they slept in the open and asked the villagers only for food and water to wash with.</p></li>
<li data-ll="21.600,73.000" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Ankleshwar</span> <span class="jr-date">26 March 1930</span><p>The march crossed the Narmada River on the way from Derol to Ankleshwar. Every day more people joined, until the procession was at least 3 km long.</p></li>
<li data-ll="21.205,72.840" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a></span> <span class="jr-date">1 April 1930</span><p>After crossing the Tapi River from Delad the marchers entered Surat, where 30,000 people greeted them. Foreign journalists and three Bombay cinema companies were filming newsreels along the route.</p></li>
<li data-ll="20.951,72.923" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Navsari</span> <span class="jr-date">3 April 1930</span><p>The marchers went on from Vanz to Navsari on 3 April, two days before they arrived at the seashore at Dandi.</p></li>
<li data-ll="20.891,72.806" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Dandi</span> <span class="jr-date">6 April 1930</span><p>Gandhi reached the seashore on 5 April. Next morning he raised a lump of salty mud, declaring that he was shaking the foundations of the British Empire, and boiled it in seawater to make illegal salt. He urged his followers to do the same.</p></li>
<li data-ll="20.949,72.914" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Jalalpur</span><p>After Gandhi broke the salt laws, about 700 telegrams were sent from the post office nearest to Dandi, most of them by the journalists who had come to report the news.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Arrest and Dharasana</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="9">
<li data-ll="20.891,72.806" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Dandi</span> <span class="jr-date">4 May 1930</span><p>Gandhi set up a temporary ashram near Dandi. Around midnight of 4 May, while he slept on a cot in a mango grove, the District magistrate of Surat arrived with two Indian officers and thirty heavily armed constables and arrested him.</p></li>
<li data-ll="18.521,73.855" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a></span><p>Gandhi was arrested under an 1827 regulation calling for the jailing of people engaged in unlawful activities. He was held without trial near Poona, now Pune.</p></li>
<li data-ll="20.683,72.917" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Dharasana</span><p>Abbas Tyabji and Kasturba Gandhi were arrested before reaching Dharasana, and Sarojini Naidu led the march on. Soldiers clubbed the satyagrahis with steel tipped lathis, and they did not resist. Webb Miller reported fractured skulls and broken shoulders, and police kicking the seated men and dragging them into ditches.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Gandhi broke the salt law at Dandi on 6 April 1930 and was arrested on the night of 4 to 5 May. At Dharasana, police clubbed the unresisting satyagrahis, and more than sixty thousand Indians were jailed as a result of the Salt Satyagraha.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Salt March&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="long-march" data-kids="0">
<h2>The Long March of the First Front Army</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1934–1935 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/long-march">The First Front Red Army of the Chinese Communist Party</a> &#183; 10 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">After defeat in Chiang Kai-shek's Fifth Encirclement Campaign, the Chinese Communist Party abandoned its Jiangxi Soviet in October 1934. The First Front Red Army of some 86,000 troops headed west over rugged terrain, retreating from the advancing Kuomintang forces towards a new headquarters in Yan'an, Shaanxi.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Breaking out of Jiangxi</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="25.883,116.033" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/ruijin">Ruijin</a></span> <span class="jr-date">10 October 1934</span><p>After the Red Army's defeat in the Fifth Encirclement Campaign, the Party decided on 10 October 1934 to abandon the Jiangxi Soviet and its headquarters at Ruijin. The First Front Red Army of some 86,000 troops headed west.</p></li>
<li data-ll="25.957,115.487" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Yudu</span> <span class="jr-date">16 October 1934</span><p>A force under Bo Gu and Otto Braun attacked the Kuomintang positions near Yudu. More than 86,000 troops, 11,000 administrative personnel and thousands of porters completed the breakout; the wounded and ill stayed behind to fight a delaying action.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Guizhou and the Zunyi Conference</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="3">
<li data-ll="26.158,109.778" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Tongdao</span> <span class="jr-date">12 December 1934</span><p>Leaders met near the Hunan and Guizhou border to decide the army's direction. Zhou Enlai endorsed Mao's proposal to turn towards Guizhou, urging the others to overrule the objections of Bo Gu and Braun.</p></li>
<li data-ll="26.235,109.133" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Liping</span> <span class="jr-date">December 1934</span><p>In the mountains of south-east Guizhou a second dispute arose over direction. Braun wanted eastern Guizhou, Mao wanted the west, and Zhou sided with Mao, so the army marched towards Zunyi.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.705,106.934" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Zunyi</span> <span class="jr-date">15 January 1935</span><p>The army captured Zunyi, weakly defended and far from Nationalist forces, with little more than 10,000 men left. The conference there, 15 to 17 January, denounced Bo and Braun, and Mao won over the meeting.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Rivers, mountains and marshes to Shaanxi</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="6">
<li data-ll="29.915,102.230" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Luding Bridge</span> <span class="jr-date">29 May 1935</span><p>The First Army is recorded as capturing Luding Bridge on 29 May. Official history extols this as a heroic triumph, but many historians now think the battle's difficulty was exaggerated or the incident fabricated for propaganda.</p></li>
<li data-ll="31.001,102.362" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Maogong</span> <span class="jr-date">June–July 1935</span><p>The First Front Army met Zhang Guotao's Fourth Front Army. Zhang insisted on going south-west and Mao north towards Shaanxi; they could not agree, and the two armies split.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.700,102.633" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Zoigê Marsh</span> <span class="jr-date">August 1935</span><p>The First Army crossed the Zoigê Marsh. It traversed several swamps and was attacked by Hui Ma clique forces under Generals Ma Bufang and Ma Buqing.</p></li>
<li data-ll="34.109,103.894" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Lazikou Pass</span> <span class="jr-date">16 September 1935</span><p>The First Army crossed the Lazikou Pass on 16 September 1935. In October it reached Shaanxi province and joined local Communist forces there, led by Liu Zhidan, Gao Gang and Xu Haidong.</p></li>
<li data-ll="36.595,109.486" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Yan'an</span> <span class="jr-date">19 October 1935</span><p>The First Front Army arrived at Yan'an in northern Shaanxi. Only some 8,000 troops made it.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">The First Front Army reached Yan'an on 19 October 1935 with about 8,000 survivors. Mao Zedong's leadership during the retreat brought him great prestige, and the march marked the beginning of his ascent to primacy in the Party.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Long March&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="everest-1953" data-kids="1">
<h2>The First Ascent of Mount Everest</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1953 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/tenzing-norgay">John Hunt's British expedition, with Tenzing Norgay and Edmund Hillary</a> &#183; 10 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">The ninth expedition to attempt Mount Everest, led by Colonel John Hunt, was organised and financed by the Joint Himalayan Committee. Twenty Sherpas led by their Sirdar, Tenzing Norgay, joined the climbers and carried loads for the route from the Nepalese side.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">From Kathmandu to Base Camp</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="27.710,85.320" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">10 March 1953</span><p>The party was looked after by the British ambassador, Christopher Summerhayes. In early March twenty Sherpas, led by Tenzing Norgay, arrived to help carry loads. The first party left with 150 porters on 10 March, and the second followed on 11 March with 200.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.836,86.764" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Thyangboche</span> <span class="jr-date">26 March 1953</span><p>The first party reached Thyangboche on 26 March and the second on 27 March. Between 26 March and 17 April the climbers spent time getting used to the altitude.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.959,86.825" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Base Camp</span> <span class="jr-date">12 April 1953</span><p>The Icefall party reached Base Camp at 17,900 ft (5455 m). A few days then went on establishing a route, and Sherpa teams moved tonnes of supplies up to Base.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Up the mountain to the summit</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="4">
<li data-ll="27.996,86.873" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Khumbu Icefall</span><p>A route through the Khumbu Icefall was established as planned, and once it was open, teams of Sherpas carried tonnes of supplies up to Base Camp. Camp III was later set at the head of the Icefall, at 20,200 feet.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.975,86.932" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">South Col</span> <span class="jr-date">21 May 1953</span><p>Wilfrid Noyce and the Sherpa Annullu, younger brother of Da Tenzing, had reached the South Col by 21 May, just under 26,000 feet. Hillary and Tenzing later set out from Camp IX, higher on the Col.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.985,86.926" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">South Summit</span> <span class="jr-date">29 May 1953</span><p>Hillary and Tenzing left Camp IX at 6.30 am and reached the South Summit at 9 am. Bourdillon and Evans had reached it on 26 May but turned back after trouble with their oxygen sets.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.987,86.925" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Hillary Step</span><p>Hillary wrote that he climbed the 40-foot Hillary Step, just below the summit, and went on cutting steps along the ridge, bump after bump and cornice after cornice, with Tenzing holding the rope.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.988,86.925" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/mount-everest">The summit</a></span> <span class="jr-date">29 May 1953</span><p>Hillary and Tenzing reached the summit at 11:30 a.m. They stayed to take photographs and to bury some sweets and a small cross in the snow. Tenzing later wrote that Hillary was first.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The news and the return</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="9">
<li data-ll="27.804,86.710" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Namche Bazaar</span> <span class="jr-date">30 May 1953</span><p>Jan Morris of The Times heard the news at Base Camp and sent a coded message by runner to Namche Bazaar. A wireless transmitter there forwarded it as a telegram to the British Embassy in Kathmandu.</p></li>
<li data-ll="27.710,85.320" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a></span><p>Returning a few days later, the expedition learned that Hillary had been appointed a Knight Commander of the Order of the British Empire and Hunt a Knight Bachelor.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Tenzing Norgay and Edmund Hillary reached the summit on 29 May 1953 at 11:30 a.m. News of the success reached London in time to be released on the morning of Queen Elizabeth II's coronation, on 2 June.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;1953 British Mount Everest expedition&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="selma" data-kids="0">
<h2>The Selma to Montgomery Marches</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1965 &#183; <a href="/encyclopedia/selma-to-montgomery-marches">Marchers of the Selma voting rights campaign</a> &#183; 9 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Three marches were held in 1965 along the 54 mile (87 km) highway from Selma, Alabama, to the state capital, Montgomery. Nonviolent activists organised them to demand that African American citizens be allowed to exercise their constitutional right to vote.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Selma, Marion and the road to the bridge</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="32.412,-87.016" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Brown Chapel A.M.E. Church, Selma</span> <span class="jr-date">2 January 1965</span><p>Martin Luther King Jr. addressed a mass meeting here in defiance of an injunction against such gatherings, and the Selma Voting Rights Campaign officially began.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.633,-87.317" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Marion</span> <span class="jr-date">18 February 1965</span><p>C. T. Vivian led a march to the Perry County courthouse in Marion. Troopers attacked the marchers after the street lights were turned off, and trooper James Bonard Fowler shot Jimmie Lee Jackson in a café. Jackson died eight days later.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.633,-87.317" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Zion United Methodist Church, Marion</span> <span class="jr-date">28 February 1965</span><p>At a meeting after Jackson's death, with emotions running high, James Bevel called for a march from Selma to Montgomery to speak to Governor George Wallace about the killing, hoping to turn anger towards a nonviolent goal.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.406,-87.019" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Edmund Pettus Bridge, Selma</span> <span class="jr-date">7 March 1965</span><p>An estimated 525 to 600 marchers crossed the bridge and met state troopers and county possemen. The troopers shoved and beat them with nightsticks, fired tear gas and charged on horseback. Seventeen marchers were hospitalised. The day became known as Bloody Sunday.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The turned-back march and the third march</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="32.406,-87.019" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Edmund Pettus Bridge, Selma</span> <span class="jr-date">9 March 1965</span><p>King led about 2,500 marchers onto the bridge, held a short prayer and turned them around, obeying a court order and an agreement made with Sheriff Clark and Al Lingo. Many marchers were confused.</p></li>
<li data-ll="33.507,-86.803" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">University Hospital, Birmingham</span> <span class="jr-date">11 March 1965</span><p>That evening in Selma four Ku Klux Klan members clubbed three white Unitarian Universalist ministers. Activists took the worst injured, James Reeb of Boston, to Birmingham, where he died with his wife beside him.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.412,-87.016" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Brown Chapel A.M.E. Church, Selma</span> <span class="jr-date">21 March 1965</span><p>Close to 8,000 people assembled here to begin the walk to Montgomery. The Alabama National Guard, under federal control, escorted the march after Judge Johnson ruled it could go ahead.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.353,-86.327" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">City of St. Jude, Montgomery</span> <span class="jr-date">24 March 1965</span><p>Several thousand marchers reached the final campsite on the outskirts of Montgomery. That night a Stars for Freedom rally was held on a makeshift stage, with Harry Belafonte, Joan Baez and Nina Simone among the performers.</p></li>
<li data-ll="32.377,-86.301" data-by="land"><span class="jr-place">Alabama State Capitol, Montgomery</span> <span class="jr-date">25 March 1965</span><p>25,000 people marched from St. Jude to the Capitol steps, where King gave the speech &quot;How Long, Not Long&quot;. State troopers blocked the door, and a secretary of Governor Wallace took the petition.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">On 25 March 25,000 people stood at the steps of the Alabama State Capitol. That night Viola Liuzzo, a white mother of five from Detroit, was shot dead by Ku Klux Klan members while ferrying marchers back to Selma. President Johnson signed the Voting Rights Act on 6 August 1965.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Selma to Montgomery marches&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
<article class="jr-j" id="hokulea" data-kids="1">
<h2>The Voyaging of Hōkūleʻa</h2>
<p class="jr-meta">1976–2017 &#183; Hōkūleʻa and its navigators &#183; 22 stops</p>
<p class="jr-intro">Hōkūleʻa is a Polynesian double-hulled voyaging canoe (waʻa kaulua) launched on 8 March 1975 by the Polynesian Voyaging Society. Its 1976 voyage from Hawaiʻi to Tahiti was made with exclusively traditional navigation, under the Satawalese master navigator Pius &quot;Mau&quot; Piailug, and later voyages carried the canoe across Polynesia and, from 2014, around the world.</p>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The first voyage to Tahiti, 1976</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="1">
<li data-ll="20.997,-156.653" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Honolua Bay</span> <span class="jr-date">1 May 1976</span><p>Hōkūleʻa left Honolua Bay on Maui for Tahiti, with Mau Piailug as navigator and Kawika Kapahulehua as captain, as part of the celebration of the United States Bicentennial.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.540,-149.569" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/papeete">Papeʻete</a></span> <span class="jr-date">4 June 1976</span><p>Mau Piailug navigated from Hawaiʻi to Tahiti without instruments. After a conflict among the crew escalated into physical violence, he returned home to Micronesia, and the canoe was navigated back with western instruments.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The capsizing of 1978</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="3">
<li data-ll="21.284,-157.842" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Ala Wai Harbor</span> <span class="jr-date">16 March 1978</span><p>Hōkūleʻa left Honolulu's Ala Wai Harbor on a second voyage to Tahiti, with Nainoa Thompson as navigator and Dave Lyman as captain.</p></li>
<li data-ll="21.135,-157.010" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Lāʻau Point</span> <span class="jr-date">March 1978</span><p>The canoe capsized southwest of Molokaʻi and the crew held on through the night. Eddie Aikau paddled a surfboard towards Lānaʻi for help and was never seen again. The crew were rescued by the Coast Guard and the canoe was towed back to Honolulu.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Nainoa Thompson's voyage to Tahiti, 1980</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="5">
<li data-ll="19.706,-155.086" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Hilo</span> <span class="jr-date">15 March 1980</span><p>After the 1978 disaster Mau Piailug returned and trained Nainoa Thompson further. Thompson set out from Hilo to recreate the 1976 voyage, with Mau sailing as an observer.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-14.883,-148.717" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Mataiva</span><p>After 29 days at sea, before sighting Mataiva, Mau offered Nainoa one correction: a seabird in mid-morning flight carrying a fish was flying towards land to feed its young, not away from it.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.540,-149.569" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/papeete">Papeʻete</a></span> <span class="jr-date">17 April 1980</span><p>Thompson became the first Native Hawaiian in modern times to navigate a canoe thousands of miles without instruments, arriving in Tahiti after 29 days at sea.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">The Voyage of Rediscovery, 1985 to 1987</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="8">
<li data-ll="-21.233,-159.783" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Rarotonga</span> <span class="jr-date">14 September 1985</span><p>Hōkūleʻa sailed from Papeʻete to Rarotonga in the Cook Islands, with Nainoa Thompson as navigator, on the first legs of a voyage of 12,000 miles to destinations throughout Polynesia.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-35.266,174.080" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/waitangi-northland">Waitangi</a></span> <span class="jr-date">7 December 1985</span><p>The canoe sailed from Rarotonga to Waitangi on the North Island of New Zealand, with Nainoa Thompson as navigator and Shorty Bertelmann as captain.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-21.134,-175.202" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/nukualofa">Nukuʻalofa</a></span> <span class="jr-date">11 May 1986</span><p>Hōkūleʻa sailed from Waitangi to Nukuʻalofa on Tongatapu, with Leon Paoa Sterling as captain and Sione Uaine Ula of Tonga among the crew.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-14.279,-170.701" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/pago-pago">Pago Pago</a></span> <span class="jr-date">25 May 1986</span><p>The canoe went on to Pago Pago on Tutuila in American Samoa. Hector Busby of New Zealand and Sione Taupeamuhu of Tonga were among the crew.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-18.850,-159.790" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/aitutaki">Aitutaki</a></span> <span class="jr-date">16 July 1986</span><p>Hōkūleʻa sailed from Ofu Island in American Samoa to Aitutaki in the Cook Islands, with Shorty Bertelmann as captain and Tua Pittman of the Cook Islands among the crew.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-17.747,-149.161" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Tautira</span> <span class="jr-date">21 August 1986</span><p>After a short leg from Aitutaki back to Rarotonga, Hōkūleʻa sailed on to Tautira on Tahiti Iti. Nainoa Thompson was both navigator and captain.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-15.117,-147.633" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Rangiroa</span> <span class="jr-date">4 April 1987</span><p>In 1987 the canoe sailed from Tautira to Rangiroa Atoll in the Tuamotus, as the first step of the voyage home to Hawaiʻi.</p></li>
<li data-ll="21.511,-157.837" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Kualoa</span> <span class="jr-date">23 May 1987</span><p>Hōkūleʻa sailed from Rangiroa to Kualoa on Oʻahu, ending the Voyage of Rediscovery, with Shorty Bertelmann as captain and Nainoa Thompson as navigator.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 class="jr-leg">Malama Honua, the worldwide voyage, 2014 to 2017</h3>
<ol class="jr-stops" start="16">
<li data-ll="21.485,-157.965" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a></span> <span class="jr-date">18 May 2014</span><p>Hōkūleʻa and its sister vessel Hikianalia set out from Oʻahu on &quot;Malama Honua&quot;, a three-year circumnavigation of the earth.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-8.500,115.000" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a></span> <span class="jr-date">August 2015</span><p>In the Australian and Indian Ocean part of the voyage, Hōkūleʻa called at Bali in August 2015.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-15.965,-5.715" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">St. Helena</a></span> <span class="jr-date">January 2016</span><p>In the Atlantic part of the voyage, Hōkūleʻa stopped at Saint Helena in January 2016, on its way to Brazil.</p></li>
<li data-ll="23.137,-82.359" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a></span> <span class="jr-date">March 2016</span><p>Hōkūleʻa called at Havana, Cuba, in March 2016, after the US Virgin Islands in the Caribbean.</p></li>
<li data-ll="40.713,-74.006" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">New York City</span> <span class="jr-date">June 2016</span><p>On the North American leg, Hōkūleʻa reached New York City in June 2016, after visiting Washington, D.C., in May.</p></li>
<li data-ll="-27.120,-109.350" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place"><a href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Rapa Nui</a></span> <span class="jr-date">March 2017</span><p>Back in the Pacific after a two-day transit of the Panama Canal and a stop in the Galapagos in February, Hōkūleʻa reached Rapa Nui in March 2017.</p></li>
<li data-ll="21.285,-157.847" data-by="sea"><span class="jr-place">Magic Island</span> <span class="jr-date">17 June 2017</span><p>Hōkūleʻa came home to Hawaiʻi, after calling at Tahiti in April. The homecoming took place at Magic Island, Oʻahu, on 17 June 2017.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="jr-end">Hōkūleʻa and its sister vessel Hikianalia returned to Hawaiʻi on 17 June 2017 after a three-year circumnavigation of 47,000 nautical miles, with stops at 85 ports in 26 countries.</p>
<p class="jr-src">Told after the Wikipedia article &#8220;Hōkūleʻa&#8221;. Places with an encyclopedia entry are linked.</p>
</article>
